5500:
** χειροτον-έω**,
**stretch out the hand,** for the purpose of giving one's vote in the assembly, περὶ τῶν ἀνδρῶν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Phocion” 34}}; μὴ χ. **vote** against the motion, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Deor.Conc.” 9:}} —but mostly,
**II** with _accusative_ _person_, **elect,** properly **by show of hands,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 598, “Av.” 1571, etc.; εἰς τὴν ἀγορὰν χ. τοὺς ταξιάρχους.., οὐκ ἐπὶ τὸν πόλεμον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.26; with double _accusative_, στρατηγὸν χ. τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.2.11, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.50}}:—_passive_, **to be elected,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 607}}; ἐπὶ τοῦτ᾽ ἐχειροτονήθησαν, ἵνα.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 28.14; χ. ἔκ τινων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 763e}}; χ. ἐπὶ τῆς διοικήσεως Decrees cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.115: with _accusative_ _cognate_, χ. τὴν ἀρχὴν τὴν ἐπὶ τῷ θεωρικῷ 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.24, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 517 (_anapaest meter_); χειροτονηθεὶς ἢ λαχών 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 300a, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.106.}}
**II.b** later, generally, **appoint,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.112}}; of the Jewish High Priest, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 13.2.2; τὸν ὑπὸ τοῦ θεοῦ κεχειροτονημένον βασιλέα [prev. work] 6.13.9, compare 7.9.3}}; **appoint to an office** in the Church, πρεσβυτέρους {{NT|NT.Act.14.23, cf. NT.2Cor.8.19 (_passive_).}}
**II.2** with _accusative_ _of things_, **vote for** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 297 (Lyric poetry), 797, Isocrates 7.84; γνώμας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.248: with}}_infinitive_, ὁ δῆμος ἐχειροτόνησεν ἐξεῖναι.. πέμπειν **voted** to send, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.13, compare “IG” 12.57.29, 63.4:}} —_passive_, κεχειροτόνηται ὕβρις τὸ πρᾶγυ᾽ εἶναι **it is voted, ruled** to be.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.216.}}
**III** **span with the hand,** τὸ αἰδοῖον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.78 (cited in 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}; χειροκοπεῖν codices).

5501:
** χείρων**, ὁ, ἡ,
_neuter_ χεῖρον, _genitive_ -ονος, _accusative_ -ονα: _nominative_ and _accusative_ _plural_ χείρονες, -ας, χείρονα, _contraction_ in _Attic dialect_ Prose χείρους, χείρ; _dative_ χείροσι, _poetry_ χειρόνεσσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.22}}:—(for _Epic dialect_ form χερείων, _poetry_ χειρότερος, χερειότερος, (see entry).):—irregular _comparative_ of κακός: (χείρων from *χερ-yων, compare χερείων):
**I** of persons, **mcaner, inferior,** either in bodily strength and bravery, or in rank, opposed to ἀρείων, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.238, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.133; σὺ μὲν ἐσθλὸς ἐγὼ δὲ σέθεν πολὺ χείρων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.434; τοῦ γένετ᾽ ἐκ πατρὸς πολὺ χείρονος υἱὸς ἀμείνων 15.641, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.82; ἦ πολὺ χείρονες ἄνδρες ἀμύμονος ἀνδρὸς ἄκοιτιν μνῶνται 21.325}}; opposed to κρείσσων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).34(52); τὸν ὄλβιον τόν τε χ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 422 (Lyric poetry); τὰ χείρονα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 192, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 196.}}
**I.2** later in moral sense, **worse than others,** sometimes almost like a positive, **knave,** opposed to ἀγαθός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 456, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.9, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.3; οἱ πένητες καὶ οἱ δημόται καὶ οἱ χ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 1.4, compare 3.10}}; οἱ χ., opposed to οἱ ἀγαθοί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 460c, etc.}}
**I.2.b** χ. βίος, opposed to ἀμείνων,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 618d; γνώμη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.7.}}
**I.3** **worse** in quality, **inferior,** of horses, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.572}}: **inferior, less skilful,** ἰητροί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 6}}; ζωγράφοι, δημιουργοί, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 429a, “R.” 421e, etc.: χ. εἰς σοφίαν, εἰς τὴν ἀρετήν, [prev. author] “Tht.” 162c, “R.” 335b; πρὸς ἀλήθειαν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 48; with _accusative_, χ. τὰ πολεμικά 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.20}}; χ. τὴν ψυχήν, τὴν διάνοιαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.46. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.43; τὰ ἄλλα μηδὲν χ. [prev. author] 4.105}}; with _infinitive_, χ. ἡμῶν ταῦτα ποιεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.16}}; οὐδὲν χείρους ἔσεσθε.. ἀκηκοότες you will be none **the worse** for having heard.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.139}}; **less kind,** μὴ χ. περὶ ἡμᾶς αὐτοὺς εἶναι.. τῶν ὑπαρχόντων {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.2.}}
**II** of things, **inferior** in quality, ἄεθλον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.413; ὑποδήματα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 13.10; ὄνομα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 429b.}}
**II.2** **worse, harder, more severe,** νόσος {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 220; μοῖρα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 248e; τιμωρία NT.Heb.10.29.}}
**III** _neuter_,
**III.1** as a _substantive_, τὸ χ. **inferiority,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Polemo Sophista “Callimachus” 27}}; but mostly in phrases with Preps., ἐπὶ τὸ χ. τρέπεσθαι, κλῖναι, fall off, get **worse,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.2, “Mem.” 3.5.13; ἐπὶ τὸ χ. μεταβάλλει ἑαυτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 381b}}; ἀλλοιοῦσθαι ἐπὶ τὸ χ., opposed to ἐπὶ τὸ βέλτιον, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.3.3}}; also πάντα ὑποπτεύοντες ἐπὶ τὸ χ. putting the **worst** construction on.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.85; λαμβάνειν τι ἐπὶ τὸ χ. 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 16.7.4; also πρὸς τὸ χ. μεταβάλλειν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.57; κατὰ τὸ χ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 720e}}; in the **lower** sense, opposed to κατὰ τὸ κρεῖττον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 7: less frequently in _plural_, ἐπὶ τὰ χείρω ἰέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.9.9; τὰ χ. προαιρεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.110.}}
**III.2** as a predicate, ἀλλὰ σοὶ αὐτῷ χ. (i.e. ἐστί or ἔσται) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.515, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.4}}; with a _negative_, οὐ χ. [ἐστι] with _infinitive_, we may **as well,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 105a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1127a14}}; simply οὐ χεῖρον, in an answer, it is **as well,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 37; λάβ᾽, ὦγάθ᾽· οὐδὲν χ. 4th c.BC: Clearchus Comicus 4.}}
**III.3** as _adverb_, **worse,** χ. βουλεύσασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.46, compare 6.89; χ. πρᾶξαι [prev. author] 7.67}}; βιῶναι, ζῆν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 344e, 519d.}}
**III.3.b** **in inferior degree, less,** ἀγαπᾶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 928a; φυλακὰς χ. φυλαττομένας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.2.17, etc.}}
**B** _superlative_ **χείριστος**, η, ον, **worst,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 303a, etc.}}; ὁ χ., opposed to ὁ βέλτιστος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.2; especially οἱ χ.}}**men of lowest degrce,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.32}}. _adverb_ χείριστα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 687a24, “Metaphorical” 1083b2 (uncertain 1.)}} also **-τως** {{LXX|LXX.2Mac.7.39.}}

5503:
** χήρ-α**,
_Ionic dialect_ **χήρη**, ἡ, **widow,** χῆραι γυναῖκες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.289; μήτηρ χ. 22.499; μὴ παῖδ᾽ ὀρφανικὸν θήῃς χ. τε γυναῖκα 6.432; λείπειν τινὰ χ. ἐν μεγάροισιν 22.484, 24.725, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 653, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 348, “Tr.” 380; χήρας δὲ γυναῖκας ἐποίησαν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.71}}; as a name of Hera, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 8.22.2 (χῆραι· αἱ μὴ ἔχουσαι ἄνδρας, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus; ἡ μονωθεῖσα ἀπ᾽ ἀνδρὸς χ. 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 3.47).}}
**2** Comedy texts, of a dish, **widowed,** i.e. **without sauce,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Sotades Comicus 1.26.}}
**3** later _masculine_ **χῆρος**, **widower,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 612b34 (of birds), 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 17, Grammars post 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus “Epim.” 286.}}
**II** **χῆρος**, α, ον, Adj, _metaphorically_, **bereaved,** χῆρα μέλαθρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 862 (_anapaest meter_); μάνδραι 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Cererem” 106; βίος “epigram Gr.” 406.13 (from Iconium)}}; εὐνή {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 14.1389 i 12; δόμος 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 22}}; δρυμοὶ χ. **bereft** of men, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.84 (1st c.AD: Antiphanes Macedo Epigrammaticus): with _genitive_, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.408; φάρσος.. στελεοῦ χῆρον ἐλαϊνέου “Anthologia Graeca” 6.297 (from Phanias), compare 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 117.6; χήρους γυναικῶν οἰκεῖν 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 7.3.4; τὰ χῆρα φρονήσεως 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.601; ναῦς ὕδατος χ. 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 13.28. (Cf. χωρίς, χατίζω}} Sanskrit _jáhāti_ 'abandon, renounce'.)

5505:
** χῑλι-άς**, άδος, ἡ:
_genitive_ _plural_ χιλιάδων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.28 (χιλιαδέων variant in 7.28)}}:—**a thousand,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.58, 7.28, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 341; χ. τέτορες 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 91; with _genitive_, πολλὰς χιλιάδας ταλάντων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.96, compare 28; ἐννέα χιλιάδας ἐτῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 257a.}}
**2** generally, **large number,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 16.91, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 56}}; πολλαὶ χ., of lines of poetry, {{Refs|Callias Epigrammaticus “Aet.Oxy.” 2079.4.}}
**3** Χιλιάδες, αἱ, title of poem by {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euphorio Epicus, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 10.436f, etc.}}
**II** ={χιλιετηρίς}, {{Refs|Alex.Aetol. 4.4}}; Ῥωμαϊκὴ χ., title of work by Asinius Quadratus, [{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Stephanus Byzantius see entry {Ἀνθιον} compare (χιλιαρχία 111, χιλιετηρίς).}}]

5506:
** χῑλίαρχ-ος**, ὁ,
**captain over a thousand,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 304}}: especially **commandant** of a garrison, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.14, “Oec.” 4.7.}}
**II** ={χιλιάρχης}{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|11, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.19.1, +others, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.14, “Inscription Magn.” 157b (1st c.AD), etc. (the usual form in this sense).}}
**II.2** title of Persian court official, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Artoxerxes” 5, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 1.21}}; also in Macedonia, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.48.}}

5507:
** χίλιοι** [χῑ], αι, α:
_genitive_ _plural_ _feminine_ χιλιῶν, irregular in _Attic dialect_ _accusative_ to {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus Gr. 1.426 (probably only when χίλιαι was used as a _feminine_}} _substantive_ (see. below {{Refs|3))}}; _dative_ _plural_ _feminine_ (originally _locative_) χιλίᾱσιν {{Refs|“IG” 12.10.18; χιλίαισι [prev. work]76.20, 94.10; later χιλίαις}}: dialect forms, _Locrian dialect_ **χίλιοι** (as in _Attic dialect_, see above) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 9(1).334.39 (Oeanthea, 5th c.BC)}}; _Doric dialect_ **χήλιοι** (written χέλ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 5(1).1.23 (Sparta, 5th c.BC), Lesb., Thess.}}**χέλλιοι** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Etymologicum Magnum” 817.1 (compare χελληστύς), “IG” 9(2).1229.29 (Phalanna, 2nd c.BC)}}; _Ionic dialect_ **χείλιοι** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 688 C 15 (Chios, 5th c.BC)}}, which should probably be restored in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, compare δεκάχ (ε)}} ιλοι:—**a thousand,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in _neuter_, χ. μέτρα, πυρά, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.471, 8.562}}; πρῶθ᾽ ἑκατὸν βοῦς δῶκεν, ἔπειτα δὲ χίλι᾽ ὑπέστη (i.e. πρόβατα), αἶγας ὁμοῦ καὶ ὄϊς {{Refs|11.244}}: usually agreeing with its _substantive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 364, etc.}}; but sometimes as _substantive_ followed by _genitive_, χίλιοι Μακεδόνων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.80}}: to express the addition of a smaller number, that number may either precede or follow, διακόσιοι καὶ χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.87, 93, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 119b: but χ. καὶ πεντακόσιοι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.77}}; later καί is frequently omitted, {{NT+5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.33.10, LXX.Dan.12.11, NT.Rev.11.3; with Preps. χ. ἐπὶ μυρίοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 895a; τέτταρας πρὸς τοῖς χ. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cat.” 4}}; οἱ χ. λογάδες (at Argos) **the Thousand,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.67, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.80.}}
**2** _singular_ with _collective_ nouns, χιλίη ἵππος **a thousand** horse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.63, 7.41; τὴν ἵππον τὴν χιλίην [prev. author] 8.113; so ἵππον ἔχω εἰς χιλίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.2}}; also χιλία ὁλοκαύτωσις burnt-offering **of 1000 cattle,** {{LXX|LXX.3Ki.3.4.}}
**3** χίλιαι (i.e. δραχμαί) as _substantive_, **a thousand drachmae,** περὶ χιλιῶν κινδυνεύειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.21; ἐν χιλίαις ὁ κίνδυνος [prev. work]26 (χ. δραχμαί in full, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36b)}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _sahásram_ 'a thousand' (with prefix **sa- = sṃ-**); I.-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **ĝheslo-, ĝheslio-.**)

5509:
** χῐτών**,
in _Ionic dialect_Prose **κῐθών**, ῶνος, ὁ (also probably in {{Refs|“Sammelb.” 4291)}}, _Doric dialect_ **κῐτών** (which see):— **garment worn next the skin, tunic.**
**I** in early times, only of **a man's tunic** (the woman's being πέπλος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia BT 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.42), χιτῶνα περὶ χροΐ δῦνεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.60; κιθῶνας ὑποδύνειν τοῖσι εἵμασι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.155}}: sometimes with a girdle, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.72; τερμιόεις 19.242, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 537}}; μαλακός, ἐΰννητος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.437, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.580; [χ.] λαμπρός.. ἠέλιος ὥς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.234; χλαῖνάν τε χιτῶνά τε 14.132, 154; οἱ δ᾽ ἀροτῆρες ἤρεικον χθόνα δῖαν ἐπιστολάδην δὲ χιτῶνας ἐστάλατ᾽ 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 287.}}
**I.2** later worn also by women, ἅμα κιθῶνι ἐκδυομένῳ συνεκδύεται καὶ τὴν αἰδῶ γυνή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8; σύροισα χιτῶνα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.73}}; the Ionian sleeved χ. was distinguished fr the Dorian, fastened with περόναι, μετέβαλον [αἱ τῶν Ἀθηναίων γυναῖκες] ἐς τὸν λίνεον κ. ἵνα δὴ περόνῃσι μὴ χρέωνται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.87; οἱ πρεσβύτεροι [τῶν Ἀθηναίων] οὐ πολὺς χρόνος ἐπειδὴ χιτῶνας λινοῦς ἐπαύσαντο φοροῦντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.6, compare 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 954.50}}; χ. ποδήρης, ὀρθοστάδιος, στατός (see entry); κιθὼν ποδηνεκής, worn by Babylonians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.195}}; κιθῶνες λίνεοι περὶ τὰ σκέλεα θυσανωτοί, worn by Egyptians, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.81}}; κιθῶνες εἰρίνεοι, worn by Cilicians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.91; uncertain in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 288 (_plural_).}}
**II** **coat of mail,** probably of leather covered with scales or rings, στρεπτὸς χ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.113; χ. χάλκεος 13.439}}; κιθῶνες χειριδωτοὶ λεπίδος σιδηρέης **coats** of iron scales with sleeves, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.61 (assuming variant): but distinguished from θώρηξ [prev. author] 9.22, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.4.1.}}
**III** **part of a shoe that coats the foot, upper,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 8.2.5 (_plural_), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1392a31.}}
**IV** _metaphorically_, **any coat, case,** or **covering,** λάϊνος χιτών (see. λάϊνος) τειχέων κιθῶνες, i. e. walls, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.139}}; in Anatomy, **coat, membrane,** τὸν ἀμφὶ τὴν ὄψιν χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 19, compare “Aph.” 7.45, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 2.993.1; ὁ.. χ. τῆς καρδίας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Respiratione” 480a4}}; χ. ὑμενώδης, ἀραχνιώδης, {{Refs|[prev. author] “PA” 679a1, “HA” 557b16; τοῦ ᾠοῦ οἱ χ. οἱ περιέχοντες [prev. work] 561a14}}; of foetal **membranes,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.7, 58, +others}}; τριγλοφόροι χιτῶνες, of fishing-nets, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.11 (1st c.BC(?): Satyrus Epigrammaticus)}}; χιτῶνες ἀραχνίων spiders' **webs,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐντὸς παθῶν” 3}}: _plural_, **pods** or **coats** of seeds, bulbous roots, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.12.3, 8.4.1, “CP” 1.4.1, +others}}
**IV.2** **vesture,** [φύσις] σαρκῶν ἀλλογνῶτι περιστέλλουσα (i.e. ψυχάς) χιτῶνι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 126; σῶμα χ. ψυχῆς “IG” 14.2241: _plural_, προσθέσει χιτώνων ἐνυλοτέρων}} of the soul, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 209}}. (Accad. **kitinnu** 'linen garment', Hebrew **kètōnet** 'tunic'; χεθὼν γὰρ τὸ λίνον ἡμεῖς καλοῦμεν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 3.7.2.)}}

5510:
** χιών**, όνος, ἡ:
(see. entry near the end):— **snow,** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} mostly of **fallen snow,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.7, 22.152; ὡς δὲ χ. κατατήκετ᾽ ἐν.. ὄρεσσιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.205; ὕπερθε χ. γένετ᾽ ἠΰτε πάχνη 14.476; τὸν Νεῖλον ῥέειν ἀπὸ τηκομένης χιόνος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.22}}; ἐπὶ χιόνι πεσούσῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. passage], compare 4.50; Ἰδαία χ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 564; ἥλιος.. τήκει πετραίαν χιόνα [prev. author] “fragment” 300.5; καί νιν.. χιὼν οὐδαμὰ λείπει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 830 (Lyric poetry)}}; also of **falling snow,** ὥς τε νιφάδες χιόνος πίπτωσι θαμειαί thick fall the **snow**-flakes, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.278; χ. πίπτουσα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.31; κατένειψε χιόνι τὴν Θρᾴκην 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 138; ὅταν βορέας χιόνα χέῃ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 329, compare “Ba.” 662; ἐπιπίπτει χ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.4.11; χιόνες πολλαὶ γίνονται 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 24: [χ.] σφοδρὰ καὶ ἀθρόα καταφερομένη νιφετὸς ὠνόμασται 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 394a36.}}
**II** **snow-water, 'ice-coldwater,** Θρῄκην χιόνι.. κατάρρυτον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 215; χ. ποταμία [prev. author] “Tr.” 1067 (Lyric poetry)}}; used to cool wine, εἰ χιών ἐστ᾽ ὠνία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Euthycles Comicus 1; οἶνον πιεῖν.. χιόνι μεμιγμένον 5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 57; χιόνα πίνειν 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 141.10; τοῦ θέρους χιόνα.. ζητεῖς Χ. “Mem.” 2.1.30; ἡδὺ θέρους.. χιὼν ποτόν “Anthologia Graeca” 5.168 (1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus)}}: rare in _plural_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 394a16}}. [ῐ by nature, ῑ _Epic dialect_ for the sake of meter] (Cf. Sanskrit _himás_ 'cold, winter', Latin _hiems,_ Avest. **zyam-** 'winter', etc.)

5511:
** χλᾰμύς** [ῠ], ύδος, ἡ:
_accusative_ χλαμύδα, also χλάμυν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 674}}:—**short mantle,** worn properly by horsemen, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.4.4}}; borrowed with the πέτασος from Thessaly, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 34, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 10.124}}; but said to be Macedonian, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 500, 3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus 62J.}}; worn by ἔφηβοι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus [same place], compare “Anthologia Graeca” 6.282 (Theodorus Epigrammaticus)}}; μάτηρ σε.. δῶρον ἐς Ἅιδαν ὀκτωκαιδεκέταν ἐστόλισεν χλαμύδι{{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work] 7.468 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus)}}; χλαμύδεσσ᾽ ἀμφεμμένοι, of ephebi, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IGRom.” 4.360.35 (Pergam., 2nd c.AD)}}; ἐκ χλαμύδος, ={ἐξ ἐφήβου}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.752f, compare 754f; ἐκ χλαμύδος.. ᾤχετ᾽ ἐς Ἅιδα “IG” 12(7).447.6 (from Amorgos)}}; worn by Hermes, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 30}}; also by Eros, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica [prev. cited] (see. 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 10.124), 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.6, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 12.78 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus).}}
**2** generally, **military cloak,** of foot-soldiers, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 16, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 331, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Philopoemen” 11, etc.}}; of heralds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 987.}}
**3** of **the general's cloak,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.27J., 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 35, “Lys.” 13, etc.}}; worn by kings, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Demetr.” 42, etc.}}; by tragic kings and heroes, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 41}}; by Σειληνοί in a procession, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC(?): Callixinus Historicus 2}}: = Latin _paludamentum,_ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 59.17, 60.17, +others, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 4.7.3, “Codex Theodosianus [5th c.AD]” 14.10.1.}}
**4** a civilian's **mantle,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 263.2, +others (3rd c.BC), “PLond.” 2.402 ii 16 (2nd c.BC), 2nd c.AD(?): Xenophon Ephesius Scriptor Eroticus 1.8 codex, “POxy.” 1288.24 (4th c.AD). (For its shapecf. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 26.)}}

5512:
** χλευ-άζω**,
(χλεύη) **jest, scoff,** ἐπισκώπτων καὶ παίζων καὶ χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 376 (Lyric poetry); τοῖς καταγελῶσι καὶ χ. καὶ σκώπτουσι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1379a29, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.3.13, 3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus 18 ii 5, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.29 O., etc.}}
**2** with _accusative_, **scoff, jeer at, treat scornfully,** τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 397d, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 7.7, 19.23, 47.34, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus “fragment” 109.16; ἐμαυτὴν.. λέληθα χλευάζουσ᾽ 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 215; with _accusative_ _of things_, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 10, etc.}}:—_middle_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Brut.” 45}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Epicrates Comicus 11.31 (_anapaest meter_), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 952b22, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sertorius” 13, 25, 2.504f.}}

5513:
** χλιᾰρός**, ά, όν,
also ός, όν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 360}}; _Ionic dialect_ **χλιερός** (also in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 33.5, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.52}}; misspelt χλιεριον in {{Refs|“PHolm.” 16.27)}}, ή, όν· (χλίω):— **warm,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus [290]; ὕδωρ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.181, Diocl.fragment 139, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.82}}; of food, Magnes {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1, 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 125 (troch.), 143 (hexameters), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 975 (Lyric poetry); τὸ σῶμα ἡμῶν ἀτμίδα τινὰ χ. ἀφίησιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 884b17; τὸ χ. τὸ ἐν γλώσσῃ “Placita Philosophorum” 4.18.1.}} _adverb_ χλιηρῶς (variant -ρῷ){{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ συρίγγων” 9; καταπλάσσειν χλιηρόν [prev. passage]}}
**2** of persons, **lukewarm,** {{NT|NT.Rev. 3.16}}. [ῑ in Comedy texts [same places]; but ῐ in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus [same place], 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus [same place]]}}

5514:
** χλόη**, ἡ,
_Ionic dialect_ **χλοίη** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 64, compare below 11, also “PTeb.” 112.46 (2nd c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 181}}; _Doric dialect_ **χλόα**, ας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus in Lyric poetry, “Hipp.” 1139, “IA” 1058, +others)}}:— **the first green shoot of plants** in spring, ναὶ μὰ μήκωνος χλόην {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 108}}; especially **young green corn** or **grass,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.34, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hipp.” [same place], “IA” 422, etc.; χλόην νέμεσθαι [prev. author] “Ba.” 735; ποτὰ τὰ ἀπὸ χλοίης 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus}} [same place]; opposed to καρποί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 80d}}; χλόης γενομένης ἀπὸ τοῦ σπέρματος, of the corn when it first springs up, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 17.10}}; ἐν τῇ χ., opposed to ἐν τοῖς σπέρμασιν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 4.4.7, compare “HP” 8.2.4; πιαίνονται βόες χλόῃ κυάμων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 595b7.}}
**2** _poetry_, **young verdure** of trees, **foliage,** χ. ἀμπέλου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 12, compare “Supp.” 258, “Ion” 1435, “Hel.” 180 (Lyric poetry), 1360 (Lyric poetry).}}
**3** **juice** of green shoots, χ. ἐλαίας {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 7.3073.162 (Lebad. 2nd c.BC): but σατυρίου χλόῃ}} is probably _falsa lectio_ for{σαύρου χολῇ} in {{Refs|“Geoponica” 10.21.12.}}
**4** **vegetables, herbs, greens,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 1.5, 4th c.BC: Sotades Comicus 1.9, +others}}
**II** epithet of Demeter, **Verdant,** from the young corn, worshipped in Attica, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 22.1356.16, Semus 19, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.22.3; at Myconos, etc., “SIG” 1024.11 (Myconos, 3rd-2nd c.BC), 1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 28; Δήμητρος Χλοίης ἱερόν “IG” 22.5006.4}}; also Χλόη alone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]1358.49, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 835}}. (Perh. _cognate_ with Lithuanian _[zcirc]élti_ 'to be green, grow', Latin _holus._)

5515:
** χλωρός**, ά, όν,
**greenish-yellow, pale green,** χλωραὶ ῥῶπες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.47; ὄρος.. χλωρόν “hymnus ad Apollinem” 223; χλωραὶ ἐλάται 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 167, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 38; χλωραῖς ὑπὸ βάσσαις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 673 (Lyric poetry); χλωρὰν ἀν᾽ ὕλην 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 17; δόνακι χλωρὸν Εὐρώταν [prev. author] “Hel.” 349 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1132 (Lyric poetry)}}; also in Prose, σίτου ἔτι χ. ὄντος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.6; τὰ φυόμενα χ. τὸ πρῶτον εἶναι 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 78}}; ἡ χ. the **green plaster,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Andromachus Poeta Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.470}}; χλωρὸς λίθος, ={σμάραγδος}, {{Refs|“PHolm.” 5.10}}; of sea-water, poetical cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.767f(compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1084)}}; of other water, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.669.3 (5th-6th c.AD: Marianus Epigrammaticus): χλωρά, ἡ}} **green paint,** as a stage-property to represent a river in scenery, Papyrus in {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“Eos.” 32.30 (5th-6th c.AD).}}
**2** **yellow,** μέλι χ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.631, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.234}}; ἀμφὶ χλωρὰν ψάμαθον on the **yellow** sand, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1064}}; ᾠοῦ τὸ χ. **yolk** of egg, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Zopyrus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 14.61.1.}}
**II** generally, **pale, pallid,** χλωρὸς ἀδάμας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 231: most frequently}}
**II.2** of persons, **pale,** χλωρὸς ὑπαὶ δείους {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.376, 15.4}}; χ. Ἀχλύς (personified) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 265; χλωροτέρα.. ποίας ἔμμι 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.14}}; hence as an epithet of fear, χλωρὸν δέος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.479, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.43, etc.; χλωρῷ δείματι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 566 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 599 (Lyric poetry)}}: in in Medicine texts writers, **yellow, biliouslooking,** ὀφθαλμοὶ -ότεροι see reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 10; χρῶμα χ. ἴσχειν [prev. author] “Prog.” 24; σῶμα.. οὔτε χ. ἀλλ᾽ ὑπέρυθρον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49}}; also χ. πτύελος, οὖρον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 14, “VM” 10 (_comparative_).}}
**III** without regard to colour, **green,** i. e. **fresh,** opposed to dry, especially of wood, ῥόπαλον.. χλωρὸν ἐλαΐνεον of **green** olive-wood, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.320, compare 379}}; opposed to αὖος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 743; τὰ σφόδρα χ. ἄκαυστα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 387a22; χ. ξύλα [prev. work] 374a5, +others}}; of various things, χλωραὶ ἐέρσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.40}}; τυρὸς χ. **fresh** cheese, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 559, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 23.6}}; of fish, **fresh, not salted,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 7.309b}}; of fruit, **fresh picked,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae 22.1013.23, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.113.}}
**III.2** _metaphorically_, **fresh, blooming,** χλωρόν τε καὶ βλέποντα Trag. cited in {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}} (perhaps to be read in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 677 for καὶ ζῶντα καὶ β.)}}; λειμὼν ἄνθεσι (sed perhaps ἔρνεσι) θάλλων χλωροῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1297 (Lyric poetry); χλωρὸν γόνυ 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.70; χ. αἷαμα}}**fresh, living,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1055, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 127 (_anapaest meter_)}}; χ. δάκρυ **fresh, bursting** tear, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 906, compare 922, “Hel.” 1189; χλωρὰ δακρύων ἄχνα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 847 (Lyric poetry); οἴνου χλωραὶ σταγόνες}}**sparkling,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 67 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III.3** _metaphorically_, **unripe,** χ. καὶ ἄναιμα πράγματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 16. (Not _contraction_ from χλοερός but _cognate_ with it and χλόη.)}}

5517:
** χοϊκός**, ή, όν,
(χοῦς B) **of earth** or **clay,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.1Cor.15.47; κόνις 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.673.}}
**II** **of the age to take part in the festival of** χόες, {{Refs|“IG” 3.1342.}}

5518:
** χοῖνιξ**, ῐκος, ἡ,
**choenix,** a dry measure, especially for corn, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.192, etc.}}; the **choenix** of corn was one man's daily allowance, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. author] 7.187; ἡ γὰρ χ. ἡμερησία τροφή 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 8.18}}; given to slaves, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.16}}; hence, ὅς κεν ἐμῆς γε χοίνικος ἅπτηται, i. e. whoever eats of my **bread,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.28: proverbial, ἐπὶ χοίνικος καθέζεσθαι}} i. e. sit idle, live in idleness, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 197, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.703f, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 10.452e, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 1.26}}; οὐδὲ τὴν χ. ἔτι λήψει (of gold) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nav.” 27; χ. Ἀττική (1/48 of the μέδιμνος = 4 κοτύλαι) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.6}}; in Papyrus usually abbreviated χ, as in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 645.11 (3rd c.BC), “POxy.” 1044.3 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**II** from the like ness of shape, a kind of **stocks** for fastening the legs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 276, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.129.}}
**III** ={χοινικίς} VI, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 782 a. 7 (3rd c.BC), probably in “Supp.epigram” 4.447.48 (Didyma, 2nd c.BC).}}

5519:
** χοῖρος**, ὁ
(ἡ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 40, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 230, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 764)}}:— **young pig, porker** (younger than δέλφαξ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristophanes Byzantinus Philosophus cited in Ath 9.375. 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 3a), 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.73, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 24.2, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.48, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 309, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 781, etc.}}; offered as one of the smaller sacrifices, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 378a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.8.5, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.39, 4th c.AD: Heniochus Comicus 2.}}
**b** generally, ={ὗς},{σῦς}, **swine,** ἤδη δέλφακες, χοῖροι δὲ τοῖσιν ἄλλοις {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus [same place], compare 4th c.BC: Mnesimachus Comicus 4.47 (_anapaest meter_), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 7, NT.Matt.8.30.}}
**2** **pudenda muliebria,** frequently in Comedy texts poets, who are always punning on the word and its compounds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 773}} {χοιρόσακον}, etc.; said to be a Corinthian usage, {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}
**II** a fish of the Nile, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.2.4, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 7.312a, “Geoponica” 20.7.1, 13}} at Title Page (Perh. for **ĝhoryo-** 'grey', cf. Norse **griss** 'sucking-pig', OHG **grīs** 'grey', or{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|compare 2nd c.AD: Albinus Philosophus}} **de[rmacr]** 'pig'.)

5520:
** χολ-άω**,
(χολή) **to be full of black bile, to be melancholy mad,** ἀνδράσιν πείθει χολῶσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 833, compare 4th c.BC: Epicrates Comicus 5.7, Strato 1.7, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 176.}}
**II** ={χολόομαι}, **to be angry, rage,** {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 334, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 9.66, variant in 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 1.10, LXX.3Mac.3.1}}; _Epic dialect_ _participle_ χολόων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 140.}}

5521:
** χολή**, ἡ,
**gall, bile,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 131, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 184, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 682, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49, etc.}}; χ. μέλαινα black, i. e. diseased, **bile,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.23, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 83c}} (but, ={μελαγχολία}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 459); ξανθὴ χ. 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 19; πυρρά 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.658}}; χολὴν ἐμεῖν, βῆξαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Nicopho Comicus 12, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.70 (probable reading): proverbial, πικρῷ πικρὰν κλύζουσι φαρμάκῳ χολήν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 854; πικρότερ᾽ αὐτῆς τῆς χ. 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 16.12}}; χολῇ ἀλείφειν, proverbial of **giving one a disgust for a thing,** from the custom of mothers putting gall to the nipple when the child was to be weaned, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 74.}}
**2** _plural_ χολαί, **gall-bladder,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1010}}; called δοχαὶ χολῆς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 828}}; also in _singular_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 495; χολὴν τῶν ζῴων τὰ μὲν ἔχει τὰ δ᾽ οὐκ ἔχει 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 506a20, compare “PA” 677b11.}}
**3** _metaphorically_ (mostly in Poets) like{χόλος} (which see), **bile, gall,** i. e. **bitter anger, wrath,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 66; ἢ γυναιξὶν οὐκ οἴει χολὴν ἐνεῖνα; [prev. author] “Lys.” 465; οὐδεὶς χολὴν οὐδ᾽ ὀργὴν ἔχων φανήσεται 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.27}}; πάνυ ἐστ᾽ ἤδη χ. stirs my **bile,** makes me sick, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 4}}; ἐπιζεῖ χ. **the bile** boils over, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Th.” 468; χολὴν κινεῖν [prev. author] “V.” 403, compare 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 69.}}
**II** **ink of the cuttle-fish,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 473, “Th.” 561.}}
**III** in LXX = Hebrew **rôsh,** a poisonous plant, variously called **hemlock** or **poppy,** {{LXX|LXX.Psa.68(69).22, LXX.Jer. 8.14.}}
**IV** serpent's **venom,** χ. ἀσπίδος{{LXX|LXX.Job.20.14}}; of the hydra's **venom,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 2.5.2, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.11.}}
**V** **bitter drink,** {{NT|NT.Matt.27.34. (With χολή, χόλος}} cf. Latin _fel,_ ONorse **gall,** etc., 'bile, gall':—probably the name is derived from the colour of bile, and is _cognate_ with Latin (**h**) **olus, helvus,** [{{Refs 1st c.BC+|\{1st c.BC-1st c.AD\}: Germanicus Caesar Epigrammaticus}}] **gelb** 'yellow', perhaps also χλόη.)

5522:
** χοῦς**, ὁ,
also ἡ
**B**{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 10.2.19, 12.8.17, 16.1.9}}: (χέω):— **soil excavated** or **heaped up,** ὁ χ. ὁ ἐξορυχθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.150; τὸν αἰεὶ ἐξορυσσόμενον χοῦν [prev. author] 7.23, compare 1.185, 8.28, 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 121 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.76, 4.90, “IG” 22.380.26, etc.: _genitive_ χοῦ 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.27.4, “POxy.” 1631.28 (3rd c.AD)}} (un _contraction_ χόου {{Refs|“IG” 9(1).691.6 (from Corcyra))}}; also (from confusion with χοῦς A) _genitive_ χοός {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Eccl. 3.20, “PTeb.” 342.27 (2nd c.AD), “PBremen” 14.13}}; _dative_ χοΐ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 12(3), 248.10 (Anaphe, 2nd c.BC), 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**B.2** ={κονιορτός}, **dust,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.1Ki.18.38, +others, NT.Mark.6.11.}}
**B.3** χοῦς θανάτου **the grave,** {{LXX+5th c.AD+|LXX.Psa.21(22).16, compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}

5524:
** χορηγ-έω**,
_Boeotian dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ **χορ-ᾱγέω**, {{Refs|“IG” 7.3210 (Orchom.), 12(1).383 (from Rhodes)}}, etc.:— **lead a chorus,** χορῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 147, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 482b (compare signification 11): with _genitive_, χ. ἡμῶν [prev. author] “Lg.” 654a}}: hence _metaphorically_, **take the lead in** a matter, with _genitive_, τούτου τοῦ λόγου {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 179d.}}
**II** of the χορηγός, **defray the cost of bringing out a chorus** at the public festivals, _absolutely_, χορηγεῖν, τριηραρχεῖν, εἰσφέρειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.257; ἐχόρευες, ἐγὼ δ᾽ ἐχορήγουν interpolated [prev. work]265; χ. λαμπρῶς 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.12, etc.; κάλλιον 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.36: frequently in Inscrr., Θεμιστοκλῆς ἐχορήγει· Φρύνιχος ἐδίδασκεν· Ἀδείμαντος ἦρχεν cited in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 5, compare “IG” 12.770, etc.; also ὁ δῆμος ἐχορήγει “IG” 22.3079, +others: with _accusative_ _cognate_, χορηγίας χ. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.77, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.20; [τῇ φυλῇ] 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dem.Enc.” 45; χ. Ἀθηναίων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.724b}}: frequently with a word to denote the occasion, Λήναια χορηγῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1155 (Lyric poetry); χ. παισὶν Διονύσια 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.64; εἰς Ἀπολλώνια “IG” 11(2).106.1 (Delos, 3rd c.BC)}}; ἀνδράσι χ. ἐς Διονύσια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 21.2; χ. κωμῳδοῖς, πυρριχισταῖς, [prev. work]4; τραγῳδοῖς 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.60; κωμῳδοῖς “IG” 22.3090}} (less frequently with the _Article_ added, χ. τὰ Διονύσια τοῖς τραγῳδοῖς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 630); also Παναθηναίοις χ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.156}}:—_passive_, **to have choragi found for one,** χορηγοῦσιν μὲν οἱ πλούσιοι, χορηγεῖται δὲ ὁ δῆμος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 1.13}}; ἄριστα χορηγοῦνται οἱ παῖδες **are** well **found by their choragus,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.13.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, **minister to,** χ. ταῖς σεαυτοῦ ἡδοναῖς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.240; ταῖς ἐπιθυμίαις 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Par.” 12; πρὸς ἐπαινον 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 18.7; πρὸς μῆκος λόγου [prev. work] 13.26.}}
**II.3** _metaphorically_ also,
**II.3.a** with _accusative_ _person_, **furnish abundantly with** a thing, especially **with supplies for war,** χ. τὸ στρατόπεδον τοῖς ἐπιτηδείοις {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.68.8, compare 49.11, 52.7, etc.}}; χρήμασι πρός τι {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.42.7}}:—_passive_, **to be well supplied,** τοῖς ἐκτὸς ἀγαθοῖς κεχορηγημένος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1101a15, compare 1179a11: _absolutely_, κάλλιστα κεχορηγημένος}} best **furnished,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1288b14; κεχ. ἐπὶ τοσοῦτον ὥστε.. [prev. work] 1323b41; ἀρετὴ κεχορηγημένη [prev. work] 1289a33: generally, ἐμβαμματίοις κεχ., of fish, 4th c.BC: Anaxippus Comicus 1.35}};
**A** πολλαῖς ἀφορμαῖς κεχ. πρός τι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.77.2}}; διαφόρῳ φύσει, συνέσει, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.15, 2.6, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Veterum Censura” 5.6; κεχ. ὑπὸ τὴς φύσεως ἀγχινοίᾳ 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 26.2}}
**b** with _accusative_ _of things_ (with or without _dative_ _person_), **supply, furnish,** τοὺς Ἴβηρας οὓς χορηγεῖς μοι, i. e. the archers, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 551; χρήματα ἡμῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 11.6; τὰς τροφάς 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.35; σπόρον NT.2Cor.9.10; ἐξ ἰσχύος ἧς χ. ὁ θεός NT.1Pet.4.11}}; πάθη τὰ χορηγοῦντα βοήθειαν **affording,** i. e. admitting, a cure, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 13: with _dative_ _person_ only, τῷ βασιλεῖ LXX.3Ki.4.7}}:— _middle_ in _active_ sense, χορηγούμενός σοιτὸν φόρον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 920.29 (2nd c.AD):}} — _passive_, τῶν ἐκ μιᾶς δαπάνης χορηγηθέντων (i.e. δείπνων) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1281b3.}}

5525:
** χορός**, ὁ,
**dance,** αἰεὶ δ᾽ ἡμῖν δαίς τε φίλη κίθαρίς τε χοροί τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.248; μετὰ μελπομένῃσιν ἐν χορῷ Ἀρτέμιδος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.183; τοὶ δ᾽ ἄνδρες ἐν ἀγλαΐῃς τε χοροῖς τε τέρψιν ἔχον 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 272, compare 277; εἰς χ. ἐλθέμεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.508, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.194; οὐδέ κε φαίης ἀνδρὶ μαχεσσάμενον τόν γ᾽ ἐλθεῖν, ἀλλὰ χορόνδε ἔρχεσθ᾽ ἠὲ χοροῖο νέον λήγοντα καθίζειν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.393, 394; χορῷ καλὴ Πολυμήλη 16.180}}: later of the **dance** as a public religious ceremony, Διόνυσον τιμώσας χοροῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 220; φυλῆς Ἀκαμαντίδος ἐν χοροῖσιν 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 148, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.48, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.1}}; χοροὺς ἀνῆγον αἱ πόλεις (i.e. εἰς τὴν Δῆλον) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.104}}; **πεν-** ἀνδρῶν χ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 147, cf. Scholia 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.10}}; κύκλιος χ. (see. κύκλιος); θυσίῃσί σφεα (i.e. Δαμίην καὶ Αὐξησίην) καὶ χοροῖσι γυναικηΐοισι κερτόμοισι ἱλάσκοντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.83; ἵστασαν χοροὺς παρθένων τε καὶ ἠϊθέων [prev. author] 3.48; παιδικὸς χ. 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.40, etc.; χ. ἀνδρικός 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 665b}}; τραγικοὶ χοροί, at Sicyon, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.67}}: hence of the **chorus** in the Attic drama, οἱ χ. τῶν τραγῳδῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 787, compare “Pax” 807 (Lyric poetry)}}; χ. τραγικός, κωμικός, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1276b4}}; also χ. τρυγικός, τρυγῳδικός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 628 (_anapaest meter_), 886}}; arranged in six rows, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 173}}; ὃς οὐκ ἔδωκ᾽ αἰτοῦντι Σοφοκλέει χορόν (of the archon to whom the poet applied) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 15; χ. αἰτεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 513 (_anapaest meter_); διδόναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 383c, etc.}}; χορὸν λαβεῖν, ἔχειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 94, “Pax” 803, 807 (Lyric poetry)}}; χ. συλλέξαι, χοροὺς ἀθροίζειν (i.e. from the tribe), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 9.4; [χοροὺς] διδάσκειν [prev. passage]; χορὸν εἰσάγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 11: general phrases, χοροὺς ἱστάναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.48 (see. above), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 280; ἔστασεν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.114; ἱερὸν χ. ἵστατε Νύμφαις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 271 (_anapaest meter_), compare “Av.” 220 (_anapaest meter_); ἅψαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 307 (_anapaest meter_); χορῶν κατάστασις [prev. author] “Ag.” 23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 958; τοῖς χ. νικᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.4.3; χοροῦ προεστάναι [prev. passage]; χορῷ χορηγεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 482b, etc.}}
**II** **choir, band of dancers and singers, h. Ven.**{{NT+5th c.BC+|118, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.23, “fragment” 199; συμφωνία καὶ χοροί NT.Luke.15.25.}}
**II.2** generally, **choir, troop,** ἰχθύων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 762; μελιττῶν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 5.13; χ. καλλίμορφος τέκνων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 925, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 315b, “Tht.” 173b, etc.}}; of things, ἄστρων αἰθέριοι χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 467 (Lyric poetry), compare 2nd c.AD: Mesomedes Lyricus “Hymnus in Solem” 17}}; χ. σκευῶν **row** of dishes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 8.20}}; χ. δονάκων **row** of reeds, i. e. Pan's pipe, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Coluthus Epicus 124}}; χ. ὀδόντων **a row** of teeth, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 11.8 (hence οἱ πρόσθιοι χοροί}} for the front teeth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 548)}}; τὴν σοφίαν ποῦ χοροῦ τάξομε; in what **class** shall we place it? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 279c, compare 6th c.AD: Choricius Rhetor 12.28 p.160 F.-R.}}
**III** **place for dancing,** ἐν δὲ χ. ποίκιλλε.. Ἀμφιγυήεις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.590; λείηναν δὲ χ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.260, compare 264; ὅθι τ᾽ Ἠοῦς ἠριγενείης οἰκία καὶ χοροί εἰσι 12.4; Νυμφέων καλοὶ χ. ἠδὲ θόωκοι [prev. work] 318}}; at Sparta the ἀγορά was called χορός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 3.11.9; so perhaps in Crete, “Supp.epigram” 2.509.6 (Eltynia, probably 5th c.BC)}}: see below (Acc. to {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus χορός}} ={κύκλος},{στέφανος}, and therefore properly denotes **a ring-dance.**)

5526:
** χορτ-άζω**,
**feed, fatten,** properly of cattle {{Refs 8th c.BC+|(2nd c.AD: Aelius Dionysius Grammaticus “fragment” 326), χ. ἕλικας βόας ἔνδον ἐόντας 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 452}}; χορτάσω τὸν κάνθαρον (the beetle being comically treated as a horse), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 176; τούτοισι (i.e. σιτίοις).. τοῦτον χορτάσω [prev. work] 139}}; with _accusative_ _of things_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 372d}}:—_passive_, **eat their fill,** of cattle, {{Refs|[prev. work] 586a}}; τινος of a thing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 4.9.1.}}
**II** of persons, **feed,** βολβοῖς ἐμαυτὸν χορτάσω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 7.5; ἡ θυγάτηρ τὴν μητέρα οὐκ ἐχόρτασεν οὔτε ἡ μήτηρ τὴν θυγατέρα “Supp.epigram” 6.187 (from Phrygia)}}: with _genitive_, **fill full of**.., θεράπευε καὶ χόρταζε τῶν μονῳδιῶν (_metaphorically_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 154}}:—_passive_, with _accusative_, χορταζόμενοι γάλα λευκόν {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 142 (hexameters): with _dative_, χ. ἅπασιν ἀγαθοῖς 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 28: with _genitive_, στεμφύλων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 107: _metaphorically_, λύπης 3rd c.AD: Pseudo-Callisthenes Historicus 2.22; ζωῆς LXX.Tob.12.9; ἔν τινι LXX.Psa.16(17).15}}: _absolutely_, **feast,** κεχόρτασμαι.. οὐ κακῶς {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 30.1, cf. Araros 21, 4th c.BC: Nicostratus Philosophus 20, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 465, NT.Mark.7.27, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.9.19.}}

5527:
** χόρτ-ασμα**, ατος, τό,
mostly in _plural_, **fodder, forage,** for cattle, {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.4.3, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.42, 3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus 36 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, LXX.Gen.24.25, 32, +others}}
**2** **food** for men, {{NT|NT.Act.7.11 (_plural_).}}

5528:
** χόρτος**, ὁ,
properly **enclosed place** (see. entry near the end), but always with collateral notion of **a feeding-place**: in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”}}, **farmyard,** in which the cattle were kept, αὐλῆς ἐν χόρτῳ {{Refs|11.774; αὐλῆς ἐν χόρτοισι 24.640.}}
**2** generally, **any feeding-ground, pasturage,** frequently in _plural_, χόρτοι λέοντος, of Nemea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.44; χόρτοι εὔδενδροι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 134 (Lyric poetry)}}; χόρτος οὐρανοῦ **the expanse** of heaven, poetical cited in {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II** **fodder, provender,** especially for horses and cattle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.16 (of fish); θηρῶν ὀρείων χόρτον, οὐχ ἵππων λέγεις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 495}}; **grass,** {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 606, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 771, NT.1Cor.3.12; χ. κοῦφος}}**hay,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.10}}; χ. ἐβλάστησεν, ἐξηράνθη, {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Matt.13.26, NT.1Pet.1.24; ἄνθος χόρτου NT.Jam.1.10: opposed to σῖτος (food for man), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.41, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.6.12}}; χόρτον ἔχει ἔπὶ τοῦ κέρατος as translation of the Latin proverb, **foenum habet in cornu,** of a dangerous ox, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Crassus” 7.}}
**II.b** **green crop,** [γῆ] ἐσπαρμένη χόρτῳ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 27.72 (2nd c.BC), +others}}
**II.2** poetical, **food** generally, δούλιος χ. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 35.6, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 507 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.BC: Crates Thebanus Poeta Philosophus 10}}; compare χορτάζω. (Cf. Latin _hortus,_ Welsh **garth** 'fold, enclosure', Irish **gort** 'crop', 'field'.)

5530:
** χράω**.
**B**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}} FORMS: _contraction_ χρῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 35}}, _Ionic dialect_ χρᾷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.62 (also 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 3.2)}}; _infinitive_ χρᾶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.135 (also 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 19)}}; _Ionic dialect_ _participle_ χρέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 253, _feminine_ χρέωσα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.111}}; _Epic dialect_ χρείων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.79, “hymnus ad Apollinem” 396}}: _imperfect_ ἔχραον [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.92 (variant{ἔχρεον}), 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.454}}]; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔχρη {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 3.3, 4th-3rd c.BC: Hermesianax Elegiacus 7.89, (ἐξ-) 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 87}}: _future_ χρήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 132, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.19, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1083}}: _aorist_ ἔχρησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.156, etc.}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ ἐχρήσθην {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.49}}, etc.: _perfect_ κέχρησμαι (variant{κέχρημαι}) {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.164, 7.141}}: _pluperfect_ ἐκέχρηστο (variant{ἐκέχρητο}) {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.147, 151, 3.64, etc.}}:—_middle_, χρῶμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.126, etc.}}, _Ionic dialect_ χρέομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}}, _infinitive_ χρέεσθαι {{Refs|1.157 (χρᾶσθαι [prev. work] 172)}}; _participle_ χρεώμενος {{Refs|4.151:}}_imperfect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐχρέωντο (variant{ἐχρέοντο}) {{Refs|4.157, 5.82}}: _future_ χρήσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.492, etc.}}
**B.I** in _active_ of the gods and their oracles, **proclaim,** _absolutely_, χρείων μυθήσατο Φοῖβος {{Refs 6th c.BC+|8.79: χρείων ἐκ δάφνης γυάλων ὕπο Παρνησοῖο “hymnus ad Apollinem” 396: with _accusative_ _of things_, χρήσω ἀνθρώποισι Διὸς βουλήν [prev. work] 132, compare 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 807, Pi.[same place], 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.9.9; ἡ Πυθίη οἱ χρᾷ τάδε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.55, compare 4.155}}; χρῆσεν οἰκιστῆρα Βάττον **proclaimed** him the colonizer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.6}}; also in Trag., ὁ χρήσας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 798; χρήσειν ἔοικεν ἀμφὶ τῶν αὑτῆς κακῶν [prev. author] “Ag.” 1083; χρῇ μοι τοιαῦθ᾽ ὁ Φοῖβος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 35; σοὶ δ᾽ οὐκ ἔχρησεν οὐδέν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1268; χ. φόνον [prev. author] “El.” 1267: also with _accusative_ _cognate_, χ. χρησμόν [prev. author] “Ph.” 409; ὑμνῳδίαν [prev. author] “Ion” 681 (Lyric poetry)}}: with _infinitive_, **warn** or **direct by oracle,** ἔχρησας ὥστε τὸν ξένον μητροκτονεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 202; without ὥστε, [prev. work] 203}}; χρήσαντ᾽ ἐμοὶ.. ἐκτὸς αἰτίας κακῆς εἶναι **that** I should be.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ch.” 1030}}; with _infinitive_ _aorist_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 159}}: rare in _Attic dialect_ Prose, τάδε ὁ Ἀπόλλων ἔχρησεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.80.10; τὸν Ἀπόλλω ταύτην τὴν γῆν χρῆσαι οἰκεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.102; τοῦ θεοῦ χρήσαντος [prev. author] 5.32, compare 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 99; ἔχρησεν ὁ θεός “SIG” 1044.5 (Halic., 4th-3rd c.BC)}}; ὁ θεὸς ἔχρησε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).122.78 (Epid., 4th c.BC).}}
**B.II** _passive_, **to be declared, proclaimed** by an oracle, τίς οὖν ἐχρήσθ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 792}}; mostly of the **oracle delivered,** τὰ ἐκ Δελφῶν οὕτω τῷ Κροίσῳ ἐχρήσθη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.49; τὰ χρηστήρια ταῦτά σφι ἐχρήσθη [prev. author] 9.94; ἠπίως χρησθῆναι [prev. author] 7.143}}; τὸ χρησθέν, τὰ χρησθέντα, the **response,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.63, 7.178; ἐν Πυθῶνι χρησθὲν παλαίφατον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.39; πεύθου τὰ χρησθέντ᾽ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 604}}; χρησθὲν αὐτῷ ἐν Νεμέᾳ τοῦτο παθεῖν **since it was foretold** him **by an oracle** that.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.96}}; ἃ τοῦδ᾽ ἐχρήσθη σώματος which **were declared** about it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 355; τὸν κεχρησμένον θάνατον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.164 (-χρημ- codices)}}; τοῦ κακοῦ τοῦ κεχρησμένου [{{Refs|[prev. author] 7.141 (variant{-χρημ-}): _impersonal_, with}}]_infinitive_, καί σφι ἐχρήσθη ἀνέμοισι εὔχεσθαι{{Refs|[prev. work] 178}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ἐκέχρηστό σφι.. τοῦτον βασιλεύσειν {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.147}}; with _infinitive_ _aorist_, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.220.}}
**B.III** _middle_, of the person to whom the response is given, **consult a god** or **oracle,** with _dative_, ψυχῇ χρησόμενος Θηβαίου Τειρεσίαο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.492, 565}}; χ. θεῷ, χρηστηρίοισι, μαντηΐῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.47, 53, 157; τῷ θεῷ 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.124; χ. μάντεσι Μούσαις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 724 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 686a; ὅσοι μαντικὴν νομίζοντες οἰωνοῖς χρῶνται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.3}}; χ. χρηστηρίῳ εἰ.. **inquire at** the oracle whether.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.57: _absolutely_, ὑπέρβη λάϊνον οὐδὸν χρησόμενος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.81, compare “hymnus ad Apollinem” 252, 292; ἀπέστειλε ἄλλους χρησομένους 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.46}}; οἱ χρώμενοι the **consulters,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 957; χρωμένῳ ἐν Δελφοῖς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.126; also χ. περὶ τοῦ πολέμου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.220, compare 1.85, 4.150, 155, etc.}}; κεχρημένος **having inquired of an oracle,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1398b33: with}}_infinitive_, σωφρονεῖν κεχρημένον **being divinely warned** to be temperate, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 829, compare 4th c.AD(?): Marcellinus Biographus “Vita Thucydidis” 6}}: later simply, **receive a divine revelation,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.3.14.— 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} has the word in this sense only in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”: the}}_active_ only in _present_ _participle_ χρείων (_future_ χρήσω {{Refs|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 132): the}}_middle_ only in _participle_ _future_ χρησόμενος.
**B** **furnish with** a thing, in which sense the _present_ was **κίχρημι**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.12, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 29}}; Cretan dialect _3rd.pers._ _singular_ κίγχρητι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Inscription Cretan dialect” 1 xxiii 3 (Phaestus, 2nd c.BC)}}; Delph. _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _present_ _subjunctive_ κιχρῇ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 324.17 (4th c.BC):}}_aorist_ χρέη{{Refs|[prev. work]13}}; _present_ _participle_ κιχρέντε {{Refs|[prev. work]adn. (rarely}}**χρηννύναι**, **χρηννύω**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 5.10, 10.13}}: _middle_, χρηννυόμεθα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 304.4 (3rd c.BC))}}: _future_ χρήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.58:}}_aorist_ ἔχρησα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. passage], 6.89, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 219, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.18, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.24, “IG” 12.108.16, etc.}} (_3rd.pers._ _singular_ written ἔκχρησεν {{Refs|“IG” 12(3).1350.4 (from Thera))}}; _imperative_ χρῆσον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1159, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus}} Comedy texts 205: _perfect_ κέχρηκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 461, 598, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 29.21.6 (= 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 31.10)}}: _pluperfect_ ἐκεχρήκει {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 2.29:}} —_passive_, _perfect_ κέχρημαι (δια-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.11}}:—_middle_, _present_ κίχρᾰμαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.534b}}; _infinitive_ κίχρασθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 30.20}}: _imperfect_ ἐκιχράμην{{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.584.10}}: _aorist_ ἐχρησάμην, _imperative_ χρῆσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 191 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.:—**furnish the use of** a thing, i.e. **lend,** usually in a friendly way, δανείζω being the word applied to usurers (but χ. ={δανείζω} in {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista 54), [same places]; οὐ δεδωκώς, ἀλλὰ χρήσας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1162b33, cf. LXX.Exo.11.3; ἡ πειρατικὴ δύναμις χρήσασα ταῖς βασιλικαῖς ὑπηρεσίαις ἑαυτήν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 24; χ. τὴν ἑαυτοῦ σχολήν τισι [prev. author] “Phil.” 13}}; χ. τὰν χέρα, in the formula of manumission, {{Refs|“IG” 9(1).189, 194 (from Tithora)}}:—_middle_, **borrow,** τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 191 (Lyric poetry), 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 30.20: _absolutely_, χρησαμένη γὰρ ἔνησα καὶ οὐκ ἔχω ἀνταποδοῦναι Batrachomyomachia 186}}; πόδας χρήσας, ὄμματα χρησάμενος having **lent** feet and **borrowed** eyes, of a blind man carrying a lame one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.13 (Plato Junior Epigrammaticus), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Demodocus” 384b, 384c.}}
**B.II** ={χρηματίζω}{{Refs|111, τοῦ χρέοντος γραμματέως “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2562.18 (assuming variant, Hierapytna).}}

5531:
** χράω**,
**A**used in _Epic dialect_ only in _aorist_ 2, **fall upon, attack, assail,** with _dative_ _person_, στυγερὸς δέ οἱ ἔχραε δαίμων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.396; τίς τοι κακὸς ἔχραε δαίμω; 10.64; so ἠϊθέοις οὐκ ἔστι τόσος πόνος, ὁππόσος ἡμῖν.. ἔχραε “Anthologia Graeca” 5.296 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus): compare ἐπιχράω (B).}}
**A.II** with _accusative_ _of things_, **inflict** upon a person, κακὸν δέ οἱ ἔχραε κοῖτον {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 315.}}
**A.III** with _infinitive_, **conceive a desire** to.., τίπτε σὸς υἱὸς ἐμὸν ῥόον ἔχραε κήδειν ἐξ ἄλλω; why **did he want** (or **needed he**) to vex my stream of all others? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.369}}; μνηστῆρες.., οἳ τόδε δῶμα ἐχράετ᾽ ἐσθιέμεν καὶ πινέμεν ye suitors.., who **have become so eager** to.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.69. (For significations 1, 11, compare ζαχρηή; for 111 perhaps compare χρή, κέχρημαι (χράω (B) C), χρῇ, χρῇς.)}}

5532:
** χρεία**
(written **χρέα** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 25.2, 148 (3rd c.BC))}}, _Ionic dialect_ **χρείη** {{Refs|Callias Epigrammaticus in “PSI” 11.1216.43}}, ἡ: (χράομαι, κέχρημαι):— **need, want,** χρείας ὕπο {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 287}}; ἵν᾽ ἕσταμεν χρείας considering in what great **need** we are, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1443}}; χρείᾳ πολεμεῖν to war with **necessity,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 191 (_anapaest meter_)}}: with _genitive_, **want of**.., φαρμάκων χρείᾳ κατεσκέλλοντο {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 481; ἐν χρείᾳ τύχης [prev. author] “Th.” 506}}; ἐν χρείᾳ δορός in the **need** or **stress** of war, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 963; φορβῆς χρείᾳ [prev. author] “Ph.” 162 (_anapaest meter_), compare 1004: ἵππων ἡμῖν χρεία μὲν οὔτε τις πολλῶν οὔτε πολλή [ἐστιν] 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 834b}}; ἦ μὴν ἔτ᾽ ἐμοῦ χρείαν ἕξει will have **need** of my help, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 170 (_anapaest meter_)}}, cf. Call.[same place]; ἀφίκοντο εἰς χρείαν τῆς πόλεως came to feel **the need** of its assistance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 244d}}; ἰατρῶν ἐν χρείαις ἐσόμεθα, ἐν χρείᾳ ἡγεμόνος εἶναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 373d, 566e; ὅτου σε χ. ἔχει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 646}}; so τίς χ. σ᾽ ἐμοῦ [ἔχει]; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 976, compare χρεώ 1.2: χρείαν ἔχω}} with _infinitive_, {{NT|NT.Matt.3.14}} (followed by (ίνα, {{NT+4th c.BC+|NT.John.2.25); significations 1.1 and 111 in the same sentence, οὐχ οὕτως χ. ἔχομεν τῆς χ. παρὰ τῶν φίλων ὡς.. 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 34: proverbial, χ. διδάσκει, κἂν βραδύς τις ᾖ, σοφόν}} '**necessity** is the mother of invention', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 715, compare “El.” 376, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 263: _plural_, αἱ χρεῖαι τολμᾶν βιάζονται 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.1; αἱ τοῦ σώματος χ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.12.5; πρώτη γε καὶ μεγίστη τῶν χ. ἡ τῆς τροφῆς παρασκευή 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 369d; αἱ ἀναγκαῖαι χ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.148, compare 45.67 (_sing._); πολεμικαὶ χ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1328b11.}}
**2** **want, poverty,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 175 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 420, etc.; διὰ τὴν χ. καὶ τὴν πενίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 534 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**3** **a request of necessity,** opposed to ἀξίωσις (a claim of merit), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.37, compare 33}}: generally, **request,** τὴν πρίν γε χ. ἠνύσασθ᾽ ἐμοῦ πάρα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 700}}; κἀγὼ.. τοιάνδε σου χ. ἔχω make such **a request** of or to thee, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ch.” 481.}}
**II** **business,** ὡς πρὸς τί χρεία; for what **purpose?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1174, compare 1435; χρῆσθαί τινι χρείαν ἣν ἂν ἐθέλωσι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 868b; δοῦναι ἑαυτὸν εἰς τὴν χ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.16.11.}}
**II.b** especially **military** or **naval service,** ἡ πολεμικὴ χ. καὶ ἡ εἰρηνική the **employments** of war and of peace, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1254b32}}; αἱ κατὰ θάλατταν [χ.], ἡ ἐν τῇ γῇ χ., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.52.1, 31.21.3}}; οἱ ἐπὶ τῶν χ.{{LXX+1st c.BC+|Aristeas 110, LXX.1Mac.12.45; οἱ πρὸς ταῖς χ. LXX.Jdt.12.10; οἱ ἐπὶ χρειῶν τεταγμένοι “BGU” 543.1 (1st c.BC)}}; in military sense, **action, engagement,** αἱ κατὰ μέρος χ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.84.7, +others}}
**II.c** generally, **business, employment, function,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.45.2, etc.}}; ἡ ἐγκεχειρισμένη χ. the **duty** assigned, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 741.11 (2nd c.BC)}}; οὓς καταστήσομεν ἐπὶ τῆς χ. ταύτης {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Act.6.3; χ. πολιτικαί 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 32, etc.}}
**II.d** **a business, affair, matter,** like{χρέος}, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.49.9, +others}}; τὴν ὑπὲρ τούτων χ. the **study** of these things, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.29U.}}
**II.e** χ. ἀναγκαία **need** of nature, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.33; τροφῆς χ. 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.472.}}
**III** **use,**
**III.1** as a property, **use, advantage, service,** χρείης εἵνεκα μηδεμιῆς {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 62; τῆς χ. τοῦ παιδὸς ἀποστερηθῆναι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.4; ἡ χ. τῆς ῥητορικῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 480a; πωλοῦντες τὴν τῆς ἰσχύος χ. [prev. author] “R.” 371e}}; χρείαν ἔχειν τοῖς ἀνθρώποις to be **of service** to mankind, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 204c}}; τὰ οὐδὲν εἰς χρείαν things of no **use** or **service,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Prooemia” 56.3}}; χρείαν ἔχει εἴς τι is of **service** towards.., [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC(?): Sosipater Comicus 1.41; for 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 725, see at {ἐρευνάω} 1: _plural_, χρεῖαι.. φίλων ἀνδρῶν}}] **services rendered** by them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.42}}; χρείας παρέχεσθαι render **services,** Decrees cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.84, “IG” 22.654.15, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.16.8 (_sing._)}}; ἵνα σοι τὰς χ. παρέσχωμαι (sic) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 498 (3rd c.BC)}}; μεγάλην παρεῖχε χ. τοῖς κοινοῖς πράγμασιν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.97.4}}; παρέχειν χ. to be **serviceable, useful,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Aristo Stoic. 1.79; ἑξήκοντα καὶ τριακόσια χρειῶν γένη παρέχον δένδρον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.724e; χ. ναυτικαί}}**equipments,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 2.10.}}
**III.2** as an action, **using, use,** κτῆσις καὶ χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.4.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 451c}}; ἐν χρείᾳ εἶναι in **use,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 87c}}; κατὰ τὴν χ. for **use,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 330c; πρὸς τὴν ἀνθρωπίνην χ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.25}}; ἡ χ. τῶν λόγων the **employment** of words, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 239d, compare “Plt.” 272d: _plural_, λάμπει γὰρ ἐν χρείαισιν ὥσπερ.. χαλκός}} is made bright by **constant use,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 864.}}
**IV** of persons, **familiarity, intimacy,** τινος **with** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.63}}: generally, **any relation of business** or **intercourse,** ἐν χρείᾳ τινὶ τῇ πρὸς ἀλλήλους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 372a; ἡ πρὸς ἀλλήλους χ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1376b13; [Νικόμαχος] συνεβίω Ἀμυντᾳ.. ἰατροῦ καὶ φίλου χρείᾳ}}**in the relationship, capacity,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 5.1.}}
**V** Rhet., **pregnant sentence, maxim,** frequently illustrated by an anecdote, Sen.{{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Ep.” 33.7, 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “προγυμνάσματα” 3, Aphthon, “Prog.” 3, 1st-2nd c.AD(?): Theon Rhetor “Progymnasmata - Rhet.” 5, etc.}}: _plural_, title of works by Zeno {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 6.91), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristippus Philosophus, etc.; by 3rd c.BC: Macho Comicus}}, a collection of sayings of courtesans, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.577d; ἡ τοῦ Κλεομένους χ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.218a; χρεῖαι καὶ ἱστορίαι [prev. work]78f.}}

5534:
** χρή**,
_impersonal_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.216, etc.}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **χρῆ** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 20, 35, 46}}; other forms are contractions (crases) of χρή (probably originally a _neuter_ _substantive_) with forms of εἰμί (**sum**): _future_ χρἤσται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 504, “fragment” 599, 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 103, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 362, 5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 34 (on this form, for which codices generally have χρῆσται or χρῆσθαι, see Scholia 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “OC” [same place])}}: _subjunctive_ χρῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 999, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 49, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 133; _optative_ χρείη 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 215, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 162, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.44, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 4c}}; _infinitive_ χρῆναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 276, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 210, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.84, etc.}}; also χρῆν (see. below 111); _participle_ _neuter_ _plural_ χρηεόντα (or χρὴ ἐόντα) {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 174}}: _imperfect_ ἐχρῆν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.44, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 907, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 107.6 (only here in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, χρῆν l. 5), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 152, +others, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.114, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.22, +others, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.57, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 335c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.3, +others, etc.}}: frequently also without the augment, χρῆν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 123.1, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 529, 579, “Tr.” 1133, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 535 (_anapaest meter_), +others, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 8.6, +others, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.63, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.33, +others, etc.}}; both forms in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 540, ἐρεῖ τις, οὐ χρῆν· ἀλλὰ τί ἐχρῆν εἴπατε}}: _future_ χρήδει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.8}}: —**it is necessary**: with _infinitive_ praes. aut _aorist_, **it must needs, one must** or **ought** to do (like δεῖ, which is only once used in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus), νῦν δὲ χ. τετλάμεν ἔμπης 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.209; τὸν νῦν χ. κομέειν 6.207, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.216, 4.57, etc.; σήμαιν᾽ ὅ τι χ. σοι συμπράσσειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 297 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ὅτι χ. πάσχειν ἐθέλω{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1067; ὅ τι χρείη ποιεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 4c, compare 9a; τούτου θανειν χρῆν αὐτὸν οὕνεκ᾽ ἐκ σέθε; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 579; χρὴ τὸ λέγειν 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 6.1}}: more frequently with _accusative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, **one must,** ἐμὲ δὲ χ. γήραϊ πείθεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.644; τῷ σε χ. πόλεμον.. παῦσαι 7.331}}; οὐδέ τί σε χ. νηλεὲς ἦτορ ἔχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|9.496; τί χ. με.. στέγειν ἢ τί λέγει; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 135 (Lyric poetry): χρῆν γὰρ Κανδαύλῃ γενέσθαι κακῶς (where γενέσθαι is _infinitive_ of an _impersonal_ verb) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8.}}—Sts. the _infinitive_ must be supplied from the context, especially in [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus in phrases like{τίπτε μάχης. ἀποπαύεα; οὐδέ τί σε χρή}}}] (i.e. ἀποπαύεσθαι) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.721, compare 19.420}}; ὅθι χ. πεζὸν ἐόντα (i.e. μάρνασθαι) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.50}}; so in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, πορθεῖν ἃ μὴ χ. (i.e. πορθεῖν) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 342}}; φύς τ᾽ ἀφ᾽ ὧν οὐ χρῆν (i.e. φῦναι), ξὺν οἷς τ᾽ οὐ χρῆν (i.e. ὁμιλεῖν) ὁμιλῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1184}}; ἔκανες ὃν οὐ χρῆν (i.e. κτεῖναι) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 930}}; ἐπιπλεύσειέ τις ὡς χ. (i.e. ἐπιπλεῦσαι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.89}}; θύσαντες οἷς χ. (i.e. θῦσαι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 415e; ἀκαιρότερον ὄντα ἢ χ. [prev. author] “Plt.” 307e; λαλεῖν μετὰ ἀφελείας ἔνθα χ. 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 34.2.; so ὡς χ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1556 (_anapaest meter_), etc.}} —The _imperfect_ frequently expresses something that ought to have been, but is not, ἐνθάδ᾽ οὐ παραστατεῖ, ὡς χρῆν, Ὀρέστης{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 879, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1133; and sometimes stands for χρή, χρῆν τι λέγειν ὑμᾶς σοφὸν ῳ νικήσετε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 487 (_anapaest meter_), compare 432: _absolutely_, ἐρεῖτις, οὐ χρῆν· ἀλλὰτί ἐχρῆν εἴπατε [prev. author] “Ach.” 540.}}
**2** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus without}}_infinitive_, with _accusative_ _person_ et _genitive_ _of things_, οὐδέ τί σε χρὴ ἀφρποσύνης thou **hast** no **need of** imprudence, i. e. it does not **befit** thee, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.109; οὐ μέν σε χ. ἔτ᾽ αἰδοῦς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.14; τί με χ. μητέρος αἴνο; 21.110}}; μυθήσεαι ὅττεό (i.e. ὅτου) σε χ. {{Refs|1.124; τέο σε χ.; 4.463: τί χρὴ φίλων}} is found in most codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 667 (δεῖ codex V).}}
**3** with _dative_ _person_ pro _accusative_ is not found; in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.490, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 3}}, the _dative_ belongs to the _infinitive_ μέλει; in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 736}} Dobree restored με for γ; in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 886 ᾗ}} depends on μετεῖνα; in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Ion” 1316 Dobree read τοὺς δέ γ᾽ ἐνδίκους, and in 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 28.10 δικαίους.. ὑφελομένους was restored by Cobet.}}
**II** sometimes in a less strong sense, πῶς τοῦτο περᾶσαι χρ; how **is one to** get through this? {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.45}}; τί ἐχρῆν με ποιεῖ; what **was I to** do? {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.28}}; ἓν οὐδὲν κατέστη ἴαμα ὅτι χρῆν προσφερόντας ὠφελεῖν there was no one remedy by the application of which one **could** (or **was bound to**) help them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.51, compare 1.91.}}
**III** τὸ χρῆν (_infinitive_) **fate, destiny,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 260 (assuming variant, τὸ χρὴ Nauck)}}; τὸ χρή {{Refs|[prev. author] “HF” 828}}; conjecture for τὸ χρεών (_monosyllable_) in {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 733.3, “IT” 1486.}}

5535:
** χρῄζω**,
{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 958, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 376, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 891 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.109, etc.; in _Attic dialect_ hardly found except in}}_present_ and _imperfect_ (but see below {{Refs|11)}}: _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ **χρηΐζω**, as always in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, and 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.41, 5.19, 30, 9.87}}; written **χρηιίζω**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 57.41 (Milet., 5th c.BC)}}; also **χρεΐζω**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7.64}}; _Doric dialect_ **χρῄζω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 56.23 (Argos, 5th c.BC)}}; also **χρήζω** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work]1006.3 (Cos, 3rd c.BC)}}; **χρείζω** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work]953.27 (Calymna, 2nd c.BC)}}: sicil. _Doric dialect_ **χρῄσδω** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 8.11}}; Megarian dialect _Doric dialect_ **χρῄδδω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 734}}: _future_ χρῄσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 99a}}, _Ionic dialect_ χρηΐσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.38}}: _aorist_ _Ionic dialect_ χρηΐσαι probably {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.65}}; _participle_ χρηΐσας{{Refs|[prev. work] 20, 7.38}}: (χρή):—**want, lack, have need of,** with _genitive_, χρηΐζοντα.. ἰητῆρος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.835; εἴρετο.. ὅττευ χρηΐζων ἱκόμην 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.121, compare 558; οὐδ᾽ ἐμοῦ διδασκάλου χρῄζεις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 376}}; δύο χρῄσει [μεσοτάτων] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus}} [same place]: _absolutely_ in _participle_ χρηΐζων **needy, poor,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.340, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 351.}}
**2** **desire, long for, crave,** χρηΐζειν ἀπεόντος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 367; τοῦτον ὦν δοκέω.. ποιήσειν ὧν ἂν χρηΐζωμεν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.30; χρημάτων χ. [prev. author] 9.87; βορᾶς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 530}}; τοῦ μακροῦ χ. βίου Soph.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Aj.” 473: rarely with _accusative_, πᾶν μᾶλλον δοκέων μιν χρηΐσειν ἢ τὸ ἐδεήθη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.38; ὥστ᾽ ἄλλα χρῄζειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 595, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 123}}; an _infinitive_ may frequently be supplied, φράζε.. ὅ τι χρῄζεις (i.e. φράζειν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 359, compare 453 (both _anapaest meter_); ἴθ᾽ ὅποι χρῄζεις (i.e. ἰέναι) [prev. work] 891 (_anapaest meter_),compare “Th.” 751, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 928, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 365}}; τί δῆτα χρῄζει;{{Refs|[prev. work] 622, “OC” 643.}}
**2.b** with _accusative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, **ask** or **desire** that one should do a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.41, 112, 152, +others}}; so with _genitive_ _person_ et _infinitive_, **desire of** one to do, {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.19, 65, 9.55}}; in Trag., with _infinitive_ only, **desire** to do a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 235, 285, +others, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 91, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 347, etc.: rare in Prose, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.109, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.15.}}
**2.c** with _double_ _genitive_ _person_ et _of things_, τῶνδε ἐγὼ ὑμέων χρηΐζων συνέλεξα hdt.{{Refs|7.53.}}
**2.d** χρῄζειν παρά τινος with _infinitive_, Ps.-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus “Vitae Homeri” 17.}}
**3** _participle_ χρῄζων is used _absolutely_ for εἰ χρῄζει, **if one will, if one chooses,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 958, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 340 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ἄλλα φανεῖ χρῄζων (i.e. Ἑρμῆς) **if propitious,** {{Refs|[prev. work] [815] (Lyric poetry)}}; ἢν τὸν θεὸν χρῄζοντ᾽ ἔχῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 597}}; ποταγγελλέτω ὁ χρῄζων, ={ὁ βουλόμενος}, {{Refs|“IG” 12(1).677.34 (from Ialysus)}}; also τὸ χρῇζον **your solicitation, E.IA** 1017.
**II** _passive_, χρησθείς is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 24.}}

5536:
** χρῆμα**, ατος, τό:
(χράομαι):— **need,** in the phrase παρὰ χ. or παραχρῆμα (which see); **a thing that one needs** or **uses,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 1.9f (_plural_)}}: hence in _plural_, **goods, property** (χρήματα λέγομεν πάντα ὅσων ἡ ἀξία νομίσματι μετρεῖται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1119b26), 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.78, 203, +others (never in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”), 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 320, 407, etc.}}; of temple-**treasures, heirlooms,** etc., {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“Mnemos.” 57.208 (Argos, 6th c.BC)}}; τὰ ἱρὰ χ. τῆς Ἀθηναίης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.28, compare 9.81; θησαυρούς.. ἄλλα τε χρύσεα ἄφατα χ. [prev. author] 7.190; πολλῶν χ. ἐξαίρετον ἄνθος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 954; πειρῶ τὸν πλοῦτον χρήματακαὶ κτήματα κατασκευάζειν· ἔστι δὲ χ. μὲν τοῖς ἀπολαύειν ἐπισταμένοις, κ. δὲ τοῖς κτᾶσθαι δυναμένοις 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.28; τοῖς σκεύεσι καὶ τοῖς χρήμασιν ἀποθήκη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.97}}; πρόβατα καὶ ἄλλα χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.4; τὰ ἀνδράποδα.. καὶ χρήματα τὰ πλεῖστα ἀπέδρα αὐτούς [prev. work] 7.8.12: proverbial, χρήματα ψυχὴ πέλεται.. βροτοῖσι}} a man's **money** is his life, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 686}}; χρήματ᾽ ἄνηρ '**money** makes the man', {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 49, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.11; ἐν χρήμασιν οἰκεῖ πατρώοις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 757, compare “Ch.” 135; also χρημάτων πένητες 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 37; τὰ χρήματ᾽ ἐνεχυράζομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 241; χρήματα πορίζειν [prev. author] “Ec.” 236; ἄτιμοι ἦσαν τὰ σώματα, τὰ δὲ χ. εἶχον 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.74; χρημάτων ἥσσων 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 50; χρημάτων κρείσσων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.60; χρήμασι νικώμενος [prev. passage]; χρημάτων ἀδωρότατος [prev. work] 65; ἐλπίδα χρήμασιν ὠνητήν [prev. author] 3.40; μήτε χρημάτων φειδομένους μήτε πόνων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 78a; ζημιοῦσθαι χρήμασιν [prev. author] “Lg.” 721b}}; even of debts, διαλῦσαι τὰ χ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.12; δεθέντ᾽ ἐπὶ χρήμασιν ἐν τῷ δεσμωτηρίῳ [prev. author] 24.168.—Acc. to 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 9.87 the _Ionic dialect_ used also the _sing._ in this sense, and so we find, ἐπὶ κόσῳ ἂν χρήματι..}}; for how much **money**.. ? Answ. ἐπ᾽ οὐδενί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.38; ταύτην (i.e. τὴν χλανίδα) πωλέω μὲν οὐδενὸς χ. δίδωμι δὲ ἄλλως [prev. work] 139}}; also in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 197}}, χ. δ᾽ ὃ μὲν Διόθεν καὶ σὺν δίκῃ ἀνδρὶ γένητα; in _Attic dialect_, οὐδενὸς ἂν χ. δεξάμενοι at no **price,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.4}}; and in later Prose, **fund, sum of money, Arch. f. Religionswiss.**{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|10.211 (Cos, 2nd c.BC)}}; τὸ πλῆθος τοῦ χ. {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.106, cf. NT.Act.4.37, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 1.20; χρήματα}}**merchandise,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 90, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.6.37, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.74}}; **property, substance,** {{Refs|Berl.Sitzb. 1927.161 (from Cyrene).}}
**II** generally, **thing, matter, affair,** especially in _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 332, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 344, 402; χρημάτων ἄελπτον οὐδέν 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 74; πάντων χ. δικαιότατον 6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 8; πρῶτον χρημάτων πάντων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.145}}; ἀντὶ πάντων χ. on every **account,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.21; δεινότατον ἁπάντων χρημάτων [prev. work] 1}}; πᾶν χ. ἐκίνεε 'left no stone unturned', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.96}}; τεκμαίρει χρῆμ᾽ ἕκαστον 'deeds show the man', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.74; πάντων χ. μέτρον ἄνθρωπος 5th c.BC: Protagoras Philosophus 1}}; περαίνεται τὸ χ. the **issue** is being decided, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 47}}: _plural_, simply, **things,** ὁμοῦ πάντα χ. ἦν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 1, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 440a, “Euthd.” 294d, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.2.1.}}
**II.2** χρῆμα is frequently expressed where it might be omitted, δεινὸν χ. ἐποιεῦντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.16; οἷόν τι χ. ποιήσειε [prev. work] 138}}; ἐς ἀφανὲς χ. ἀποστέλλειν ἀποικίην to send out a colony without any certain **destination,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.150}}; frequently in Trag., τί χρῆμ; ={τί}; **what?** τί χ. λεύσσ; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 300, “Ch.” 10}}; or **why?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 512}}; so in _genitive_, τοῦ χ. (i.e. ἕνεκα); {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1223; τί χ. δρᾷ; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 288, compare “Ph.” 1231; τί χ. πάσχε; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 909}}; τί δ᾽ ἐστὶ χρῆμ; what is **the matter?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 885; πικρόν τί μοι δοκεῖ χ. εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 485b; ἡδύ [prev. author] “Tht.” 209e, +others}}; μάλιστα χρημάτων most **of anything,** i. e. **certainly,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|Anonymus “Oxy.” 1611.68 (3rd c.AD);compare χρέος 11.2.}}
**II.3** used in periphrases to express something strange or extraordinary of its kind, ὑὸς χ. μέγα a huge **monster** of a boar, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.36; ἦν τοῦ χειμῶνος χ. ἀφόρητον [prev. author] 7.188}}; τὸ χ. τῶν νυκτῶν ὅσον what a **business** the nights are! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 2}}; λιπαρὸν τὸ χ. τῆς πόλεως what a grand city! {{Refs|[prev. author] “Av.” 826, compare “Lys.” 83}}; κλέπτον τὸ χ. τἀνδρός a thievish **sort** of fellow, {{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 933; τὸ χ. τοῦ νοσήματος [prev. author] “Lys.” 1085}}; μακάριον.. λέγεις τυράννου χ. your tyrant-**creature,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 567e; χ. θαυμαστὸν γυναικός 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 31}}: without a _genitive_, ἔλαφον, καλόν τι χ. καὶ μέγα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.8}}; σοφόν τοι χρῆμ᾽ ἄνθρωπος truly a clever **creature** is he! {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.83}}; κοῦφον χ. ποιητής ἐστιν καὶ πτηνὸν καὶ ἱερόν, of the poet, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 534b}}; χ. καλόν τι such a fine **thing!** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.23}}; also in a periphrastic use, οὐδὲν χ. τοῦ ἀγκῶνος κάμψαι δύνανται cannot bend the elbow **at all,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 42.}}
**II.3.b** so, to express a great number or mass, as we say, **a deal, a heap of**.., πολλόν τι χ. τῶν τέκνων, χ. πολλὸν ἀρδίων, νεῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.109, 4.81, 6.43; χ. πολλόν τι χρυσοῦ [prev. author] 3.130; σμικρὸν τὸ χ. τοῦ βίου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 953}}; ὅσον τὸ χ. παρνόπων what a **lot** of locusts! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 150; ὅσον τὸ χ. τοῦ πλακοῦντος [prev. author] “Eq.” 1219; πολὺ χ. τεμαχῶν [prev. author] “Pl.” 894}}; τὸ χ. τῶν κόπων ὅσον what a **lot** of them! {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ra.” 1278; τῶν λαμπάδων ὅσον τὸ χ. [prev. author] “Th.” 281}}; also of persons, χ. θηλειῶν woman**kind,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 198; σφενδονητῶν πάμπολύ τι χ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.5; μέγα χ. Λακαινᾶν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 18.4: without a _genitive_, ὅσον τὸ χ. ἐπὶ δεῖπνον ἦλθε}} what a **crowd**.. ! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1192.}}
**III** (χράω (B) A) **oracle,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 115.1.}}

5537:
** χρημᾰτ-ίζω**,
_future_ **-ίσω**{{NT|NT.Rom.7.3}}, _Attic dialect_ **χρημᾰτ-ιῶ** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 37}}: _perfect_ κεχρημάτικα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.103, “OGI” 106.7 (Egypt, 2nd c.BC)}}: (χρῆμα):—Prose Verb, **negotiate, have dealings,** especially in money matters (in this sense mostly _middle_ (see #11)), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.87, 5.61, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.81.5; χ. τι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.62, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.157, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 18.}}
**2** of public assemblies, **deliberate,** περὶ Εὐριπίδου ὅ τι χρὴ παθεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 377, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1298b29, “Rh.” 1359b3, Legal cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.8; τὰ λοιπὰ τῶν δημοσίων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 38; περὶ ὧν ἂν ἅπαξ γνῷ τὸ δικαστήριον, πάλινχρηματίσαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.55}}; of presiding officers, **conduct business,** Decrees cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.75, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.23}}; of the βουλή, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.169; ὅσα δεῖ χρηματίσαι τὴν βουλήν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 43.3.}}
**2.b** with _dative_, **transact business with,** τῇ βουλῇ, τῷ δήμῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 3.1}}; **negotiate with,** πόλεσι περὶ φιλίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.5: _absolutely_, [prev. work] 61}}; ἰδίᾳ χ., of intriguing persons, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.278; χ. ὑπὲρ δημοσίων καὶ κοινῶν πραγμάτων 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 3.4}}:—_middle_,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 3.3.}}
**3** **give audience to,** πρεσβευταῖς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.66.6, compare 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 1.13a.}}
**4** of an oracle, **give a response** to those who consult it, {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.Jer.33(26).2, +others, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.10, “JAJ” 11.8.4, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.435c, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 2.48; δι᾽ ὕδατος 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 3.11}}; of gods, **give ear to,** χ. τοῖς εὐχομένοις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pseudol.” 8}}:—_passive_, **receive an answer, warning,** in “NT” of **divine warnings** or **revelations,** {{NT|NT.Matt.2.12, etc.; ὑπ᾽ ἀγγέλου NT.Act.10.22}}; ἦν αὐτῷ κεχρηματισμένον **a warning had been given** him, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Luke.2.26; χ. ὑπὸ δαιμονίων καὶ φαντασίας εἰδώλων 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 67.5.}}
**5** **issue** ordinances, etc., χ. ἀπορρήσεις {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.438}}; **administer justice,** ἐν τῷ Προσωπίτῃ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” [same place]; ταῖς πόλεσι 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Ἰβηρική” 98.}}
**5.b** **issue orders** for payment, **pay,** ἀπὸ τῆς.. τραπέζης {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PGrenf.” 2.23.4 (2nd c.BC)}}; τινι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Ostr.Bodl.” i248 (2nd c.BC)}}; λόγον χ. ἐς τὰ δαμόσια γράμματα **furnish** an account.., **Arch. f. Religionswiss.** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|10.211 (Cos, 2nd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, ἐχρηματίσθη πολλὰ διάφορα he **was furnished with** large sums, {{Refs|Aristeas 9.}}
**6** **take cognizance of, decide upon** petitions, [ἐντευξιν] χ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEnteux.” 75.9 (3rd c.BC), “PFay.” 12.28 (2nd c.BC)}}; ἔντευξις κεχρηματισμένη {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.3 (3rd c.BC).}}
**7** generally, **have dealings with, stand in any relation to** a person, οὐδὲν αὐτῷ (sic legendum videtur) πρὸς γένος ἐχρημάτιζεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Ctesias Historicus “fragment” 29.2: hence even μόλις ταῖς ἀναγκαίαις [ὀρέξεσι] χ.}} **to be influenced, affected** by them, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.125b.}}
**8** Astrology texts, **operate,** of influences, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 5.7.}}
**II** _middle_, χρηματίζομαι: _future_ _Attic dialect_ **-ιοῦμαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 29.14, etc.}}: _perfect_ κεχρημάτισμαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.15}}:—**negotiate** or **transact business for oneself** or **to one's own profit, make money,** οἰόμενοι χρηματιεῖσθαι μᾶλλον ἢ μαχεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.13; χρηματιούμενος ἀλλ᾽ οὐ πρὸς ὑμᾶς φιλοτιμησόμενος 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator [same place]; οἱ χρηματισάμενοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330c; ἄλλῳ χ. καὶ οὐχ αὑτῷ [prev. author] “Grg.” 452e}}; especially by base arts, ἐξ αὐτῆς τῆς πόλεως {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator [same place], compare 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 9.25}}; χ. ἀπό τινος **to make money** of or from a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 225e; ἀπὸ τῶν κοινῶν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1286b14; ἀπὸ γεωμετρίας 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de communi mathematica scientia” 25; ἔκ τινος 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.3; ἐ, φιλοσοφίας 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.1; also with _accusative_ _cognate_, χ. τὸν ἐκ γῆς χρηματισμόν [prev. author] “Lg.” 949e, compare “Grg.” 467d; χρήματα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.5.}}
**II.2** generally, **transact business, have dealings with**.., τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.118, 7.163.}}
**II.3** with _accusative_ _of things_, χ. τὸ νόμισμα **traffic in** money, like a money-lender or banker, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1257b34}}; but with _accusative_ _person_, χ. τινας **make money out of** any one, i. e. **get** it **from** them **by extortion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 32.5.13; so χ. παρὰ τῶν νεωτέρων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.6.}}
**III** in later writers, from {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus downwards, the}}_active_ χρηματίζω takes some special senses:
**III.1** **to take and bear a title** or **name, to be called** or **styled** so and so, χρηματίζειν βασιλεύς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.57.2, 30.2.4, cf. Aristeas 298; Πτολεμαῖος.. νέος Διόνυσος χ. 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.44}}; ἐχρημάτιζε Χαλκηδόνιος, Κρητικός, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 13.1.55, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Σικελική” 6; νέα Ἶσις ἐχρημάτιζε 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 54}}; μὴ πατρόθεν, ἀλλ᾽ ἀπὸ μητέρων χ. **to call themselves** not after their fathers, but after their mothers, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] 2.248d; χ. ἀπὸ τοῦ δήμου 1st-2nd c.AD: Harpocratio Grammaticus}} see at {δημοτευόμενο; χ. τοὺς μαθητὰς Χριστιανούς} {{NT|NT.Act.11.26}}; τιμῆς καὶ πίστεως χ. ἄξιοι **to be deemed**.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 2.111.}}
**III.2** generally, **to be called,** μοιχαλίς {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Rom.7.3: μήτηρ 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.440}}; καὶ ὡς χ. 'and so forth' (omitting some of the writer's names), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 100.1 (2nd c.AD), etc.; also with _dative_, ἀεὶ -ίζων τῷ προκειμένῳ ὀνοματίῳ [prev. work] 2131.8 (3rd c.AD).}}
**III.3** **change** or **be changed,** εἴς τι {{Refs|“Geoponica” 12.1.9.}}

5538:
** χρημᾰτ-ισμός**, ὁ,
**negotiation, giving audience to ambassadors,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 28.16.10 (_plural_)}}; ἐποιεῖτο τὸν χ. καὶ τοὺς λόγους{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 16.4, compare 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 63.20 (_plural_).}}
**2** **decree** or **ordinance,** made by a sovereign or some public authority, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.64, 70 (but χ. στρατηγῶν}} **business** introduced by the generals, Decrees Del. cited in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 14.10.14)}}; **decision** on a case or petition, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 25.2 (2nd c.BC), “OGI” 139.18(_plural_, Philae, 2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**3** **administration of justice by** χρηματισταί, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 513 (3rd c.BC).}}
**4** any **public instrument** or **document,** {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.13, “Supp.epigram” 3.367.34 (Lebad., 2nd c.BC), LXX.2Mac.11.17}}; **public records,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|PEnteux. 22.9 (3rd c.BC), “PPar.” 65.15 (2nd c.BC), “Chron.Lind.” 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 7 (_plural_), “Supp.epigram” 3.674.32 (Rhodes, 2nd c.BC).}}
**4.b** generally, **deed, legal instrument,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Arch.Papyrus 4.130iii 41 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; δημόσιος χ. notarial **instrument,** Mitteis {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Chr.” 200.9 (3rd c.AD), etc.: _plural_, [prev. work]192.35 (Edict of Mettius Rufus, 1st c.AD)}}; τῶν δούλων τοὺς χ. the **title-deeds** referring to the slaves, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PUniv.Giss.” 20.8 (2nd c.AD).}}
**5** **oracular response, divine injunction** or **warning,** {{LXX+NT+2nd c.AD+|LXX 2. LXX.Mal.2.4, NT.Rom.11.4, 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.2, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 1.7, “PMag.Par.” 1.2206.}}
**II** (from _middle_) **doing business for one's own gain, money-making,** frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, ἀμελήσας χρηματις μοῦ καὶ οἰκονομίας “Ap.” 36b; ἰάτρευσις καὶ ὁ ἄλλος χ. “R.” 357c; χ. διὰ βαναυσίας καὶ τόκων “Lg.” 743d; ὁ ἐκ γῆς χ. [prev. work] 949e: _plural_, “R.” 465c, “Lg.” 741e, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.50; χρηματισμός, οὐ λῃτουργία γέγονεν ἡ τριηραρχία 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.167.}}
**III** later, **appellation, title, designation**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“SIG” 739.2 (Delph., 1st c.BC), 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.48 (_plural_)}}; **use of a name** or **designation,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Arch.Papyrus 4.122v7 (2nd c.AD)}}; simply, **name,** Dosith.p.382K.
**IV** 'affair', χ. εὔχρηστος (of σπάργανα) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.111 (sed to be read σχημ-.)}}

5539:
** χρήσῐμ-ος**, η, ον,
also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.8.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 480b, “R.” 333b}}: (χράμαι):— **useful, serviceable,** first in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 406; εἰς ἀνάγκαν, ἔνθ᾽ οὐ ποδὶ χρησίμῳ χρῆται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 878 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὸ χ. φρενῶν the **excellence** of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1740 (Lyric poetry); τὸ αὐτίκα χ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.56; ἡ διὰ τὸ χ. φιλία 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1159b13; τὰ χ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 579}}; χ. εἴς τι **useful** for something, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.109, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 493 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “R.”}}[same place]; ἐπί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Grg.” [same place]; πρός τι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 482 (_comparative_)}}; ἰδίᾳ ἑκάστῳ χ. καὶ ὑπὲρ τοῦ κοινοῦ ὠφέλιμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.34; τοῦτ᾽ οὖν τί ἐστι χρήσιμο; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 202}}; χρήσιμόν ἐστι, with _infinitive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Av.” 382 (troch.).}}
**2** of persons, **serviceable, useful,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 410, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.7, etc.}}; _comparative_ **-ώτερος**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 819c}}: especially, like{χρηστός}, **a good and useful citizen,** χ. πόλει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 910; χ. πολίτης 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 118; χ. τινι 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator “fragment” 16.1; ἐπί τι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.31}}; τοὺς εὐπόρους δεῖ χ. αὑτοὺς παρέχειν τοῖς πολίταις to show themselves **useful, serviceable** to the state, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 42.22, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 887, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator “fragment” 10.1 (_comparative_)}}; τοῖς σώμασι -ώτεροι more **able**-bodied, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 5.9}}; opposed to ἀργαλέος τὴν ὄψιν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.61.}}
**3** **used, made use of,** τέμενος -ώτατον a **muchfrequented** sanctuary, uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.178.}}
**4** χρησίμη διαθήκη an **available** (i.e. **authentic**) will, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.30.}}
**5** νομίσματα οὐ χρήσιμα ἔξω money **that will** not **pass** abroad, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 3.2.}}
**II** _adverb_, -μως ἔχειν to be **serviceable,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.44, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.5.9}}; χ. τινὶ σωθῆναι **with advantage** to him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.91, compare 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 6.2.9; τὰ -μως λεγόμενα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.36d.}}

5540:
** χρῆσις**, εως, ἡ,
(χράομαι)
**Iemployment, use** made of a thing, ἀνέμων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 11(10).2; χρημάτων 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 282; οἰκίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 394d, compare “SIG” 987.33 (Chios, 4th c.BC)}}; τὴν κατ᾽ ἀξίαν χ. ποιοῦνται ἑκάστῳ (perhaps to be read ἑκάστου) {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “Protrepticus” 5}}; **use, practice,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 4}}; in _plural_, **uses, advantages,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.30}}; αἱ ἐς τὰ πολεμικὰ χ. the **uses** of war, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.5.7; αἱ πολιτικαὶ χ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1267a23}}; opposed to κτῆσις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 238b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1098b32, 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Familiares” 7.29.1}}; opposed to πώλησις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 3.9.}}
**I.2** **usefulness,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.5}}; opposed to ἀχρηστία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 333d; ἐς χρῆσιν κρατύνεσθαι}}**so as to become useful,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 27}}; ἔχειν χρῆσιν to be **useful,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 11.8.}}
**I.3** **intimacy, acquaintance,** ἡ οἰκειότης καὶ ἡ χ. [τῆς πόλεως] {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator “Epistulae” 2.14; ἡ χ. ἡ πρὸς ἀλλήλους 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1280a36; αἱ οἴκοι χρήσεις 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.11; ἡ τῶν ἀφροδισίων χ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 841a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 581b13, compare “Pol.” 1262a34 (_plural_), NT.Rom.1.26}}; τὰ ἐν χρήσει **familiar** objects, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.4.37.}}
**I.4** Grammars, **usage,** of words, ἡ ἐξαλλαγὴ τῆς συνήθους χ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Amm.” 2.3}}; ἀναστρέφων τὰς χ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work]2,compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 119.24, +others; ἡ Πλάτωνος χ. [prev. author] “Pron.” 72.18}}; in concrete sense, **example** of a word or use, πυκνῶς αἱ χ. παρὰ Αἰολεῦσιν {{Refs|[prev. work] 66.3}}; **passage** cited, _falsa lectio_ for{κρίσει} in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 4.3}}; indicated by the symbol ☧, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|Anonymus “Oxy.” 1611.56 (3rd c.AD)}}; ☧ Ἀριστοφάνους (referring to {{Refs|“Av.” 1181) “Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 2.452.}}
**II** (χράω (B) A), **oracular response,** ἀπὸ κείνου χρήσιος at his **bidding,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.76.}}
**III** (χράω (B) B), **lending, loan,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1131a4, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.23.4, 1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 106.}}

5543:
** χρηστός**, ή, όν,
(χράομαι) of things, like{χρήσιμος}, **useful, good of its kind, serviceable,** [τόξα] χρηστὰ οὐδέν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.78; [ἀτραπὸς] οὐδὲν χ. τισι [prev. author] 7.215; χ. ἐπίπλοα [prev. author] 1.94}}; [γῆ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 594}}; οἰκία, opposed to μοχθηρά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 504a}}; ἡ χ. μέλιττα, opposed to οἱ κηφῆνες, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 624b23}}: frequently of **wholesome** food, μελίτωμα {{Refs|Batrachomyomachia 39}}; ποτόν, σῖτος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 438a; περὶ τὸ σῶμα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 313d}}: with _genitive_, **for** a thing, νεύρων **for** the sinews, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 14.21; ῥάφανος 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 15.8; ὄψον 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 242, etc.}} (but **pleasant** to taste, **nice,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 2.10): generally, πολιτεία 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.135; βίος 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.179}}; of victims and omens, **auspicious,** ἱρά, σφάγια, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.44, 9.61, 62}}; τελευτὴ χ. a **happy** end or issue, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.157; εἰ.. τοῦτό γε δοκέει ὑμῖν εἶναι χρηστόν [prev. author] 5.92.ά}}: _plural_, τὰ χ., as _substantive_, **benefits, kindnesses,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.41, 42; χρηστὰ φέρειν [prev. author] 4.139}}; χρηστόν τι συμβουλεύειν, χρηστὰ ἐπιτηδεύειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 793, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.9}}; χρηστὰ λέγειν, πράττειν, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 725, 787, etc.: but τὰ χ.}} also, **happy event,** ἐκτελοῖτο δὴ τὰ χ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 228 (troch.)}}; **prosperity, success,** τὰ χ. δ᾽ αὔθ᾽ ἕκαστ᾽ ἔχει φίλους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1227.}}
**2** in moral sense, opposed to κακός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus in “PSI” 11.1213.2}}; opposed to πονηρός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 313d}}; τὸ χ., opposed to τὸ αἰσχρόν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 476}}; χρηστός, opposed to λυπρός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 601: but λῦπαι χρησταί}} if **working for good,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 499e.}}
**3** **good for its purpose, effective** (even for evil), τραῦμα, δῆγμα, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Symp.” 44, “Alex.” 55.}}
**4** Grammars, **in use, current,** ποιηταῖς χρηστά {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 215.8.}}
**II** of persons, **good,** especially in war, **valiant, true,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.109, 6.13, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 437, etc.}}: generally, **good, honest, worthy,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 610; οἰκέται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 9.5}}; of women, ἐρεῖ τις ὡς Κλυταιμνήστρα κακή· Ἄλκηστιν ἀντέθηκα χρηστήν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 117.11, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 634}}; of **good** citizens, **useful, deserving,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.7: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἃ χρηστοὶ ἐγένεσθε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.64; χ. περὶ τὴν πόλιν γεγενημένος 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.31; χ. καὶ φιλόπολις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 900; collectively, ὀλίγον τὸ χ. [prev. author] “Ra.” 783}}; but also ironically, ὁ χ. οὑτοσί {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Nu.” 8; οἱ χ. πρέσβεις οὗτοι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.30, compare 89; ἐκλελάκτικεν ὁ χ. ἡμῖν μοιχός 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 16.}}
**II.b** frequently on Epitaphs, {{Refs|“IG” 3.3149,3155, +others}}
**II.c** with _infinitive_, ὅσοι προβατεύειν χ. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Orationes” 14.32.}}
**II.2** οἱ χρηστοί, like{οἱ ἀγαθοί}, those of **good** family, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 1.4, 6.}}
**II.3** of the gods, **propitious, merciful, bestowing health** or **wealth,** θεῶν χρηστῶν ἥκειν εὖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.111, compare 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 9.11.}}
**II.4** of men, **good, kindly,** δούλῳ.. χ. γενόμενός ἐστι δεσπότης πατρίς {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 265; ὡς ἡδὺ δούλῳ δεσπότου χρηστοῦ τυχεῖν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 556, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 227; ὁ χ., ὡς ἔοικε, καὶ χρηστοὺς ποιεῖ 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 203b, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Phocion” 10; χ. περί τινα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.2; ἐπί τινας NT.Luke.6.35; εἰς ἀλλήλους NT.Eph.4.32.}}
**II.4.b** sometimes **simple, silly,** like{εὐήθης, χρηστὸς εἶ ὅτι ἡγῇ}.., you're **a nice fellow,** to think that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 264b, compare “Tht.” 161a; ὦ χρηστέ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.318.}}
**II.5** of a man, **strong, able** in body for sexual intercourse, ={γυναικὶ χρῆσθαι δυνάμενος}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Genit.” 2.}}
**II.6** of the **dead,** whence χρηστὸν ποιεῖν ={ἀποκτιννύναι}, in a treaty between the Spartans and Tegea, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 592.}}
**III** _adverb_ **-τῶς** **well, properly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.117, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 32; χ. ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 219; σκευάσαι χ. τοὖψον 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 149.6.: ironically, χ. τὴν πατρίδα ἐπετρόπευσας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.36.}}

5544:
** χρηστότης**, ητος, ἡ,
**goodness, honesty, uprightness,** χρηστότητα ἀσκεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 872; μέγιστον ἀγαθόν ἐστι μετὰ νοῦ χ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 788, compare 472.1}}; χρηστότητος εἵνεκα as a reward for **honesty,** {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristopho Comicus 14.4 (troch.), 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 8.17; ποιεῖν χ. LXX.Psa.13(14).3}}; ἀκολουθεῖ τῇ ἀρετῇ χ.) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Virtutibus et Vitiis” 1251b33}}; ἡ σὴ χ., as a honorific address, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PGiss.” 7.15 (2nd c.AD), “PLond.” 2.411.6 (4th c.AD), etc.}}
**II** **goodness of heart, kindness,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 2.7}} (but in depreciatory sense, **soft-heartedness,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 579); εὔνοιαν καὶ χ. παρέσχητο 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.9.9}}; χ. καὶ φιλοστοργία, φιλανθρωπία καὶ χ., {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agis” 17, “Comp. Dem.Cic.” 3, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 8, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 73.5; ἡ χ. καὶ ἡ φιλανθρωπία τοῦ θεοῦ NT.Tit.3.4, cf. NT.Rom.11.22, +others; χ. ἐθ᾽ ἡμᾶς NT.Eph.2.7}}; ποιεῖν χρηστότητα to show **kindness,** {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.Psa.118(119).65; πολλὰ τῇ χ... κτῶνται 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.262S.}}
**II.2** **simplicity, silly good nature,** ἤθους ἀπλαστία μετ᾽ ἀλογιστίας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Definitiones” 412e.}}

5545:
** χρῖσμα** ατος, τό,
(χρίω) later form for χρῖμα (which see), found in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.4, “An.” 4.4.13, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Odoribus” 8, 15, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.4, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.402, etc.}}
**II** **anointing, unction,** {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Exo.29.7, 35.15, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.892.}}
**II.2** in “NT” of spiritual **grace,** χρῖσμα ἔχετε ἀπὸ τοῦ ἁγίου {{NT|NT.1John.2.20, compare 27.}}
**III** **coating** of wall, **plaster,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.9, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 62. (The usual accent χρίσμα is wrong, compare χρῖμα.)}}

5547:
** χριστός**, ή, όν,
(χρίω) **to be rubbed on, used as ointment** or **salve,** opposed to πιστός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 480, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 516, Triclin.ad 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.1}}; τὸ ἔλαιον τὸ χ. **anointing** oil, {{LXX|LXX.Lev.21.10.}}
**II** of persons, **anointed,** ὁ ἱερεύς ὁ χ. {{LXX|[prev. work] 4.5, 16, 6.22: _plural_,LXX.2Mac.1.10.}}
**II.2** especially of the Kings of Israel, ὁ χ. Κυρίου{{LXX|LXX.1Ki.24.7, cf. LXX.Psa.17 (18). 51; also τῷ χ. μου Κύρῳ LXX.Isa.45.1}}; _plural_, of the patriarchs, {{LXX|LXX.Psa.104 (105). 15.}}
**II.3** in **NT,** ὁ χ. the **Messiah,** {{NT|NT.Matt.2.4, etc.; ὁ χ. Κυρίου NT.Luke.2.26}}; then used as _proper name_ of Jesus, Ἰησοῦς χ. {{NT|NT.Matt.1.1, etc.; Ἰησοῦς ὁ λεγόμενος χ. [prev. work] 16.}}

5548:
** χρίω**,
_Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ χρῖον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.252, also χρίεσκε 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.871}}: _future_ χρίσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 789}}: _aorist_ ἔχρῑσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.364, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ χρῖσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.680, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.49}}: _perfect_ κέχρῑκα {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.10.1, +others}}:—_middle_, _future_ χρίσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.220:}}_aorist_ _participle_ χρῑσάμενος{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 96, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 523, etc.}}:—_passive_, _future_ χρισθήσομαι {{LXX|LXX.Exo.30.32}}: _aorist_ ἐχρίσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 675, 5th c.BC: Achaeus Tragicus 10}}: _perfect_ κέχρῑμαι {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.189, 195, Magnes 3, etc., later κέχρισμαι LXX.2Ki.5.17:}}_pluperfect_ ἐκέχριστο _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.2}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐκέχριντο {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC(?): Callixinus Historicus 2}}. [Even in _present_ and _imperfect_ ι is long, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.179 (ἐπι-χρῑοντες), 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.186, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 675, etc.}}; χρῐει only in late Poets, as {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.275 (3rd c.BC(?): Nossis Epigrammatica): in}}_future_ and all other tenses ῑ without exception, whence the proper accent. is χρῖσαι, κεχρῖσθαι, χρῖσμα, etc.:—**touch the surface of a body slightly,** especially of the human body, **graze,** hence,
**I** **rub, anoint with scented unguents** or **oil,** as was done after bathing, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, λόεον καὶ χρῖον ἐλαίῳ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.252; ἔχρισεν λίπ᾽ ἐλαίῳ 3.466; λοέσσαι τε χρῖσαί τε 19.320}}; of a dead body, χρῖεν ἐλαίῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.186}}; **anoint** a suppliant, {{Refs|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.170 (from Cyrene)}}; πέπλον χ. **rub** or **infect** with poison, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 675, compare 689, 832 (Lyric poetry): _metaphorically_, ἱμέρῳ χρίσασ᾽ οἰστόν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 634 (Lyric poetry); οὐ μέλανι, ἀλλὰ θανάτῳ χ. τὸν κάλαμον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.841e}}:—_middle_, **anoint oneself,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.96; κάλλεϊ ἀμβροσίῳ οἵῳ.. Κυθέρεια χρίεται 18.194, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 523; ἐλαίῳ 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.417; ἐκ φαρμάκου 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 13:}} with _accusative_ _of things_, ἰοὺς χρίεσθαι **anoint** (i. e. poison) **one's** arrows, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.262}}:—_passive_, χρίεσθαι ὑπὸ τοῦ ἡλίου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.124}}; βακκάριδι κεχριμένος Magnes [same place]; συκαμίνῳ τὰς γνάθους κεχριμέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 98.3: _metaphorically_, Σοφοκλέους τοῦ μέλιτι κεχριμένου 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 581.}}
**I.2** in LXX, **anoint in token of consecration,** χ. τινὰ εἰς βασιλέα {{LXX+NT|“4 Ki.” 9.3; εἰς ἄρχοντα LXX.1Ki.10.1; εἰς προφήτην LXX.1Ki.19.16; also χ. τινὰ τοῦ βασιλεύειν LXX.Judg.9.15: with double _accusative_, χ. τινὰ ἔλαιον NT.Heb.1.9.}}
**II** **wash with colour, coat,** αἰγέαι κεχριμέναι ἐρευθεδάνῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.189}}; πίσσῃ{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work] 195, compare “Inscription Délos” 442 A 188 (2nd c.BC)}}; ἀσφάλτῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.22 (_passive_)}}; στοάν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Supp.epigram” 4.268 (Panamara, 2nd c.AD):}} —_middle_, τὸ σῶμα μίλτῳ χρίονται **smear their** bodies, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.191.}}
**III** **wound on the surface, puncture, prick, sting,** of the gadfly in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 566, 597, 880 (all Lyric poetry):}} —_passive_, ὀξυστόμῳ μύωπι χρισθεῖσ᾽{{Refs|[prev. work] 675.}}

5549:
** χρον-ίζω**,
(χρόνος):
**I** _intransitive_, **spend time,** περὶ Αἴγυπτον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.61.}}
**I.2** **last, continue,** τὸ μὲν καλῶς ἔχον ὅπως χρονίζον εὖ μενεῖ βουλευτέον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 847; ἐν τῇ ὑστέρᾳ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 523a23}}; χρονίζωσι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 537a7; οὐ χ. τὸ ἀλγοῦν συνεχῶς ἐν τῇ σαρκί 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 4, compare 4th c.BC: Diogenes Cynicus Philosophus Oen.58.}}
**I.3** χ. δρῶν **persevere** in doing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 255b.}}
**I.4** **take time, tarry, linger,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1356, “Ch.” 64 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.49, 8.16}}; κεχρονικότες, opposed to ὑπόγυιοι τῇ ὀργῇ ὄντες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1380b5; κεχρονικὼς ἐν Ῥώμῃ 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 33.16.6; χρονίσαι κατὰ τὸ βαλανεῖον 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.417; ἡ ναῦς καὶ τὸν χρόνον τοῦτον ὃν ἐπιστέλλω σοι χρονίζει 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 14}}: with _infinitive_, **delay** to do, χ. καταβῆναι {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.32.1 (also χ. τοῦ ποιῆσαί τιLXX.Gen.34.19), NT.Luke.12.45.}}
**I.5** of ailments, **to be** or **become chronic,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 3.28.}}
**I.6** of wine, **to be** or **become old, to have age,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.33a.}}
**II** _passive_, **to be prolonged** or **delayed,** τῶνδε πίστις οὐκ ὄκνῳ χρονίζεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 54, compare “Ch.” 957 (Lyric poetry); πολέμου χρονισθέντος 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.27; [τὴν εὔνοιαν] χρονιζομένην.. φιλίαν γενέσθαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1167a11; χ. ἐν τῷ σώματι}}**continue,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Pr.” 907b22; τὰ κεχρονισμένα νοσήματα 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).31.}}
**II.2** **grow up,** χρονισθεὶς δ᾽ ἀπέδειξεν ἔθος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 727 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.3** **to be located in time, made temporal,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 716.11, 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 405.}}

5550:
** χρόνος**, ὁ,
**time,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (see. below), etc.: distinct from καιρός, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.35, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Ammonius Grammaticus “περὶ ὁμοίων καὶ διαφόρων λέξεων” p.79 V.}}; τῶν δὲ πεπραγμένων ἀποίητον οὐδ᾽ ἂν χ. δύναιτο θέμεν τέλος P.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|“O.” 2.17; μυρίος χ. [prev. author] “I.” 5(4).28, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 618; μακρὸς κἀναρίθμητος χ. [prev. author] “Aj.” 646; ὁ πᾶς χ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.46, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 484; πρόπας χ. [prev. work] 898; ἐς τὸ πᾶν χρόνου [prev. work] 670}}; but in Prose, τοῦ χ. τὸν πλεῖστον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.30, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.41; τὸν πρῶτον τοῦ χ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 1.5; τὸν δι᾽ αἰῶνος χ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 554}}; χρόνου πολλοῦ δέονται take **a** long **time,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.4, etc.; δότε τι τῷ χ. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.86.}}
**b** **time** in the abstract, ἀμερὴς χ. Timo 76; τριμερής {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 10.197, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.153b; defined by 4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.26, 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus [prev. work] 3.260.}}
**2** **a definite time, period,** δεκέτης, τρίμηνος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 715 (Lyric poetry), “Tr.” 164}}; χ. βίου, ἥβης χ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 670, “El.” 20; πολὺν ἀριθμὸν χρόνου γεγονότες 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.49: _plural_, of points or periods of time, τοῖς χ. ἀκριβῶς}} with **chronological** accuracy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.97}}; τοῖς χ. **by** the **dates,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.36}}; μετενεγκόντα τοὺς χ. altering the **dates,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.225; μακρῶν καὶ πολλῶν χρόνων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 798b; τεσσαράκοντα χρόνους ἐνιαυτῶν “IG” 5(1).728.7 (from Sparta), compare 14.1747.3 (from Rome)}}; χρόνων μῆκος (uncertain, to be read χρόνου) {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Choricius Rhetor 35.51 p.403 F.-R.}}
**2.b** **date, term** of payment due, {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 1.10, +others}}
**2.c** **year,** Ἑλληνικά 1.233 {{Refs 1st c.BC+|(Rhamnus, 1st c.BC), “PLond.” 2.417.14 (4th c.AD), “App.Anth.” 6.154.1 (to be read εἷς ἔτι), Ps.- 1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Centil.” 24, compare “Etymologicum Magnum” 254.13.}}
**2.d** **equatorial degree,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 44, 4th c.AD: Paulus Alexandrinus Astrologus “A.” 2, +others, “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 5(1).240.}}
**3** Special phrases:
**3.a** _accusative_, χρόνον **for a while, for a long** or **short time,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.599, 6.295, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.175, 7.223, etc.}}; πολὺν χρόνον **for a** long **time,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.161; δηρὸν χ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.206; οὐκ ὀλίγον χ. 19.157; τοῦτον τὸν χ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.75}}; ἐς τὸν αἰὲν χ. for ever, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 207 (Lyric poetry)}}; οὐ πολὺς χ. ἐξ οὗ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|.. Pl.R. 452c; παλαιὸς ἀφ᾽ οὗ χρόνος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 600 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἦν χρόνος ἐν ᾧ.., or ὅτε.., Linusap.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius “Prooem.” 4, 5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 25.1 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; ἕνα χ.}}**once for all,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.511.}}
**3.b** _genitive_, χρόνου περιιόντος as **time** came round, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.155}}; so χ. ἐπιγενομένου, διεξελθόντος, προβαίνοντος, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.28, 2.52, 3.53}}; χρόνου γενομένου after **a time,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.109}}; ὀλίγου χρόνου **in a** short **time,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134; πολλοῦ.. οὐχ ἑόρακά πω χρόνου 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 98}}; οὐ μακροῦ χ., τοῦ λοιποῦ χ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 478 (Lyric poetry), 817; βαιοῦ κοὐχὶ μυρίου χ. [prev. author] “OC” 397; ποίου χρόνο; 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 278}}; πόσου χ.; after how **long?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 83.}}
**3.c** _dative_, χρόνῳ **in process of time,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 18, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80, 176, +others: frequently in Trag., as 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 126, 463, “Ch.” 650 (all Lyric poetry); also χρόνῳ κοτέ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.62; τῷ χ. ποτέ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 865}}; χρόνῳ, χρόνοις ὕστερον, **long** after, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.8, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.39}}; οὐ χρόνῳ **immediately,** Ps.Democr.Alch.p.49B.: also with _Article_, τῷ χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 66, 1242.}}
**3.d** ὁ ἄλλος χ., in _Attic dialect_, of **past time,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.16, ὁ λοιπὸς χ.}} of **future,** see at {λοιπός} 3; so χ. ἐφέρπων, ἐπαντέλλων, μέλλων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.97, 8.28, 10(11).7}}; also κατὰ χ. ἱκνούμενον or κατὰ χ. [τὸν] ἱ. at a later (or the fitting) **time,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 27.4 (compare ἱκνέομαι 111.2).}}
**4** with Preps.:—ἀνὰ χρόνον in course of **time,** after **a time,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.173, 2.151, 5.27, +others}}
**4.b** ἀφ᾽ οὗ χρόνου from such **time** as.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.13.}}
**4.c** διὰ χρόνου after **a time,** after **an interval,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 758, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 904, “Pl.” 1055, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.94; διὰ χρόνου πολλοῦ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.27; διὰ π. χ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1476; διὰ μακρῶν χρόνων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 22d: but χρόνος.. διὰ χρόνου προὔβαινέ μοι}} means **one space of time** after **another,** day after day, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 285.}}
**4.d** ἐκ πολλοῦ τευ χ. a long **time** since, long **ago,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.58.}}
**4.e** ἐν χρόνῳ, like{χρόνῳ}, **in course of time, at length,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 1000 (Lyric poetry)}}; **for a long time,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 278d; ἐν πολλῷ χρόνῳ [prev. work] 228a}}; ἐν χρόνοισι perhaps **formerly,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus] “Sphaer.” 108 (to be read Κάρπιμος).}}
**4.f** ἐντὸς χρόνου within **a certain time,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.104.}}
**4.g** ἐπὶ χρόνον for **a time,** for **a while,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.299, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.193, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.116; πολλὸν ἐπὶ χ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.407; χρόνον ἐπὶ μακρόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.81}}; παυρίδιον or παῦρον ἐπὶ χ., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 133, 326.}}
**4.h** ἐς χρόνον **hereafter,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.72, 9.89.}}
**i** μετὰ χρόνον after a **time,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.52, etc.}}; μέχρι τοῦ αὐτοῦ χ. up to the same **time,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.13.}}
**k** πρὸ τοῦ καθήκοντος χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.126; so τοῦ χρόνου πρόσθεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 461.}}
**l** σὺν (ξὺν) χρόνῳ, like{χρόνῳ} or διὰ χρόνου, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1378, “Eu.” 555 (Lyric poetry).}}
**m** ὑπὸ χρόνου by **lapse of time,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.21: but ὑπὸ αὐτὸν τὸν χ.}} **about** the same time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.165, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.100 (_plural_).}}
**II** **lifetime, age,** ὁ μακρὸς ἀνθρώπων χρόνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 306; χρόνῳ παλαιοί [prev. author] “OC” 112; χρόνῳ μείων [prev. work] 374}}; τοσόσδε τῷ χ. so far gone **in years,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 365b; χρόνῳ βραδύς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 875.}}
**III** **season** or **portion of the year,** περιγράψαι τοῦ ἔτους χρόνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.12.}}
**IV** **delay,** οὐδ᾽ ἐποίησαν (perhaps ἐνεποίησαν) χ. οὐδένα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.163; χρόνον δ᾽ αἱ νύκτες ἔχοντι}}**linger,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.25}}; χρόνους ἐμποιεῖν to interpose **delays,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.93.}}
**V** Grammars,
**V.1** **tense** of a verb, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Thucydide” 24, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 123.17, 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 638.3.}}
**V.2** **time** or **quantity** of a syllable, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 39.4, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 130.4, +others: βραχὺς χ. a short syllable, [prev. work] 309.23}}; of the augment, {{Refs|[prev. work] 237.10.}}
**V.3** in Rhythmic and Music, **time,** διαιρεῖται ὁ χ. ὑπὸ τῶν ῥυθμιζομένων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoxenus Musicus “Rhythmica” p.79}} W., etc.; ὁ πρῶτος [χ.] **time-unit,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. passage], 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1st c.AD: Quintilianus Rhetor Latinus 1.14, etc.}}; χρόνος κενός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work]18: frequently in _plural_, λέξις εἰς χρόνους τεθεῖσα διαφέροντας 4th c.BC: Aristoxenus Musicus “Rhythmica” p.77 W., cf. Anonymus Rhythmicus “POxy.” 9ii6; [μέτρα] προχωρεῖ ἕως λ χρόνων 3rd c.AD(?): Aristides Quintilianus Musicus 1.23.}}

5551:
** χρονοτρῐβέω**,
**waste time, loiter,** {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1406a37, 1st-2nd c.AD: Leonidas Medicus cited in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.225b, NT.Act.20.16, Men.Prot. p.21 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 39.29 (2nd c.BC).}}
**2** with _accusative_, χ. τὸν πόλεμον **protract** the war, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 53, 4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.242 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}

5552:
** χρύσεος**, η, ον,
also ος, ον in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.30 (1st c.BC: Antipater Thessalonicensis Epigrammaticus)}}, _Attic dialect_ _contraction_ **χρυσοῦς**, ῆ, οῦν {{Refs|“IG” 12.358.4, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ **χρύσειος**, η, ον: {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus use both χρύσεος and -ειος, χρυσῇ is uncertain in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.425}}; Lyric poetry used χρύσεος, α, ον, and this form sometimes occurs in Trag. dialogue and even in Prose, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 5.5 codices, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 37, 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 2.5.10, 2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 36.1}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **χρύσιος** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.8, +others}} (but χρύσεα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 29.37)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **χρούσιος** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 337.8 (4th c.BC)}}: (χρυσός):—**golden,** frequently in _Epic dialect_, especially of what belonged to gods, χρυσέῳ ἐν δαπέδῳ, χρυσέοις δεπάεσσι, χρύσειον ἐπὶ θρόνον, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.2, 3, 8.442, +others}}; χ. τάλαντα the **golden** scales of Zeus, {{Refs|22.209; χ. ἰτύς, ζυγόν, of Hera's chariot, 5.724, 730; ἱμάσθλη χ., of Zeus and Poseidon, 8.44 = 13.26}}; ζώνη χ., of Calypso and Circe, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.232 = 10.545, etc.}}; δόμος {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica}} [prev. cited]; of possessions of mortals, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.133, 5.425, +others}}: sometimes **enriched** or **adorned with gold,** χ. σκῆπτρον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.15, compare 246; μάχαιρα 18.598; θύραι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.88}}; κλῖναι, κρητῆρες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.82, 80}}; ἕστηκε.. Ἀλέξανδρος ὁ χ., of a statue, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 8.121; χρυσοῦς στάθητι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pseudol.” 15.}}
**2** χρύσεια μέταλλα **gold**-mines, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.105 (the only instance of χρύσειος in _Attic dialect_, compare χρυσεῖον).}}
**3** χρυσοῦς (i.e. στατήρ), ὁ, **a gold coin,** ={στατήρ}, {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|“IG” 7.303.98, +others (Oropus, 3rd c.BC), “IPE” 12.32 A 13 (Olbia, 3rd c.BC), LXX.Gen.24.22, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 25}} (in Papyrus, not a coin, but the equivalent of {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|20 silver drachmae, “PCair.Zen.” 28.11 (3rd c.BC), etc.)}}; χρυσοῖ ἐπίσημοι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.56.3, compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 9.53, 59, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**4** χρύσεον, τό, **gold plaque**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 1122.7 (Selinus, 5th c.BC).}}
**II** **gold-coloured, golden-yellow,** ἔθειραι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.42, 13.24; χ. νέφη [prev. work] 523, etc.}}; ἀέρος κόμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Pae.” 6.137}}; τῶν τὰ χρυσᾶ the **yolks** of eggs, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 9.376d.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, **golden,** χρυσέη Ἀφροδίτη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.64, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.337; Μοῖσα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).5; σθένος ἀελίου χ. [prev. author] “P.” 4.144; ὦ χ. θύγατερ Διός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 188 (Lyric poetry); Ἐλπίς [prev. work] 158 (Lyric poetry)}}; τιμή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ant.” 699; ὦ χρυσοῖ θεοί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 483; χ. ὑγίεια 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.73; λογισμοῦ ἀγωγή 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 645a; ἦθος 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 212.5; τὸ χ. ὀρνίθων γένος [prev. author] 175}}; χ. γένος ἀνθρώπων, of the {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Golden” Age, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 109, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 468e, “Phdr.” 235e, “Cra.” 397e.}}
**III.b** sometimes used ironically, ἐγὼ δὲ ὁ χ. but I, **fine fellow that I am**.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Laps.” 1}}; Πλάτωνα χρυσοῦν (i.e. Ἐπίκουρος ἐκάλει) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 10.8, compare 3rd c.BC(?): Menodotus Samius Historicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “Subf.Emp.” 63.}}
**III.2** **wealthy,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Palaephatus Paradoxographus 31.4 (assuming variant). [χρῡσέη, χρῡσέην, χρῡσέου, χρῡσέῳ, etc., in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} must be pronounced as _disyllable_, as is fully proved by such passages as {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.15, 374: but Lyric Poets sometimes used ῠ in χρύσεος, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.73, 4.4, 144, +others, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 9.6, 15.2}}; so also Trag., but only in Lyric poetry, not in Iambics or Anapaestics, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 157, 188, “Ant.” 103, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 632, 978, “IA” 1051, “IT” 1253, “Tr.” 856, “Ba.” 372, “Heracl.” 916, “HF” 351, 396, “El.” 192; sometimes also Epigrammatists, “Anthologia Graeca” 6.292.2 (3rd c.BC: Hedylus Epigrammaticus), 7.233.1 (1st c.AD: Apollonides Epigrammaticus), 13.18.4 (from Parmeno), “APl.” 4.96.8.]}}

5553:
** χρῡσίον**, τό,
_diminutive_ of χρυσός, **a piece of gold,** generally, **gold,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.95, 97, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 288e, “R.” 336e, +others}}
**2** **anything made of gold, gold plate, ornaments of gold,** etc., ἄσημον {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13: _plural_, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.10, 48.55, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 167, NT.1Pet.3.3, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 15.}}
**3** especially **gold coin, money,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 161; οὔτ᾽ ἀργύριον οὔτε χρυσίον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 472, compare “Pl.” 808, “Ra.” 720 (troch.), etc.; χ. Ἀττικόν “IG” 22.1687.16; λῆρος πάντα πρὸς τὸ χ. 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 232.1; ἐγὼ δ᾽ ὑπέλαβον χρησίμους εἶναι θεοὺς τἀργύριον καὶ τὸ χ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 537.4: but στατῆρας χρυσίου 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 112; χρυσία}}**pieces of gold,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 336e.}}
**4** **gold thread,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 32.}}
**II** as a term of endearment, **my little treasure!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 930, compare “Ap” 11.232 (Callias argument).}}
**II.2** ={τὸ τῶν παιδίων αἰδοῖον}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

5554:
** χρῡσο-δακτύλιος** [ῠ], ον,
**with ring of gold,** ἀνήρ {{NT|NT.Jam.2.2.}}
**2** gloss on{χρυσοκόλλητος}, χ. σφραγίς **set in a gold ring,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

5557:
** χρῡσός**, ὁ,
**gold,** τιμῆς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.475, etc.; coupled with other precious things, e.g. χαλκός, σίδηρος, 6.48}}; ἐσθής, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.38}}; χρυσὸν κέρασιν περιχεύας (of a victim) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.294 = 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.384, compare 437; ὡς δ᾽ ὅτε τις χρυσὸν περιχεύεται ἀργύρῳ 6.232; χ. δαμασίφρων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.78}}; κοῖλος ἄργυρος καὶ χ. silver and **gold** plate, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 283a, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nav.” 20}}; λευκὸς χ. white **gold,** i.e. gold alloyed with silver, opposed to χ. ἄπεφθος refined **gold,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.50; χ. ἑψόμενος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.82; χρυσὸν καθαίρειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 303d; βασανίζειν ἐν πυρί [prev. author] “R.” 413e.}}
**2** **gold,** to express anything made of gold, e. g. **golden** armour or raiment, χρυσὸν.. ἔδυνε περὶ χροΐ, of Zeus, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.43}}; of Poseidon, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|13.25; τὸ ἐμὸν σῶμα μήτε ἐν χ. θῆτε.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.25}}; ἀραρότως σύνδεσμα χρυσὸς (a gold crown) εἶχε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1193; ἐν χρυσῷ πίνειν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc. Cond.” 26.}}
**3** frequently used by Poets to denote **anything dear** or **precious,** ταῦτα μὲν.. κρείσσονα χρυσοῦ.. φωνεῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 372 (_anapaest meter_); ὁ χ. ἧσσον κτῆμα τοῦ κλάειν ἂν ἦν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 557; ὡς χρυσὸς αὐτῷ τἀμὰ.. κακὰ δόξει ποτ᾽ εἶναι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 432, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 9.4; compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.1, 3.42, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sertorius” 5: _metaphorically_ also, χρυσὸς ἐπῶν}} **golden** words, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 268; χρυσῷ πάττειν τινά [prev. author] “Nu.” 912 (_anapaest meter_); ὗσαι χρυσόν τινι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.50. [ῡ] in χρυσός}} and all derivations, though Lyric Poets sometimes made υ short in the _adjective_ χρύσεος (which see); once we have χρῠσός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.78}}.] (Borrowed from Semitic, cf. Hebrew **chārūts,** Assyr. _h_urāšu_ 'gold', Aramaic _hara_ 'yellow'.)

5559:
** χρώς**, ὁ,
_genitive_ χρωτός, _dative_ χρωτί, _accusative_ χρῶτα (_Attic dialect_ χρώ only in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 1.248H.): _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialectgenitive_ χροός, _dative_ χροΐ, _accusative_ χρόα (also in Lesbian, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 10.6, +others)}}, as always in Hom.and {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus, except _genitive_ χρωτός in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.575}}, _accusative_ χρῶτα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.172, 179, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 556; 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus uses χρωτός, 76.3 (but χροΐ 100.17); 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus uses χρωτί, χρῶτα, “P.” 1.55, “I.” 4(3).23(41)}}; these forms are frequently in Trag., but _Ionic dialect_ _dative_ χροΐ occurs in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 605, and χροός, χροΐ, χρόα are frequently in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus, “Hec.” 548, “Med.” 1175, “Ph.” 264, +others:— _dative_ χρῷ occurs in the phrase ἐν χρῷ see below 1.2 and in 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.10.}} —rare in Comedy texts and _Attic dialect_ Prose.
**I** of the human body, **skin** or **flesh,** οὔ σφι λίθος χρὼς οὐδὲ σίδηρος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.510; καὶ γάρ θην τούτῳ τρωτὸς χρώς 21.568; χρῶτ᾽ ἀπονιψαμένη 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.172; ἀκρότατον δ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ὀϊστὸς ἐπέγραψε χρόα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.139; ταμέειν χρόα νηλέϊ χαλκῷ 13.501}}; λιλαιοένη χροὸς ἆσαι, of a spear,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|21.168; κακὰ χροΐ εἵματ᾽ ἔχοντα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.506; χρῷ πῦρ ὐπαδεδρόμακεν 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.10; μύροις χρῶτα λιπαίνων 4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 18.1 (_anapaest meter_)}}: especially **flesh,** opposed to bone, φθινύθει δ᾽ ἀμφ᾽ ὀστεόφι χρώς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.145; οὐδέ τί οἱ χρὼς σήπεται 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.414, compare 19.33}} (which usage is said by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus to have been Ionic, 18(2)}}.435, with reference to {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 9); τὸ δέρμα τοῦ χρωτός LXX.Lev.13.11, etc.; τοῦ χρωτὸς ἥδιστον ἀπέπνει 4th c.BC: Aristoxenus Musicus “Fragmenta Historica” 84}}: generally, **one's body, frame,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.55, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 192.6 (_anapaest meter_); χριμφθῆναι χροΐ [prev. author] “Supp.” 790 (Lyric poetry); στεῖλαί νυν ἀμφὶ χρωτὶ.. πέπλους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 821, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 605: _plural_, διὰ τί.. οἱ χρῶτες ὄζουσ; 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 877b21; also κατεδήσαντο.. τοὺς ὑγιεῖς χρῶτας, ὡς τραυματίαι 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 9.50.}}
**I.2** ἐν χροΐ, or ἐν χρῷ, **close to the skin,** ἐν χροΐ κείρειν to shave **close,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.175; ἐν χρῷ κεκαρμένοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.8; ἐν χρῷ κουριῶντας 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 30}}:—_metaphorically_, **to the quick,** ξυρεῖ γὰρ ἐν χρῷ τοῦτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 786}}; ἐν χρῷ παραπλεῖν sail past **so as to shave** or **graze,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.84}}; τὴν μάχην συνάψαι ἐν χρῷ to fight **at close quarters,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 27}}; ἡ ἐν χρῷ συνουσία **close** acquaintance, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ind.” 3: with _genitive_, ἐν χρῷ τινος}} **close to, hard by** a person or thing, τοῦ θώρακος (variant{σώματος}) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.345a}}; τῆς γῆς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 925b, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 5: _absolutely_, ἐν χρῷ}}**near at hand,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|[prev. author] “Hist.Conscr.” 24, +others; compare “Etymologicum Magnum” 313.53, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II** **the colour of the skin, complexion,** μελαίνετο δὲ χρόα καλόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.354}}; τρέπεται χ. his **colour** changes, i. e. he turns pale, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|13.279, compare 17.733; ὠχρήσαντα χρόα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.529; χρόα.. ἀμείβειν 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.41; μεθίστη χρωτὸς.. φύσιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 174; μεταλλακτῆρα πουλύπουν χροός Ion Trag. 36}}; τί χρὼς τέτραπτα; (_paratragoedia_) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 127; φεῦγε δ᾽ ἀπὸ χρώς Theocr. 23.13}}; rare in _Attic dialect_ Prose, ἐπὶ τῷ χρωτὶ μέγα φρονεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.54, compare “Oec.” 10.5}}: in _Ionic dialect_ Prose, of the **colour** of a finger, χροΐ δῆλα [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Pherecydes Syrius Philosophus cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.118 (variant{χρωΐ},compare “Vorsokr.” 5i.44).}}]
**II.2** generally, **colour,** ἀμείβων χρῶτα πορφυρᾷ βαφῇ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 317; τὸν χρῶτα [μεταβάλλει] ὁ χαμαιλέων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 832b14; χρὼς αἵματος 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 660.}}

5560:
** χωλός**, ή, όν,
**lame** in the feet, **halting, limping,** with _accusative_, χωλὸς δ᾽ ἕτερον πόδα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.217, compare 9.503, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.308, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92.β, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 486, 1032; χ. καὶ οὐκ ἀρτίπους 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.161; χ. τὼ σκέλει 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 24}}; also with _dative_, **σκέλει** χωλός {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.739b; χωλὸς ἀμφοτέροις 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 20}}: later also of the hand, like{κυλλός}, χωλὸς τὴν χεῖρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 343; χεῖρα χωλὴν ἕξειν 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.1, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 794e: of animals, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.5, etc.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **defective,** φύσις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 71e}}; **one-sided,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 535d}}; βασιλεία Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.3.3.}}
**II.2** of metre, especially of the χωλίαμβος (which see), **halting,** μέτρον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus 5.4, 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 301}}; also of a trochaic metre, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Aristides Quintilianus Musicus 1.25.}}
**II.3** ἀείδειν χωλά, of a '**lame** tale', {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.71.}}

5561:
** χώρα**,
_Ionic dialect_ **χώρη**, ἡ, ={χῶρος}, **space** or **room in which a thing is,** defined as **partly occupied space,** distinguished from κενόν and τόπος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.26 (compare2.163), 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 3.124; ποταγορεύοντι τὰν ὕλαν τόπον καὶ χώραν 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 94b (in ὁ τόπος τῆς χ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 705c χώρα}} = **country**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|(compare 11.1); so χώρας ἐν τόποις Λιβυστικοῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 292); οὐδέ τι πολλὴ χώρη μεσσηγύς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.521; νόμισμα.. χώρας μεγάλης δέοιτ᾽ ἄν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 7.5}}; χώραν τινὶ καταλιπεῖν leave **room** for it, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.123f, etc.}}
**2** generally, **place, spot,** στρέψεσθ᾽ ἐκ χώρης ὅθι.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.516, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.352; ὀλίγῃ ἐνὶ χ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.394}}; χώραν ἐκ χώρας μεταβάλλειν move from **place** to **place,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 181c}}; **field** in a ceiling, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).103.193, 106ii139 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; ἡ πρώτη χ. the first **field** (on the chest of Cypselus), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.17.6}}; **socket** or **cavity** of a joint, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 79, 80}}; of the eye, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).121.76 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; as euphemism for the **genital organs,** {{Refs|Hippiatr. 33, 71.}}
**3** **the position, proper place** of a person or thing, ἐνὶ χώρῃ ἕζεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.349}}: especially a soldier's **post,** Ἄρης οὐκ ἔνι χώρα is not at his **post** (or perhaps in the **land,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 524) 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 78 (_anapaest meter_)}}; χώραν λιπεῖν, προλείπειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.126, 2.87}}; μισθοφορεῖν κεναῖς χ. draw pay for unfilled **vacancies,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.146; ἐπιγράψαι αὐτῷ τὴν χ. “UPZ” 14.88 (2nd c.BC): later τὴν χ. τινὸς ἀποπληρῶσαι, ποιῆσαι}} fill a person's **place,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|POxy. 136.15(6th c.AD), “PMasp.” 32.11 (6th c.AD): χώραν λαβεῖν}} take a **position,** find **one's place,** ἕως ἂν χώραν λάβῃ [τὰ πράγματα] till they are brought into **position,** into **order,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.37; οὐ διδοὺς ἑτέρῳ τόπον οὐδὲ χώραν διακονίας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.62d}}; οὐκ ἂν ἔχοι χώραν νοήσεως ἡντινοῦν τὸ ἀγαθόν the Good cannot have any **possibility** of thinking, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.6.6}}; σοὶ ἀστρονομεῖν χ. your **province** is astronomy, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 5.15; ἐν τοῖς ἀτέχνοις χώραν ἔχει τὸ αὐτόματον 4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.225D.: frequently in the phrase ὥρα καὶ χ.}} time and **place,** ἐν ὁποία ἀξία φυτευθῆναι καὶ ὥρὰ καὶ χώρᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hipparchus” 225c; ἐν ἄλλῃ καὶ χώρῃ 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ χυμῶν” 14}}; πρὸς ὥρας καὶ χώρας καὶ διαίτας{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 16, “Aph.” 3.3; ἥ τε τοῦ ἔτους ὥρα καὶ χ. καὶ φύσις τοῦ θεραπευομένου σώματος 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).399, compare 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor Trall. 1.10, 7th c.AD: Stephanus Medicus “in Hp.” 1.161, 180 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}} b. in metric, **position** of a foot in a verse, τὸ δακτυλικὸν δέχεται δακτύλους καὶ σπονδείους κατὰ πᾶσαν χ. {{Refs 7th c.AD+|7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus 7.1, compare 8.1; αἱ περιτταὶ χ. [prev. author] 5.1, 6.1.}}
**4** _metaphorically_, **station, place, position,** ἐν χώρᾳ τινὸς εἶναι to be in his **position,** be counted the same as he is, ἐν ἀνδραπόδων or μισθοφόρου χώρᾳ εἶναι to be in the **position** of slaves or mercenaries, to pass or rank as such, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.13, “Cyr.” 2.1.18}}; ἐν οὐδεμιᾷ χ. εἶναι to have no **place** or **rank,** be in no **esteem,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev. author] “An.” 5.7.28; οὗ μέλλει χώρην μηδεμίαν θέμεναι 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 152; τούτων τοι χώρη.. ὀλίγη τελέθει [prev. author] 822; τὰς μεγίστας χ. ἔχειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.43.1.}}
**5** in senses {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3 and 4 frequently with a _preposition_, ἐκ χώρας ὁρμᾶν, opposed to πορευόμενος μάχεσθαι, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.33}}; εἰς τὰς ἑαυτῶν χ. πάρεισι are at their **posts,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.2.4, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.57; εἰς τὰς τῶν λοχαγῶν χ. καταστήσεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.23}}; ἐν χώρᾳ in **one's place,** at **one's post,** ἐν ταῖς χ. γενέσθαι {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 4.8.15}}; ἐν χώρᾳ πίπτειν, ἀποθνῄσκειν, die at **one's post,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 4.2.20, 8.39}}; ἐπὶ χώρας ἕσσαι set it in **its place,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.273}}; also μένειν ἐπὶ χώρας, ={μένειν κατὰ χώραν}, remain in **force**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 90.16 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC), “BGU” 183.9 (1st c.AD)}}; κατὰ χώρην εἶναι be in **one's place,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.135; [φόροι] κατὰ χώρην διατελέουσι ἔχοντες [prev. author] 6.42, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 367, “Ra.” 793; κατὰ χ. μένειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.95, 8.108, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1354, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.26}}; ἤλπιζον.. οὐ μενεῖν κατὰ χ. τὰ πράγματα{{Refs|[prev. work] 76; μένει τὸ ὅρκιον κατὰ χ.}}**as it was, undisturbed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.201}}; ἐᾶν κατὰ χ. τὴν πόλιν leave in **its place,** leave **as it was,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.6, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.17; κατὰ χώραν μένειν τοὺς ἄλλους [νόμους] ἐᾶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.5}}; κατὰ χ. ἀπιέναι retire in their old **order,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.11.}}
**II** **land,** _i.e. _,
**II.1** **a land, country,** ἅς τινας ἵκεο χώρας ἀνθρώπων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.573; ἡ χ. ἡ Ἀττική 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.13; ἐμπορεύεσθαι εἰς τὴν χ. “IG” 12.57.21, compare 63.22, +others: frequently in Trag., Ἑλλάδα χώραν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 271 (Lyric poetry); Εὐβοῖδα χ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 74, etc.}}; **territory,** ὁ τύραννος ἢ πόλεων ἢ χ. πολλῆς [ἐπιθυμεῖ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 4.7: _plural_, “OGI” 54.11 (Adule, 3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**II.2** **landed estate,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.4.28, 8.6.4}}. b. **country town,** τοὺς κήρυκας διαπέμψαντες ἐς τὰς χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 688 B 8 (Chios, 5th c.BC).}}
**II.3** **the country,** opposed to to the town, ἡ πόλις καὶ ἡ χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 1; τὰ ἐκ τῆς χώρας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.5, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.6.11}}; ὁ ἐκ τῆς χ. γιγνόμενος σῖτος{{Refs|[prev. work] 13; οἱ ἐν τῇ χ. ἐργάται [prev. author] “Hier.” 10.5}}; ἐν τῇ χώρᾳ κοιταῖον γίγνεσθαι, opposed to ἐν ἄστει, Decrees cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.37}}; ἁ κοινὰ χ. (of two cities) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).77.2 (Epid., 2nd c.BC)}}: especially of Egypt as opposed to Alexandria, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 56.5 (Canopus, 3rd c.BC), “PHib.” 1.27.167 (3rd c.BC), etc. (but in “PTeb.” 5.98 (2nd c.BC) ἐν τῇ Ἀλεξα (νδρέων) χ. means 'in Alexandria')}}; ἡ ἄνω χ. καὶ ἡ κάτω, Upper and Lower Egypt, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 90.46 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC), cf. Wilcken “Chr.” 109.9 (3rd c.BC).—χῶρος is another form: in signification 11 χώρα}} alone is used in _Attic dialect_; whereas in _signification_ 1 χῶρος is common, except in the special sense of **one's proper place** or **post** (χῶρος and χώρα perhaps _cognate_ with χῆρος, χῆτος).

5562:
** χωρ-έω**,
{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 108, etc.}}; _future_ χωρήσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.629, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.89, 8.68.β, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος παιδίου” 18}}, and in later Prose, as {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.9, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 20.15, etc.}}; _Attic dialect_ only in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.82 (except in compounds, ἀνα-χωρήσω [prev. author] 7.72, ἀπο- 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 6.2, προ- 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.4, προσ- [prev. author] 2.2, συγ- [prev. author] 1.140, etc.)}}; elsewhere in Trag. and _Attic dialect_ always in _middle_ form, χωρήσομαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 476, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 404, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.20, etc., and frequently in compounds:}}_aorist_ ἐχώρησα, _Epic dialect_ χώρησα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.655, “hymnus ad Cererem” 430, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.120, etc.}}: _perfect_ κεχώρηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.120, 122, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.122, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 19, etc.:}} —_passive_, _future_ χωρηθήσομαι (συγ-) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.17.5:}}_aorist_ ἐχωρήθην (συν-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.31, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 38.4:}}_perfect_ κεχώρηται (παρα-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 11.52, (συγ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 15a}}: (χῶρος):—**make room for another, give way, withdraw,** ἐχώρησαν πάλιν αὖτις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.533}}; γαῖα ἔνερθε χώρησεν the earth **gave way** from beneath, i. e. opened, {{Refs|“hymnus ad Cererem” [same place]}}; χ. πρύμναν, ={κρούεσθαι πρύμναν}, **put back, retire,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1120; χωρεῖτε}}**begone!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 196, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1678, “Med.” 820, etc.}}—The uncom pounded word does not occur in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”}} and only _future_ and _aorist_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”}} —Construction:
**1** with _genitive_ _of things_ vel loci, χώρησεν τυτθὺν ἐπάλξιος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.406; νεῶν ἐχώρησαν 15.655; νεκροῦ χωρήσουσι 16.629; also νηῶν ἄπο.. ἐχώρησαν προτὶ Ἴλιον 13.724; ἀπὸ κρατερῆς ὑσμίνης χωρήσαντες 18.244; ἔξω τῶνδε δωμάτων χωρεῖτε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 180; ἐκ πυλῶν [prev. author] “Th.” 476; ἐκ προαστίου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1432.}}
**2** with _dative_ _person_, **give way to** one, **make way for** him, **retire before** him, οὐδ᾽ ἂν Ἀχιλλῆϊ χωρήσειεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.324, compare 17.101.}}
**II** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **go forward, advance,** τὸ πῦρ.. πρόσω κεχώρηκεν {{Refs|Callias Epigrammaticus in “PSI” 11.1216.34}}; simply, **go** or **come,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.10, etc.}}; **go on one's journey, travel,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 750; χ. ἐπ᾽ ἀδελφεοῦ βίαν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.73, etc.; ἐς ναῦν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 379}}; χ. πρὸς ἔργον **come** to action, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 116, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 884}}; χ. πρὸς ἧπαρ **go** to one's heart, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 938}}; χωρῶν ἀπείλει νῦν **go and** threaten, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OC” 1038; διὰ φόνου χ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 176}}; τὰ τοξεύματα ἐχώρει διὰ τῶν ἀσπίδων, of weapons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.2.28}}; τὸ ὕδωρ κατὰ τὰς τάφρους ἐχώρει it **went off** by.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 7.5.16; ἄνω ποταμῶν χωροῦσι παγαί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 410 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.10}}; χώρει κάτω **go** downwards, i.e. beginning from the upper parts of the body, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 74; διὰ στόμα χωροῦντα.. ἀφρόν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1174}}; χ. κύκλῳ [ὁ ποταυός] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 113b}}; ὁμόσε χ. τισί to join battle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.101, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 451, compare ὁμόσε 1.2; χ. ὁμόσε τοῖς λόγοις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 921; χ. δειπνήσων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 272; πρὸς τὸ ἱερὸν χωρῆσαι δρόμῳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.134; χωροῖς ἂν εἴσω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1491, “Ph.” 674; χώρει, ξέν᾽, ἔξω [prev. author] “OC” 824: of Time, νὺξ ἐχώρει}} the night **was passing, near an end,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 384; βιοστερὴς χ.}}**wander about,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 747: in Medicine texts, of excretions, τὰ χωρέοντα μὴ τῷ πλήθει τεκμαίρεσθαι, ἀλλ᾽ ὡς ἂν χωρέῃ οἷα δεῖ 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 1.23}}; also of the menses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mul.” 1.2: with _accusative_ loci, Κεκροπίαν χθόνα χ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1572.}}
**II.b** **to be in motion** or **flux,** πάντα χωρεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus cited in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 402a, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 1.5.}}
**II.2** **go forward, make progress,** τοὔργον οὐ χωρεῖ πρόσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Dict.” in “PSI” 11.1209.16; πῶς οὖν οὐ χωρεῖ τοὔργο; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 472 (Lyric poetry)}}; χωρεῖ.. τὸ πρᾶγμα{{Refs|[prev. work] 509; τόκοι χωροῦσιν [prev. author] “Nu.” 18; χωρεῖ τὸ κακόν [prev. author] “V.” 1483, “Nu.” 907 (both _anapaest meter_).}}
**II.3** **come to an issue, turn out** in a certain manner, παρὰ σμικρὰ.. κεχώρηκε **have come** to little, of the event of oracles, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.120; εὐτυχέως χ. [prev. author] 3.39}}; κακῶς χ. **turn out** ill, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 684e; δόξα δ᾽ ἐχώρει δίχα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 117 (_anapaest meter_), compare “Hel.” 759}}: frequently _absolutely_, **advance, succeed,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, 5.89; πάντα διὰ πράξεων καὶ.. ἀγώνων κεχωρηκότα.. Ῥωμαίοις 1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus “Praef.” 8; τὰ πράγματα χωρεῖ κατὰ λόγον 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 28.17.12; ὁ λόγος ὁ ἐμὸς οὐ χωρεῖ ἐν ὑμῖν NT.John.8.37 (unless in signification 111. below)}}; also, **to be possible,** οὐ γάρ οἱ χωρεῖ περιβαλεῖν κτλ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 1.3 (sed to be read ἐγχωρεῖ).}}
**II.4** **to be spread abroad,** ἡ φάτις κεχώρηκε a report **spread,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.122}}; διὰ πάντων οὕτως ἐχώρει τίς ἕψετα;” {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.62; κλαυθμὸς διὰ πάντων ἐχώρει 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 19}}; ὄνομα δόξῃ διὰ πάντων ἀνθρώπων κεχωρηκός a name **spread abroad,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 1.}}
**II.5** of money, **to be spent,** τὰς μὲν δαπάνας χωρεῖν ἐντελεῖς ἐκ τῶν οἴκων, τὰ δὲ ἔργα μὴ τελείσθαι λυσιτελούντως πρὸς τὴν δαπάνην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 20.21; τὰ προσγεινόμενα χωρήσει εἰς ἃ ἂν ὁ δῆμος θελήσῃ “IG” 5(1).18 B 6 (from Sparta), compare 1432.4 (Messene, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD).}}
**III** _transitive_, **have room for** a thing, **hold, contain,** frequently of measures, κρητὴρ χωρέων ἀμφορέας ἑξακοσίους {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.51, compare 192, 4.61, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1238, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 214a; οὐκ ἐχώρησεν αὐτοὺς ἡ πόλις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.17, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.200, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.164, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 941; οὐκ ἐχώρει αὐτοὺς ἡ γῆ κατοικεῖν ἅμα LXX.Gen.13.6; ποτήρια.. οὐχὶ χωροῦντ᾽ οὐδὲ κόγχην 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 143.3 (troch.); κοτύλας χ. δέκα 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Kol.fragment” 2, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 96, etc.}}; χωρήσατε ἡμᾶς **take** us **into your hearis!** {{NT|NT.2Cor.7.2; οὐ πάντες χωροῦσι τὸν λόγον τοῦτον}}**find room for..,** {{NT|NT.Matt.19.11 (so perhaps _intransitive_, NT.John.8.37, see above 11.3)}}; **to be capable of,** τὸ Κάτωνος φρόνημα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 64}}: with _infinitive_, **to be capable** of doing, οὐ χωρεῖ μεγάλην διδαχὴν ἀδίδακτος ἀκούειν (variant for{ἀκουή}) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 89; δωρεὰν ὅσην οὐκ ἐχωρήσατε αἰτεῖσθαι “IG” 7.2713.11 (Acraeph., Oratio Neronis).}}
**III.2** _impersonal_, ὅταν μηκέτι χωρῇ αὐταῖς ἐργαζομέναις [ταῖς μελίτταις] when **there is** no more **room** for them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 626b11.}}

5563:
** χωρ-ίζω**,
_perfect_ _passive_ κεχώρισμαι, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _Ionic dialect_ κεχωρίδαται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.140, 151, +others}}: (χωρίς).
**I** in local sense, **separate, divide,** χ. ἀλλήλων λόχους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 108}}; **exclude,** τὴν πτέρνην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 11, etc.: τί τινος, frequently in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, χ. τὴν ψυχὴν τοῦ σώματος “R.” 609d, compare “Phlb.” 55e; ἀπὸ τοῦ σώματος τὴν ψ. “Phd.” 67c, compare “Plt.” 268c, etc.; πάντα κατὰ φυλάς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 9.8}}; with _infinitive_ added, [τὴν τάξιν] ἐπὶ τῷ μέσῳ ἐχώρισεν ἕπεσθαι {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 6.5.11}}; οἱ χωρίζοντες **the Separators,** a name given to those Grammarians (Xenon and Hellanicus _accusative_ to {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Chrestomathia - Homeri Opera” p.102 Allen)}} who ascribed the Iliad and Odyssey to different authors, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia A 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.356, 649, 11.692, 21.416}}:—_passive_, **to be separated, severed,** or **divided,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.151, 3.12, +others}}; τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1002, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 31b; σοφόν.. πάντων κεχωρισμένον 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 108.}}
**II** **separate in thought, distinguish,** ἡδύ τε καὶ δίκαιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 663a; ἀπὸ τῶν ὠφελίμων τὰ καθ᾽ αὑτά 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1096b14; χ. καὶ διασπᾶν [prev. author] “PA” 642b18; especially in Logic, τὸν ἴδιον τῆς οὐσίας ἑκάστου λόγον ταῖς.. οἰκείαις διαφοραῖς χ. [prev. author] “Top.” 108b6, compare 132a13}}:— _passive_, **to be different,** κεχωρίδαται πολλὸν τῶν.. ἄλλων ἀνθρώπων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.140: less frequently with _dative_, κεχώρισται οὗτος ὁ χειμών.. τοῖσι ἐν ἄλλοισι χωρίοισι γινομένοισι χειμῶσι [prev. author] 4.28; ἀπ᾽ ἀλλήλων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.49}}; νόμοι κεχωρισμένοι τῶν ἄλλων ἀνθρώπων laws **apart from others, far different,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.172, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.23.11; γνῶμαι κεχωρισμέναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.11}}; opposed to συγκεχυμένος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 524c; κεχώρισται πλεῖστον τό τ᾽ εἶναι καὶ τὸ τοῦτον φάσκειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.26.}}
**III** _passive_, κεχωρισμένη ἀπὸ τοῦ ἀνδρός **divorced,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.26.6}}; θᾶσσον.. οἰστοῦ.. χωρίζεται, of a wife, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1063.13.}}
**IV** later in _passive_, **depart, go away,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.94.9, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.65, Heraclitus Paradoxographus “de incredibilibus - Mythographi Graeci” 8; ἐκ θρόνων 2nd c.BC: Ezekiel Poeta Judaeus “Exagoge - Mnemosyne” 76.}}

5564:
** χωρ-ίον**, τό,
_diminutive_ (only in form, compare χ. μέγιστον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.19) of χῶρος and χώρα:}}
**1** **place, spot, district,** very frequently in Prose from {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus down wards, e.g. 2.8, 10, 29, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.54}}; also in Comedy texts, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 209, etc.}}; never in Trag.: ἐκ τοῦ αὐτοῦ χ. from the same **spot,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.11}}; χ. ἔρημον, χ. χαλεπὰ καὶ πετρώδη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.9; χ. ἱππάσιμα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.14}}: _plural_, **sites,** οἰκίσαι χωρία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.12.}}
**2** **town,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 100}}, etc.; χωρίων κατάληψις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 455b, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 28.7, etc.}}
**3** **landed property, estate,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.106, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 844b, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.4, “IG” 12.325.10}}; used with ἀγρός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.1, etc.}}
**4** **place of business, office,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.33.}}
**5** **space, room,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.63, etc.}}; especially in Geometry texts, **space enclosed by lines, area, figure,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 82bf, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 152, 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra “Data” 55, 3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 240.17}}: especially **rectangle,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ κωνοειδέων καὶ σφαιροειδέων” 2, +others}}
**6** **passage in a book,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.117 (unless interpolated), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 12, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 15.672a, 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus}}**inCael.**{{Refs|126.4.}}
**6.b** **subject,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.97}}: _plural_, **topics,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 31.}}
**7** in Medicine texts, **part** of the body, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 2, compare “Aph.” 1.21 (_plural_)}}; τὸ χ. τὸ ἐπὶ τοῦ ἥπατος _periphrastic_ for the gall-bladder, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Morb.” 4.36.}}

5565:
** χωρίς**,
_adverb_, also **χωρί**, which see: (see. χῆρος):— **separately, apart,** once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”, 7.470; χ. μὲν πρόγονοι, χ. δὲ μέτασσαι, χ. δ᾽ αὖθ᾽ ἕρσαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.221, compare 4.130, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 20a. 16, “IG” 12.108.32, +others; χ. ἡ τιμὴ θεῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 637; κεῖται χ. ὁ νεκρός 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.62; χ. περὶ αὐτῶν ἑκάστου οἱ νόμοι κεῖνται 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.10}}; χίλια τάλαντα.. χ. θέσθαι **set** them **apart, in reserve,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.24}}; χ. οἰκεῖν live **apart,** have an independent establishment, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.36, 47.72}}; χ. γενόμενοι being **separated,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.1.18; χ. ἀπ᾽ ἀλλήλων 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.56, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 98c}}; μή με χ. αἰτιῶ **without evidence,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 608}}; χ. ποιῆσαι **distinguish,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.68}}; χ. βλέπειν look **two ways, squint,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 27.6}}; opposed to κοινῇ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.160}}; opposed to κοινόν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 860}}; χ. δέ.. and **separately,** and **besides,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13}}; **separately,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.16, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aristides” 20}}; λέγειν χ. περὶ ἑκάστου Legal cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.35; χ. καὶ ἐν μέρει [prev. author] 3.2}}; περὶ τὸ ἓν καὶ χ. about the one and **without** [the one] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 203a14}}; **otherwise,** χ. δὲ μηδαμῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 950c}}; χ. ἢ ὁκόσοι **except** so many as.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.77; χωρὶς ἢ ὅσα 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 53.21; χωρὶς ἤ ὅτι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.94, 4.61, 82}}; also χ. εἰ μή (condemned by Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus post Moer. p.462P.), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.698f, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 91.8, +others; χ. πλήν 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.34.4.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **of different nature, kind,** or **quality,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.1; χ. τό τ᾽ εἶναι καὶ τὸ μὴ νομίζεται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 528; χ. τό τ᾽ εἰπεῖν πολλὰ καὶ τὰ καίρια 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 808; χ. ᾤμην εἶναι τὸ συνεῖναί τε ἀλλήλοις διαλεγομένους καὶ τὸ δημηγορεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 336b.}}
**II** as _preposition_ with _genitive_, **without,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 926, etc.}}; **without the help** or **will of,** χ. Ζηνός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1002 (Lyric poetry)}}: after its case, πόνου χ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 915, cf. Theon. Sm. p.1H.}}
**II.2** **separate from, apart from,** χ. ἀθανάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.41; χ. ἀνθρώπων στίβου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 487; χωρὶς ᾤκισται θεῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 2; χ. ὀμμάτων ἐμῶν [prev. author] “Or.” 272; ἡ ψυχὴ χ. τοῦ σώματος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 67a, etc.}}
**II.3** **independently of, without reckoning,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.93, 106, 6.58; χ. τε γένους οὐκ ἔστιν ὅτῳ μείζονα μοῖραν νείμαιμ᾽ ἢ σοί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 293 (_anapaest meter_)}}; χ. δὲ τῆς δόξης οὐδὲ δίκαιόν μοι δοκεῖ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 35b.}}
**II.4** **differently from, otherwise than,** χ. μυρηρῶν τευχέων πνέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 180.5; χ. δήπου σοφία ἐστίν ἀνδρείας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 195a, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.13.}}

5567:
** ψάλλω**,
_future_ ψᾰλῶ {{LXX+NT|LXX.Judg.5.3, NT.1Cor.14.15}}: _aorist_ ἔψηλα {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 209b, etc., and in LXX ἔψᾱλα LXX.Psa.9.12, +others}}:—**pluck, pull, twitch,** ψ. ἔθειραν **pluck** the hair, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 1062: especially of the bow-string, τόξων χερὶ ψάλλουσι νευράς}} **twang** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 784; κενὸν κρότον 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 1453}}; ἐκ κέραος ψ. βέλος **send** a shaft **twanging** from the bow, {{Refs|“APl.” 4.211 (Stat. Flacc.)}}; so μιλτοχαρὴς σχοῖνος ψαλλομένη a carpenter's red line, which **is twitched** and then suddenly let go, so as to leave a mark, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.103 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus): _metaphorically_, γυναῖκας ἐξ ἀνδρῶν ψόγος ψάλλει, κενὸν τόξευμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 499.}}
**II** mostly of the strings of musical instruments, **play** a stringed instrument **with the fingers,** and not with the plectron, ψῆλαι καὶ κρούειν τῷ πλήκτρῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus}} [same place], et ibi {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Scholia; ἐάν τις ψήλας τὴν νήτην ἐπιλάβῃ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 919b15; μουσικώτατος ὢν χατὰ χεῖρα δίχα πλήκτρου ἔψαλλε 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.183d}}; opposed to κιθαρίζω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.155, “SIG” 578.18 (Teos, 2nd c.BC)}}; πρὶν μέν σ᾽ ἑπτάτονον ψάλλον (i.e. τὴν λύραν) Ion Eleg.3.3: _absolutely_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus [same place], 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 522, Hippias (?) in “PHib.” 1.13.24; κόραις 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 260; ψάλλειν [οὐκ ἔνι] ἄνευ λύρας 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Par.” 17:}} —Prov., ῥᾷον ἤ τις ἂν χορδὴν ψήλειε 'as easy as falling off a log', {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 26(14).31.}}
**II.2** later, **sing to a harp,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.7.18, 9.12, +others; τῇ καρδίᾳ NT.Eph.5.19}}; τῷ πνεύματι {{NT|NT.1Cor.[prev. cited]}}
**II.3** _passive_, of the instrument, **to be struck** or **played,** ψαλλομένη χορδή {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 919b2}}; also of persons, **to be played to on the harp,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Macho Comicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.348f.}}

5568:
** ψαλ-μός**, ὁ,
**twitching** or **twanging** with the fingers, ψαλμοὶ τόξων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 173 (Lyric poetry); τοξήρει ψαλμῷ [τοξεύσας] [prev. author] “HF” 1064 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** mostly of musical strings, πηκτίδων ψαλμοῖς κρέκον ὕμνον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Telestes Lyricus 5, cf. Diog.Trag. 1.9, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.1.}}
**II.2** **the sound of the cithara** or **harp,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 125, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Phrynichus Tragicus 11; ψαλμὸς δ᾽ ἀλαλάζει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 57.7 (_anapaest meter_)}}; there were contests in τὸ ψάλλειν, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Michel” 898.10(Chios, 2nd c.BC), 913.6(Teos, 2nd c.BC).}}
**II.3** later, **song sung to the harp, psalm,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.2Ki.23.1, +others, NT.Eph.5.19; βίβλος ψαλμῶν NT.Luke.20.42.}}

5571:
** ψευδής**, ές
(the _neuter_ _singular_ ψευδές is not found in early writers, ψεῦδος being used instead, compare ψεῦδος 111; it is found in later Gr., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 669.54 (Egypt, 1st c.AD), 4th c.AD: Palaephatus Paradoxographus 6, +others, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).782); _genitive_ _sing._ ψευδοῦς [prev. author] 15.168}}; old _Attic dialect_ _accusative_ _plural_ ψευδᾶς {{Refs|“IG” 12.700}}: (ψεύδομαι):— **lying, false, untrue,** of things, opposed to ἀληθής, ψ. λόγοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 229; μῦθοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 685, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1288 (_anapaest meter_)}}; τρέπεσθαι ἐπὶ ψευδέα ὁδόν to betake oneself to **false**hood, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.117}}; ψ. κατηγορία, αἰτίαι, **false** charges, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.183, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.138, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.41.3; λόγοι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 526; λόγος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 240e, “Cra.” 385b: ψ. λόγοι}} are also **fallacies,** in Logic, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 162b3ff}}; ἥδε ἡ ψ. οὐσία this **unreal** Being (i.e. the world of sense), {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.8.9}}: irregular _superlative_ ψευδίστατος, εἴδη {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 14.37.}}
**2** of persons, **lying, false,** and as _substantive_, **liar,** οὐ γὰρ ἐπὶ ψευδέσσι πατὴρ Ζεὺς ἔσσετ᾽ ἀρωγός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.235 (only here in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}; perhaps ψεύδεσσι from ψεῦδος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|is the true accent; so Hermappias cited in Hdn.Gr. 2.45 against 3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus and Ptol.Asc. [prev. passage]); τοὺς θεοὺς ψευδεῖς τίθης 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 992, compare “Ant.” 657; ψ. ἔφυς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1608}}; ψ. φανήσεσθαι to be detected **in falsehood,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.27, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 148b; Κριτίαν ψευδῆ ἐπιδείξω [prev. author] “Chrm.” 158d}}: irregular _superlative_ ψευδίστατος **arrant liar,** {{Refs|EM 110.29, compare 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1441.25.}}
**3** τὰ ψευδῆ **falsehoods, lies,** οὐ ψευδῆ λέγω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 625, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.10, etc.; οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ ὅπως λέξαιμι τὰ ψευδῆ καλά 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 620; τινὰς ψ. διαβάλλειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 64; ψευδῶν συγκολλητής [prev. author] “Nu.” 446 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**4** ψευδέων ἀγορή, in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 3.1}}. ή, ιβ, said to be a name of **the monkey-market,** perhaps as being **villanous counterfeits of humanity.**
**II** _passive_, **beguiled, deceived,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 852.}}
**III** _adverb_ ψευδῶς **falsely,** λέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “IT” 1309 codices; προσποιήσασθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.137}}; **mistakenly,** ψ. δοξάζειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 40d; ψ. γενέσθαι τὸν φόβον}}**groundlessly,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.110.7.}}

5579:
** ψεῦδος**, εος, τό,
_Epic dialect_ _dative_ _plural_ ψεύδεσσι, see at {ψευδής} I.2: (ψεύδω):— **falsehood, lie,** ψεύδεα.. ἐτύμοισιν ὁμοῖα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.203, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 27; ψεῦδός κεν φαῖμεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.81; ψεῦδος δ᾽ οὐκ ἐρέει 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.20}}; εἴ τε ψεῦδος ὑπόσχεσις εἴ τε καὶ οὐκί whether the promise be a **lie** or no, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.349; οὔτι ψεῦδος ἐμὰς ἄτας κατέλεξας 9.115; ψεύδεσσιν θέλγειν τινά 21.276, compare 23.576, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.387; οὐ ψεύδεϊ τέγξω λόγον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.19}}; ψ. ποικίλα, αἰόλον ψ., {{Refs|[prev. author] “O.” 1.29, “N.” 8.25}}; ψ. γλυκύ a sweet **deceit,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “P.” 2.37; ψεῦδος οὐδὲν ὧν λέγω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1220; οὐδὲν ἕρπει ψ. εἰς γῆρας χρόνου [prev. author] “fragment” 62; εἴ τι ψεῦδος εἴρηκα 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.2; ψ. ἐπιφέρειν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.41: ψ. λέγειν distinguished from ψεύδεσθαι, “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.42.}}
**2** in Logic, **false conclusion, fallacy,** συλλογισμὸς τοῦ ψεύδους {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 61b3}}; συμβαίνει ψ.{{Refs|[prev. work] 37a36:}} —in “NT” of what is opposed to religious truth, **false doctrine,** {{NT|NT.Rom.1.25}}; ποιῶν βδέλυγμα καὶ ψ., i.e. doing what is repugnant to the true faith, {{NT|NT.Rev.21.27}}; of false anatomical doctrine, τὸ οἴεσθαι.. ψ. ἐστι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.17.}}
**II** in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 12.24 ψεύδεα}} are **spots, pimples** on the nose; supposed to be characteristic of liars, cf. Scholia (at [prev. work]); compare ψυδράκιον.
**II.2** **white spots** on the fingernails, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “προβλήματα ἀνέκδοτα” 2.58.}}
**III** In {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus ψεῦδος is frequently opposed to ἀληθές, “Grg.” 505e, “R.” 382d, “Euthd.” 272b, +others}}; so almost like an _adjective_ (compare ψευδής [near the start]), ὄνομα ψεῦδος καὶ ἀληθὲς λέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Cra.” 385c; παράδοξόν τε καὶ ψεῦδος ὄνομα “Plt.” 281b: compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 462 codices}}

5582:
** ψεῦσ-μα**, ατος, τό,
**lie, untruth, fraud,** εὐτυχέστατον ψεῦσμα ἐψευσμένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 71d, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 55, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Artoxerxes” 13.}}
**II** τοὺς ἐπὶ τῆς ῥινὸς φυομένους ἰόνθους Σικελιῶται ψεύσματα ἔλεγον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Scholia 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 12.24, cf. Kaibel “CGF” p.218.}}

5583:
** ψεύσ-της**, ου, ὁ,
(ψεύδω) **liar, cheat,** {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.261; ἀνὴρ ψ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.209, cf. LXX.Sir.15.8:}} with _genitive_ _of things_, ὧν.. ψεῦσται φανούμεθα wherein we shall be found to be **liars,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1195, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1127b16, “Anthologia Graeca” 12.70 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1457.12.}}
**2** _adjective_, ={ψευδής}, **lying, false,** ψ. λόγος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.29}}; ψεύστης δ᾽ οτος ἔπεστι λίθος, of a cenotaph, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.273 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus)}}; τὸν ψεύσταν δέ με τύμβον.. θέντο· τί θαῦμ; Κρῆτες ὅπου ψεῦσται, καὶ Διός ἐστι τάφος{{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev. work] 275 (1st c.AD: Gaetulicus, Cn. Lentulus Epigrammaticus), alluding to Κρῆτες ἀεὶ ψεῦσται, 6th c.BC: Epimenides Philosophus 1}}, which is cited by {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Jovem” 8, NT.Tit.1.12.}}

5584:
** ψηλᾰφ-άω**,
mostly used in _present_: _future_ **-ήσω**{{LXX|LXX.Zech.9.13:}}_aorist_ ἐψηλάφησα{{LXX|LXX.Gen.27.22:—}}_passive_, _future_ ψηλαφηθήσομαι{{LXX|LXX.Nah.3.1}}: _aorist_ ἐψηλαφήθην {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 8.108, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.599c}}:—**feel** or **grope about** to find a thing, like a blind man or hoodman-blind, χερσὶ ψηλαφόων (_Epic dialect_ for -άων), of the Cyclops when blinded, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.416}}; ψηλαφῶν οὐκ ἐδυνάμην εὑρεῖν [τὸ ἱμάτιον] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 315; ψηλαφῶντες.. ὥσπερ ἐν σκότει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 99b: _metaphorically_, ψ. περί τινων 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 1.14.}}
**2** with _accusative_ _of things_, **feel about for, grope** or **search after,** ἐψηλαφῶμεν ἐν σκότῳ τὰ πράγματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 691}}; εἰ.. ψηλαφήσειαν αὐτὸν (i.e. τὸν θεὸν) καὶ εὕροιεν {{NT|NT.Act.17.27.}}
**II** **feel, touch, handle, stroke,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.183}}; μή ποτε ψηλαφήσῃ με ὁ πατήρ, of Isaac and Jacob, {{LXX+NT+4th c.BC+|LXX.Gen.27.12, cf. NT.Luke.24.39; ψ. καὶ τρίβειν τοῖς δακτύλοις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 571a33}}; especially in in Medicine texts, of uterine examination, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.40, +others}}:—_passive_, ὧν ψηλαφωμένων ὁ ἵππος.. ἥδεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 2.4; [ὄρνιθες] τῇ χειρὶ ψηλαφώμεναι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 560a9.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, **test, examine,** τὸν ν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.29.8; τὸν οἶνον “Geoponica” 7.5.1; πᾶσαν ἐπίνοιαν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.16.4; τὰς ἀλλήλων νοήσεις οἷον ὑπὸ σκότῳ διὰ φωνῆς ψ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.589b; εἴδωλον [τοῦ καλοῦ] διώκοντες καὶ ψ. [prev. work]766a:}} —_passive_, S.E.[same place], τὰ ψηλαφηθέντα ὑπ᾽ Ἀντιόχου the **attempts made** by.., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 13.9.2.}}

5585:
** ψηφ-ίζω**,
_future_ _Attic dialect_ -ιῶ (ἐπι-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.84}}: _aorist_ ἐψήφισα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.141c, (ἐπι-) 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.24:}}_perfect_ ἐψήφικα (ἐπ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.35, (κατ-) 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.8}}:— _middle_, see below {{Refs|11}}:—_passive_, see below {{Refs|111}}:—**count, reckon,** properly **with pebbles** (ψῆφοι), {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.26.13, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.168 (1st c.AD: Antiphanes Macedo Epigrammaticus), 171 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus)}}; ψ. δακτύλοις Plu. [prev. cited]
**2** ἐὰν ψηφίσῃς τὸ ἓν ἐν γράμμασιν, i.e. if you **add up the numerical values** of the letters in the word ἕν, {{Refs|“Theologumena Arithmeticae” 64.}}
**II** more frequently in _middle_ **ψηφίζομαι**: _future_ _Attic dialect_ ψηφιοῦμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 769, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.48, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 177d, etc. (ψηφίσομαι}} is _falsa lectio_ where found, e.g. in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.44, 14.47, (κατα-) 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.12, 6.10, (ἀπο-) [prev. passage]): _aorist_ ἐψηφισάμην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.97, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.48, etc.: _perfect_ ἐψήφισμαι}} in _middle_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 591 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.120, etc.}}:—properly, **cast one's vote with a pebble:**
**II.1** _absolutely_, εἰς ὑδρίαν ψ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.9, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 755 (_anapaest meter_)}}: generally, **vote,** ψήφῳ ψηφίζεσθαι μὴ φεύγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.55}}; τοῖς νόμοις ψηφίσασθαι **vote in support of** the laws, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.188; ἐναντία ψ. τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 177d.}}
**II.2** with _accusative_, **vote for, carry by vote,** τὸν πόλεμον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.86}}; ψηφίσασθαί τινι τὸν πλοῦν **vote** him the voyage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.29; ψ. παρασκευήν [prev. author] 6.25, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 951; ἐπιβολὴν ψ. [prev. author] “V.” 769; δίκην 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 3.7; ἄδειαν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.12; διαδίκασμα ψ. τινι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 17.10}}; κλῆρόν τινι ψ. **to adjudge** it to.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.6: with double _accusative_, ψ. τινα θεόν}} **vote** him a god, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.187e.}}
**II.3** with _infinitive_, **vote, resolve to do** something, with _infinitive_ _present_, ψ. μένειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.207, compare 9.55 (above quoted)}}; ψ. τι δρᾶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1353}}: with _infinitive_ _aorist_, ψ. νέας ἀποστεῖλαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.97, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 591 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 516e: with}}_infinitive_ _future_, ψ. πάντας ἀποσφάξειν (better ἀποσφάξαι as codex P) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.72}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **vote that**.., ψ. τὰς σπονδὰς λελύσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.88; ψ. ὥστε μὴ ἴσων ἕκαστον τυγχάνειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.20: ψ. ὅπως τις ἄρχοι μόνος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 54.}}
**II.4** ψ. περί, ὑπέρ τινος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Demodocus” 382d, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.154.}}
**III** _active_ is used in _signification_ **decide by vote,** δίκην κατ᾽ ἄλλου.. ἐψήφισαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 449}}, and is also found in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IGRom.” 4.293a ii 57 (Pergam., 2nd c.BC)}}; ψηφίζομεν is uncertain in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.3.4 (ἐπευφημίζομεν Schwartz):}} —_passive_, _aorist_ ψηφισθῆναι **be voted,** τοῖς στρατηγοῖς εἴ του προσδέοιντο ψηφισθῆναι ἐς τὸν ἔκπλουν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.8; τὸ ψήφισμα ἐψηφίσθη 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.29; τὰ ψηφισθέντα πλοῖα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.2.1}}: _future_, τὰ ψηφισθησόμενα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.92}}: _perfect_, ἐψηφισμένοι θανεῖν **condemned by vote,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 141; τοῖς ἰχθυοπώλαις ἐστὶν ἐψηφισμένον.. στῆσαι 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 56.}}

5586:
** ψῆφος**,
_Doric dialect_ **ψᾶφος**, _Aeolic dialect_ **ψᾶφαξ**, ἡ, _genitive_ _plural_ ψηφάων {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 4.448}}: (ψάω):—**a small round worn stone, pebble,** ψᾶφος ἑλισσομένα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).9; οὐκ ἂν εἰδείην λέγειν ποντιᾶν ψάφων ἀριθμόν [prev. work] 13.46}}; ψήφῳ μούνῃ διατετρανέεις, opposed to μόγις ἂν λίθῳ παίσας διαρρήξειας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.12}}; ψ. ἄμμου a **grain** of sand, {{LXX|LXX.Sir.18.10.}}
**2** **precious stone, gem,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.27}}; especially worn in a ring, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMeretr.” 9.}}
**II** _accusative_ to the various uses made of such pebbles:
**II.1** **pebble used for reckoning, counter,** λογίζεσθαι ψήφοις calculate or reckon by **abacus, cipher,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.36, etc.}}: hence to reckon **exactly** or **accurately,** opposed to ἀπὸ χειρὸς λ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 656 (_anapaest meter_); οὐ τιθεὶς ψήφους 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.229; ἐν ψήφῳ λέγειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 570; ἐν ψήφου λόγῳ θέσθαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 309: _metaphorically_, ταῖς τοῦ συμφέροντος ψήφοις μετρεῖν τὰς ἔχθρας καὶ τὰς φιλίας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.47.5: hence ψῆφος}} itself for a **cipher, number,** πὸτ ἄρτιον (i.e. ἀριθμὸν) ποτθέμειν.. ψᾶφον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 170}}: _plural_, **accounts,** καθαραὶ ψ., where there is an exact balance, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.227; οἱ περὶ τὰς ψ.}}**calculators,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.26; ψήφων ἄπειρος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.812e}}; δακτυλικὴ ψ. **reckoning** on the fingers, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.290 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus)}}; of astrological **calculations,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 10.15, +others}}
**II.1.b** in Magic, κατέχων τὴν ψ. (i. e. the object on which the **number** is written) λέγε.. {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.1048, compare 937.}}
**II.2** **pebble used for a draughts-** or **chess-man,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 487c; κύβος ἐν παιδιᾷ ψήφων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.427f.}}
**II.2.b** **pebble** used by jugglers, ψηφάων παῖκται {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 4.448.}}
**II.3** **pebble used in divination,** ἡ διὰ ψήφων μαντική {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 3.10.2.}}
**II.4** **cube** used in mosaic pavements, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “προτρεπτικός - Scripta Minora” 8.}}
**II.5** **pebble used in voting,** ψήφῳ ψηφίζεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.55; ἐὰν μὴ τῇ ψ... ψηφίσωνται κρύβδην ψηφιζόμενοι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.89}}: hence, **the vote** itself, ψῆφον φέρειν give **one's vote,** ἐν καρδίᾳ ψ. φέροντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 680, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.2, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.61, etc.; ὑπέρ τινος 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 7; περί τινος [prev. author] 11, etc.; ψήφου φορά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 484}}; ψῆφον τίθεσθαι, ={ψηφίζεσθαι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.123, compare 6.57; εἰς τεῦχος.. ψήφους ἔθεντο 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 816}}; with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.73; ψ. προσθέσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.40}}; ψήφῳ διαιρεῖν to determine **by vote,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 630}}; ψήφῳ κρίνειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.87, etc.; μεταλαβὼν τὸ πέμπτον μέρος τῶν ψ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36b, cf. Legal cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.47: in _collective_ sense, ἐχρῆν.. ψ. περὶ αὐτοῦ γενέσθαι}} **a vote** is taken, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.47; ἡ καθαιροῦσα ψ. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.37; ἡ σῴζουσα ψ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.66}}; οἷς ἂν πλείστη γένηται ψ. a majority **of votes,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 759d: τὴν ψῆφον ἐπάγειν}} to put **the vote** or **question,** of the president, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.119, 125; ψῆφον δοῦναι περί τινος “IG” 22.222.24, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.188; ψ. ἀναδοὺς περί τινος 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.100; so ψ. περὶ ἡμῶν ὑπὲρ ἀνδραποδισμοῦ προτεθεῖσαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.65}}; διένεμον (variants διενέμοντο, ἔφερον) τὰς ψ. were casting their **votes,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.123}}; ὑπὸ ψήφου μιᾶς with one **accord,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 270}}; ψ. φανερά open **voting,** ψ. φανερὰν διενεγκεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.74; τὴν ψ. οὐκ εἰς καδίσκους ἀλλὰ φανερὰν ἐπὶ τὰς τραπέζας τίθεσθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.37, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 767d, 855d}}; opposed to ψ. ἀφανής **voting** by ballot, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.233; κρύβδην τὴν ψ. φέρειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1433a23, compare 1424b2, “Ath.” 69.1.}}
**II.5.b** **that which is carried by vote, a vote,** ψ. καταγνώσεως **a vote** of condemnation, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82}}; ψῆφος αὐτῷ ἐπῆκτο περὶ φυγῆς **a vote** of banishment was moved for against him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.57, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 198; ψήφῳ πόλεως γνωσθεῖσαι [prev. author] “Supp.” 7 (_anapaest meter_):}} —hence,
**II.5.c** **any resolve** or **decree,** ψ. τυράννων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 60}}; λιθίνα ψᾶφος **a decree** written on stone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.87}}; διδοῖ ψᾶφον περ᾽ αὐτᾶς [the oak] gives **judgement** of itself, {{Refs|[prev. author] “P.” 4.265}}; ψ. φλεγυρὰ βροτῶν, i. e. **public opinion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 57 (Lyric poetry)}}; τίν᾽ ἂν ψῆφον θεῖ; what **judgment**.. ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 330c, compare “R.” 450a; ἡ ἐμὴ ψ. [prev. author] “Phlb.” 57a.}}
**II.5.d** Ἀθηνᾶς ψ., **calculus Minervae,** proverbial phrase to express **acquittal,** when the votes were even, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.3.}}
**II.5.e** ψ. is sometimes omitted, κἂν ἴσαι γένωνται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 685 (Lyric poetry); πάσαις κρατεῖν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Bis Acc.” 18, compare 22.}}
**II.5.f** Διὸς ψῆφος (ψῆφοι {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus), proverbial ἐπὶ τῶν ἱερῶν καὶ ἀθίκτων}} of the scene of contest betw. Athena and Poseidon, {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus, etc.}}
**II.5.g** Κόννου ψ., **negligible quantity, cipher,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 675 (_anapaest meter_), compare Κοννᾶς.}}
**II.6** **place of voting, tribunal,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 945, “El.” 1263.}}
**II.7** _metaphorically_, **influence,** πόλις μεγάλην ψ. ἔχουσα {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 18.13.}}

5587:
** ψῐθῠρ-ισμός**, ὁ,
**whispering,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.55 W., 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 15; πρός τινα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.45d, etc.}}
**2** **slandering,** {{NT|[prev. work]143f, NT.2Cor.12.20 (_plural_).}}
**3** **whistle** of a snake-charmer, {{LXX|LXX.Eccl.10.11.}}
**4** **crepitation,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.159.}}

5588:
** ψῐθῠρ-ιστής**, οῦ, ὁ,
**whisperer,** at Athens as epithet of Hermes, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.39}}; of Ἔρως, {{Refs|“Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 317}}: perhaps compare **Mercurius Susurrio**, {{Refs|“CIL” 13.12005 (from Aachen).}}
**2** **slanderer,** {{NT|NT.Rom.1.30.}}

5589:
** ψῑχ-ίον**, τό,
_diminutive_ of ψίξ, **crumb, ** NT.Matt.15.27.
**2** of stomachic residue after emesis, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 8.23.5.}}

5590:
** ψῡχ-ή**, ἡ,
**life,** λύθη ψ. τε μένος τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.296, etc.; ψ. τεκαὶ αἰών 16.453, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.523; θυμοῦ καὶ ψ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.334, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.154; λαυκανίην, ἵνα τε ψυχῆς ὤκιστος ὄλεθρος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.325}}; ψυχὰς παρθέμενοι at hazard of their **lives,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.74, 9.255; αἰεὶ ἐμὴν ψ. παραβαλλόμενος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.322}}; λίσσου᾽ ὑπὲρ ψ. καὶ γούνων by your **life,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|22.338; so ἀντὶ ψ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1326: but περὶ ψ.}} to save their **life,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.423; περί τε ψυχέων ἐμάχοντο 22.245; περὶ ψ. θέον Ἕκτορος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.161; τρέχων περὶ τῆς ψ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.37; τῆς ἐμῆς περὶ ψ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 115, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 946, “Heracl.” 984; περὶ ψ. κινδυνεύων 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.4, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.50; ἁγὼν.. σῆς ψ. πέρι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1492, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1330, “Or.” 847, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.44; τὸν περὶ ψ. δρόμον δραμεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 375 (Lyric poetry); ἀγωνίζεσθαι περὶ τῆς ψ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 1.19}}; ὃ ἂν θέλῃ, ψυχῆς ὠνεῖται [θυμός] in exchange for **life,** {{NT+7th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 85; τῆς ψ. πρίασθαί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.36; τί γὰρ δοῖ ἄνθρωπος ἀντάλλαγμα τῆς ψ. αὐτο; NT.Mark.8.37. In early poets: ψυχὰν ἀποπνεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 52; ψυχὰς ἔχοντες κυμάτων ἐν ἀγκάλαις 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 23; ψυχέων φειδόμενοι 7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 10.14; θειδωλὴν ψ. θέμενος 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 13.46; ψυχῆς εἵνεκα καὶ βιότου 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 730; ψυχὰν Ἀΐδᾳ τελέων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.68; ψυχὰς βαλον [prev. author] “O.” 8.39; χαλκῷ ἀπὸ ψυχὴν ἀρύσας 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 138; τοὐμὸν ἐκπίνουσ᾽ ἀεὶ ψυχῆς ἄκρατον αἷμα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 786}}; τῆς ἐμῆς ψ. γεγώς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 775; τὴν ψ. ἐκπίνουσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 712 (_anapaest meter_); ψ. ἀφήσω 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1171; ψ. σέθεν ἔκτεινε [prev. author] “Tr.” 1214; ψ. παραιτέεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.24}}; ποινὴν τῆς Αἰσώπου ψ. satisfaction for the **life** of {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus, [prev. author] 2.134; ψυχῆς ἀποστερῆσαί τινα 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.1.6, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.136, etc.; τὴν ψ. ἢ τὴν οὐσίαν ἢ τὴν ἐπιτιμίαν τινὸς ἀφελόμενος 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.88; τὸ τῆς ψ. ἀπαιτηθεὶς χρέος LXX.Wis.15. 8, cf. NT.Luke.12.20; ζητοῦσι τὴν ψ. μου LXX.1Ki.19.10, cf. NT.Matt.2.20; τὴν ψ. αὐτοῦ τίθησιν ὑπὲρ τῶν προβάτων NT.John.10.11, etc.}}; δεῖρον ἄχρις ἡ ψ... ἐπὶ χειλέων λειφθῇ within an inch of his **life,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.3:}} —the phrase ἐν τῇ χειρὶ τὴν ψ. ἔχοντα taking his **life** in his hands, is probably _falsa lectio_ in {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Xenarchus Comicus 4.20; ἡ ψ. μου ἐν ταῖς χερσί [σου] διὰ πάντος LXX.Psa.118(119).109, cf. LXX.1Ki.19.5, 28.21, +others}}; of **life** in animals, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.426, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 173, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.47, etc.; τὰ ἄλλα ζῷα, ὅσα ψ. ἔχει 5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 4, compare 12; πάντων τῶν ζῴων ἡ ψ. τὸ αὐτό, ἀήρ 4th c.BC: Diogenes Cynicus Philosophus Apoll.5 (compare infr. IV. 1)}}; ἡ φύσις τοιαύτη πάντων ὅσσα ψ. ἔχει Democrit.278; ἐπῴζει καὶ ποιεῖ ψ. ἔχειν (of incubation) {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 172; [ἑρπετὸν] ὃ ἔχει ἐν ἑαυτῷ ψ. ζωῆς LXX.Gen.1.30}}; ἡ ψ. πάσης σαρκὸς αἷμα αὐτοῦ ἐστιν{{LXX|LXX.Lev.17.11, cf. LXX.Deu.12.23.}}
**2** _metaphorically_ of things **dear as life,** χρήματα γὰρ ψ... βροτοῖσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 686; πᾶσι δ᾽ ἀνθρώποις ἄρ᾽ ἦν ψ. τέκν᾽ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 419; τἀργύριόν ἐστιν αἷμα καὶ ψ. βροτοῖς 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 35}}; so as an endearing name, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 1.8, +others; ζωὴ καὶ ψ. 1st c.AD: Juvenalis, D. Junius Poeta Latinus 6.195; ψ. μου 1st c.AD: Martialis Epigrammaticus Latinus 10.68.}}
**II** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **departed spirit, ghost** (ὑποτίθεται [Ὅμηρος] τὰς ψ. τοῖς εἰδώλοις τοῖς ἐν τοῖς κατόπτροις φαινομένοις ὁμοίας.. ἃ καθάπαξ ἡμῖν ἐξείκασται καὶ τὰς κινήσεις μιμεῖται, στερεμνιώδη δὲ ὑπόστασιν οὐδεμίαν ἔχει εἰς ἀντίληψιν καὶ ἁφήν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus Hist. “fragment” 102(a) 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus); ψ. Πατροκλῆος.. πάντ᾽ αὐτῷ.. ἐϊκυῖα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.65: frequently in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11, ψ. Ἀγαμέμνονος, Ἀχιλῆος, etc., 387, 467, +others}}; ψ. καὶ εἴδωλον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.104, compare 72, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.14; ψ. κατὰ χθονὸς ᾤχετο τετριγυῖα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.100}}; ψυχὰς ἡρώων, opposed to αὐτούς,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.3, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 151; ψυχαὶ δ᾽ Ἄϊδόσδε κατῆλθον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.330; ψ. δὲ κατ᾽ οὐταμένην ὠτειλὴν ἔσσυτ᾽ ἐπειγομένη 14.518}}; sometimes hardly distinct from _signification_{{Refs|1, ἅμα ψ. τε καὶ ἔγχεος ἐξέρυσ᾽ αἰχμήν 16.505}}; in swoons it leaves the body, τὸν δὲ λίπε ψ. {{Refs|5.696}}; so in later writers (seldom in Trag.), σὺν Ἀγαμεμνονίᾳ ψυχᾷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.21}}; ἑὰν ψυχὰν κομίξαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 4.159, compare “N.” 8.44; αἱ ψ. ὀσμῶνται καθ᾽ Ἅιδην 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 98; πέμψατ᾽ ἔνερθεν ψυχὴν ἐς φῶς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 630 (_anapaest meter_); ποτωμένην ψ. ὑπὲρ σοῦ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 676, compare “fragment” 912.9 (_anapaest meter_); τὰς τῶν κεκμηκότων ψ., αἷς ἐστιν ἐν τῇ φύσει τῶν αὑτῶν ἐκγόνων κήδεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 927b}}; ψ. σοφαί, perhaps 'wise **ghosts',** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 94; δὶς ἀποθανουμένη ψ. Anonymus cited in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.236d.}}
**III** the immaterial and immortal **soul,** first in Pindar, ἐς τὸν ὕπερθεν ἅλιον κείνων.. ἀνδιδοῖ [Φερσεφόνα] ψυχὰς πάλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“fragment” 133, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 81b; εἰπόντες ὡς ἀνθρώπου ψ. ἀθάνατός ἐστι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.123; ἀγένητόν τε καὶ ἀθάνατον ψ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 246a, compare “Phd.” 70c, +others; ἀθάνατος ἡμῶν ἡ ψ. καὶ οὐδέποτε ἀπόλλυται [prev. author] “R.” 608d; ἁψ. τῷ σώματι συνέζευκται καὶ καθάπερ ἐν σάματι τέθαπται 5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 14, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 400c: hence frequently opposed to σῶμα, ψ. καὶ σῶμα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.5, compare “An.” 3.2.20; ψ. ἢ σῶμα ἢ συναμφότερον, τὸ ὅλον τοῦτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.130a; εἰς θηρίου βίον ἀνθρωπίνη ψ. ἀφικνεῖται καὶ ἐκ θηρίου.. πάλιν εἰς ἄνθρωπον [prev. author] “Phdr.” 249b; κατὰ τοὺς Πυθαγορικοὺς μύθους τὴν τυχοῦσαν ψ. εἰς τὸ τυχὸν ἐνδύεσθαι σῶμα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 407b22; οὐδὲ τοῦτο ἐπείσθην, ὡς ἡ ψ., ἕως μὲν ἂν ἐν θνητῷ σώματι ᾖ, ζῇ, ὅταν δὲ τούτου ἀπαλλαγῇ, τέθνηκεν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.19; ἀνθρώπου γε ψ., ἣ τοῦ θείου μετέχει,.. ὁρᾶται δ᾽ οὐδ᾽ αὐτή [prev. author] “Mem.” 4.3.14, compare “Cyr.” 8.7.17}}; αἰθὴρ μὲμ ψυχὰς ὑπεδέξατο, σώ[ματα δὲ χθών] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.945 (5th c.BC)}}; ὁπόταμ ψ. προλίπῃ φάος ἀελίοιο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 32 f. 1; ἡμεῖς ἐσμεν ψ., ζῷον ἀθάνατον ἐν θνητῷ καθειργμένον φρουρίῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 365e.}}
**IV** the **conscious self** or **personality** as centre of emotions, desires, and affections, χερσὶ καὶ ψυχᾷ δυνατοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.39; μορφὰν βραχύς, ψυχὰν δ᾽ ἄκαμπτος [prev. author] “I.” 4(3).53(71); ἐνίους τῶν καλῶν τὰς μορφὰς μοχθηροὺς ὄντας τὰς ψ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 6.16; θνητοῦ σώματος ἔτυχες, πειρῶ τῆς ψ. ἀθάνατον μνήμην καταλιπεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.37; opposed to material blessings, κτεάνων ψ. ἔχοντες κρέσσονας 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.32; μήτε σωμάτων ἐπιμελεῖσθαι μήτε χρημάτων.. οὕτω σφόδρα ὡς τῆς ψ. ὅπως ὡς ἀρίστη ἔσται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 30b, compare 29e}}: hence regarded in abstraction, τὸ παρεχόμενον ἡμῶν ἕκαστον τοῦτ᾽ εἶναι μηδὲν ἀλλ᾽ ἢ τὴν ψ., τὸ δὲ σῶμα ἰνδαλλόμενον ἡμῶν ἑκάστοις ἕπεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 959a; ἡ ψ. ἐστιν ἄνθρωπος [prev. author] Alcaeus1.130c; οὐδὲ νῦν τήν γ ἐμὴν ψ. ἑωρᾶτε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.17, compare above 111: sometimes, therefore, distinguished from oneself, ψ. γὰρ ηὔδα πολλά μοι μυθουμένη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 227; ἡ ψ. μου πεπότηται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 319 (_anapaest meter_); τί ποτ᾽ ἔστι μαθεῖν ἔραται ψ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 173 (_anapaest meter_); ἄλλο τι βουλομένη ἑκατέρου ἡ ψ. δήλη ἐστίν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 192c}}; οἴμοι ψυχή woe is **me!** {{LXX|LXX.Mic.7.1}}; καὶ ἐρῶ τῇ ψ. μου, “ψυχή, ἔχεις πολλὰ ἀγαθά” {{NT|NT.Luke.12.19}}; in periphrases, ψ. Ὀρέστου, ={Ὀρέστης}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1127, +others: but τὴν Φιλοκτήτου ψ. ἐκκλέψεις}} his **wits,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 55; ἡ δ᾽ ἐμὴ ψ. τέθνηκεν [prev. author] “Ant.” 559, compare “OC” 999}}; so ψυχαί _absolutely_, ={ἄνθρωποι, ψ. ὀλέσασα} {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1457 (Lyric poetry)}}; ψ. πολλαὶ ἔθανον many **souls** perished, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 864; πᾶσαι αἱ ψ., υἱοὶ καὶ αἱ θυγατέρες λ γ LXX.Gen.46.15, cf. LXX.Exo.12.4, +others; [κιβωτὸς] εἰς ἣν ὀλίγοι, τοῦτ᾽ ἔστιν ὀκτὼ ψ., διεσώθησαν NT.1Pet.3.20. In apostrophe, μή, φίλα ψ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.61; ὦ μελέα ψ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 712 (Lyric poetry); ὦ ἀγαθὴ καὶ πιστὴ ψ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.3.8}}; in referring to persons, ὅταν μεγάλη ψ. φυῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 496b (compare μεγαλόψυχος)}}; καλεῖται γοῦν ἡ ψ. Κρινοκοράκα the **creature,** {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 28.2; πάσῃ ψ. τετελευτηκυίᾳ LXX.Num.6.6, 11; πᾶσα ψ. ὑποτασσέσθω NT.Rom.13.1, etc.}}: generally, **being,** ψυχὴ ζῶσα living **creature,** {{LXX|LXX.Gen.1.24, compare 20 (_plural_).}}
**IV.2** of various aspects of the **self,** ἐν πολέμοιο μάχαις τλάμονι ψ. παρέμειν) enduring **heart,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.48; διεπειρᾶτο αὐτοῦ τῆς ψ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.14, ἦν ηὰρ.. ψυχὴν οὐκ ἄκρος}} poor-**spirited,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.124; ψυχὴν ἄριστε πάντων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 457; καρτερὰν ψ. λαβεῖν [prev. author] “Ach.” 393; κράτιστοι ἂν τὴν ψ. κριθεῖεν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.40; τοῖς σώμασι δύνανται τὰς δὲ ψ. οὐκ ἔχουσιν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.29; ὁ γὰρ᾽ λόγχην ἀκονῶν καὶ τὴν ψ. τι παρακονᾷ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.33, compare “Oec.” 21.3.}}
**IV.3** of the emotional self, ὑπείργασμαι μὲν εὖ ψυχὴν ἔρωτι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 505, compare 527 (Lyric poetry); πάνυ μου ἡ ψ. ἐπεθύμει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 6.14; τίνα ποτὲ ψ. ἔχω; 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.12}}; τίν᾽ οἴεσθ᾽ αὐτὴν ψ. ἕξειν, ὅταν ἐμὲ ῒδ; how will she **feel?** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.21}}; μία ψ., proverbial of friends, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1168b7}}; ψ. μία ἤστην probably in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista “Praeparatio Sophistica” p.128B.}}; of appetite, ψυχῇ διδόντες ἡδονήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 841 (assuming variant), compare 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 297, Theocr. 16.24; λίχνῳ δὲ ὄντι τὴν ψ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 579b; τῷ δὲ ἡ ψ. σῖτον μὲν οὐ προσίετο, διψῆν δ᾽ ἐδόκει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.4.}}
**IV.4** of the moral and intellectual **self,** ἀπὸ πάμπαν ἀδίκων ἔχειν ψ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.70; ψ. τε καὶ φρόνημα καὶ γνώμην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 176; ἀρκεῖν.. κἀντὶ μυρίων μίαν ψ. τάδ᾽ ἐκτίνουσαν, ἢν εὔνους παρῇ [prev. author] “OC” 499; ψ. γὰρ εὔνους καὶ φρονοῦσα τοὔνδικον [prev. author] “fragment” 101; ἡ κακὴ σὴ ψ. [prev. author] “Ph.” 1014; ψυχῆς κατήγορος κακῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 20.15, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 353e; ἡ βουλεύσασα ψ. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.1.7, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 873a}}; τὸ σῶμα ἀπειρηκὸς ἡ ψ. συνεξέσωσεν.. διὰ τὸ μὴ ξυνειδέναι ἑαυτῇ the **mind** conscious of innocence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.93; τὸ ἐπιμελεῖσθαι καὶ ἄρχειν καὶ βουλεύεσθαι.. ἐσθ᾽ ὅτῳ ἄλλῳ ἢ ψυχῇ δικαίως ἂν ἀποδοῖμε; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 353d; τὴν τῆς ψ. ἐπιμέλειαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.304; τὰ ἐν τῇ ψ. διὰ τὴν παιδείαν ἐγγιγνόμενα [prev. work]290; τῆς ψ. ἐξελθούσης, ἐν ᾗ μόνῃ γίγνεται φρόνησις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.53; νοῦς τε καὶ ψ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 400a, compare “Phdr.” 247c, +others; ἐμπαίει τί μοι ψυχῇ σύνηθες ὄμμα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 903; ἰδὼν μὲν γνούς τε σῇ ψ., τέκνον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1171. Phrases:— ἐκ τῆς ψ. φίλος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.43}}; ἀπὸ τῆς ψ. φιλεῖν with all the **heart,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 17.3; βόσκοιτ᾽ ἐκ ψυχᾶς τὰς ἀμνάδας 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 8.35; ὅλῃ τῇ ψ. κεχαρίσθαι τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.10}}; οὐκ ἐᾷ ἡμᾶς οὐδὲ ψυχῆς λαχεῖν he won't let us call our **soul** our own, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista “Praeparatio Sophistica” p.128B.}}
**IV.5** of animals, ψ. μεγαλόφρων, of a horse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 11.1; θηρίων ψ. ἡμεροῦμεν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.12}}; ψ. χηνός, ὀρτυγίου, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 101, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 5.}}
**IV.6** of inanimate things, πᾶσα πολιτεία ψ. πόλεώς ἐστιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.138, compare 7.14; ἡ τῶνδε τῶν ἀνδρῶν ἀρετὴ τῆς Ἑλλάδος ἦν ψ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.23; οἷον ψ. ὁ μῦθος τῆς τραγῳδίας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1450a38}}; also of the **spirit** of an author, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Lysia” 11.}}
**V** Philosophical uses:
**V.1** In the early physicists, of the primary substance, the source of **life and consciousness,** ὁρίζονται πάντες (i.e. οἱ πρότεροι) τὴν ψ. τρισίν, κινήσει, αἰσθήσει, τῷ ἀσωμάτῳ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 405b11; τὸν λίθον ἔφη [Θαλῆς] ψ. ἔχειν ὅτι τὸν σίδηρον κινεῖ, of the magnet, [prev. work] 405a20}}; ψυχῇσιν θάνατος ὕδωρ γενέσθαι, ὕδατι δὲ θάνατος γῆν γενέσθαι, ἐκ γῆς δὲ ὕδωρ γίνεται, ἐξ ὕδατος δὲ ψ. (i.e. πῦρ) {{Refs 6th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 36; ἡ ψ. πνεῦμα 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 9.19}}; καρδία ψυχῆς καὶ αἰσθήσιος [ἀρχά] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 13; τοῦτο [ἀὴρ] αὐτοῖς καὶ ψ. ἐστι καὶ νόησις 4th c.BC: Diogenes Cynicus Philosophus Apoll.4; τὴν τῶν ἄλλων ἁπάντων φύσιν οὐ πιστεύεις Ἀναξαγόρᾳ νοῦν καὶ ψ. εἶναι τὴν διακοσμοῦσα; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 400a, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 404a25}}; Δημόκριτος πῦρ τι καὶ θερμόν θησιν αὐτὴν (i.e. ψυχὴν) εἶναι{{Refs|[prev. work] 404a1, compare “Resp.” 472a4.}}
**V.2** the **spirit** of the universe, ψ. εἰς τὸ μέσον [τοῦ κόσμου] θείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 34b, compare 30b; τὴν τοῦ παντὸς δῆλον ὅτι τοιαύτην εἶναι βούλεται [ὁ Τίμαιος] οἷόν ποτ᾽ ἐστὶν ὁ καλούμενος νοῦς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 407a3}}; ἐν τῷ ὅλῳ τινὲς [τὴν ψ.] μεμεῖχθαί φασιν, ὅθεν ἴσως καὶ Θαλῆς ᾠήθη πάντα πλήρη θεῶν εἶναι{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 411a8; ὁ κόσμος ψ. ἐστὶν ἑαυτοῦ καὶ ἡγεμονικόν 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.186}}; ψ. [κόσμου] {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1013e, compare 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 4.40; ψ. ἐλθοῦσα εἰς σῶμα οὐρανοῦ 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.1.2; τόδε τὸ πᾶν ψ. μίαν ἔχον εἰς πάντα αὐτοῦ μέρη [prev. author] 4.4.32}}; περὶ ψυχᾶς κόσμου καὶ φύσιος, title of work by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus}}
**V.3** In {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus}} the immaterial principle of movement and life, ὅταν παρῇ [ψυχὴ] τῷ σώματι, αἴτιόν ἐστι τοῦ ζῆν αὐτῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 399d, compare “Def.” 411c; [ψυχῆς λόγον ἔχομεν] τὴν δυναμένην αὐτὴν αὑτὴν κινεῖν κίνησιν [prev. author] “Lg.” 896a; μεταβολῆς τε καὶ κινήσεως ἁπάσης αἰτία [ἡ ψ.] ἅπασιν [prev. work] b, compare 892c}}; its presence is requisite for thought, σοφία καὶ νοῦς ἄνευ ψ. οὐκ ἂν γενοίσθην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 30c, compare “Ti.” 30b, “Sph.” 249a; defined by 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus as οὐσία ὡς εἶδος σώματος φυσικοῦ δυνάμει ζωὴν ἔχοντος “de An.” 412a20; ἐντελέχεια ἡ πρώτη σώματος φυσικοῦ ὀργανικοῦ [prev. work] 412b5}}; the tripartite division of ψ., οἱ δὲ περὶ Πλάτωνα καὶ Ἀρχύτας καὶ οἱ λοιποὶ Πυθαγόρειοι τὴν ψ. τριμερῆ ἀποφαίνονται, διαιροῦντες εἰς λογισμὸν καὶ θυμὸν καὶ ἐπιθυμίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.49.34, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 439e.ff; in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus ἡ ψ. τούτοις ὥρισται, θρεπτικῷ, αἰσθητικῷ, διανοητικῷ, κινήσει· πότερον δὲ τοὔτων ἕκαστόν ἐστι ψ. ἢ ψυχῆς μόριο; “de An.” 413b11, compare “PA” 641b4; ἡ θρεπτικὴ ψ. [prev. author] “de An.” 434a22, +others}}; in the Stoics and Epicureans, σῶμα ἡ ψ. Zeno and {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 1.38}}; of the **scala naturae,** τὰ μὲν ἕξει διοικεῖται, τὰ δὲ φύσει, τὰ δ᾽ ἀλόγῳ ψ., τὰ δὲ καὶ λόγον ἐχούσῃ καὶ διάνοιαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.150, compare 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 6.14; ἡ ψ. σῶμά ἐστι λεπτομερές.. προσεμφερέστατον πνεύματι θερμοῦ τινα κρᾶσιν ἔχοντι 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.19U.; τέλος.. τὸ μήτε ἀλγεῖν κατὰ σῶμα μήτε ταράττεσθαι κατὰ ψ. [prev. author] “Ep.” 3p.64U.}}; in the Neo-Platonists characterized by discursive thinking, τοὺς λογισμοὺς ψυχῆς εἶναι ἐνεργήματα {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.1.7}}; related to νοῦς as image to archetype, εἰκών τίς ἐστι νοῦ [ψ.] {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.1.3}}; present in entirety in every part, πάρεστι πᾶσα πανταχοῦ ψ. {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.1.2, compare 4.7.5; φύσις ψ. οὖσα, γέννημα ψυχῆς προτέρας [prev. author] 3.8.4}}; animal and vegetable bodies possess οἷον σκιὰν ψυχῆς {{Refs 5th c.AD+|[prev. author] 4.4.18; πᾶν σῶμα.. ψυχῆς μετουσίᾳ κινεῖται ἐξ ἑαυτοῦ καὶ ζῇ διὰ ψ. 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 20.}}
**VI** **butterfly** or **moth,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 551a14, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.4.4, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.636c.}}
**VI.2** τριπόλιον, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.132.}}
**VII** **Psyche,** in the allegory of Psyche and Eros, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Apuleius Scriptor Botanicus “Metam.” books 4-6, Aristophontes cited in Fulg. “Myth.” 3.6. (See ancient speculations on the derivation, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 399d - 400a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 405b29, 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.222; 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus usage gives little support to the derivation from ψύχω}} 'blow, breathe'; τὸν δὲ λίπε ψ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.696}} means 'his **spirit** left his body', and so λειποψυχέω means 'swoon', not 'become breathless'; ἀπὸ δὲ ψ. ἐκάπυσσε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.467}} means 'she gasped out her **spirit**', _i.e. _ 'swooned'; the resemblance of ἄμπνυτο 'recovered consciousness' to ἀμπνέω 'recover breath' is deceptive, see at {ἄμπνυτο, ἔμπνυτο}: when concrete the Homeric ψ. is rather **warm blood** than **breath,**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.518, 16.505, where the ψ.}} escapes through a wound; compare ψυχοπότης, ψυχορροφέω, and {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 786, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 712 (see. above 1).)}}

5591:
** ψῡχι-κός**, ή, όν,
**of the soul** or **life, spiritual,** opposed to σωματικός, ἡδοναί {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1117b28; ὁρμαί 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.10.9}}; πνεῦμα ψ. the spirit, or breath **of life,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1084e, etc.}}; νόσος {{Refs|[prev. work]524d.}} _adverb_ **-κῶς**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.81}}; opposed to σωματικῶς, νοερῶς, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 139}}; also, **heartily, from the heart,** {{LXX|LXX.2Mac.4.37, 14.24.}}
**2** **of the animal life, animal,** ὁ ψ. ἄνθρωπος the **natural** man, opposed to ὁ πνευματικός, {{NT+9th c.AD+|NT.1Cor.2.14, cf. NT.Jude.19, 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc. (which see)}}
**3** **brave,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 338.}}
**II** **for the soul** or **spirit** of one deceased, ψ. δῶρα διδούς, i.e. to Hermes, {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 815.4 (from Crete).}}
**III** **cooling,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 6.27 (assuming variant).}}

5592:
** ψῦχος**, εος, τό,
(ψύχω {{Refs|11)}} **cold,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 65.2}}; opposed to θάλπος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 3.4}}; opposed to ἀλέα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 598a1}}; opposed to καύματα, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mete.” 362b17}}; ἐν ψύχει in **winter-time,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 17; ἐν τῷ ψ. καθηῦδον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220d}}; ψ., ={ῥῖγος}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 97: _plural_ ψύχεα}}**frosts, cold weather,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.28, 129, 5.10; ψύχη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 5.4, “Cyn.” 5.9; ἐν τοῖς σφόδρα ψ. καὶ ἐν ταῖς σφόδρα ἀλέαις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 599a19, compare “Mete.” 379a26; _sing._, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 16.}}
**2** once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **coolness,** ψύχεος ἱμείρων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.555: _metaphorically_, ψ. ἐν δόμοις πέλει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 971.}}

5593:
** ψῡχρός**, ά, όν,
**cold,** χάλαζα, νιφάδες, χιών, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.171, 19.358, 22.152}}; ψ. χαλκός (as we say '**cold** steel') {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5.75: frequently of water, ψ. ὕδωρ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.392, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49}}; ψυχρόν (without ὕδωρ) {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 263; λοῦνται ψυχρῷ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.37; ἀναγαργαρίζεσθαι ψυχρῷ “IG” 42(1).126.30 (Epid., 2nd c.AD) (but τὸ ψυχρὸν}} also ={ψῦχος}, **cold,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.142); ψ. ὥστε λούσασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.13.3}}: of the air, αὔρη ψ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.469; αἰθήρ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.88 (assuming variant); νύκτες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.87; κυνὸς ψυχρὰ δύσις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 432.11}}; ψ. βίος life **in the cold,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 263: especially of dead things, νέκυς (opposed to θερμὸν αἷμα) 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 622}}; of **cold** meats, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 173.4, etc.}}; of a snake, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.58}}: _comparative_ **-ότερος**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.22, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 24b}}: _superlative_ **-ότατος**{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.41.}}
**II** _metaphorically_,
**II.1** **ineffectual, vain,** ἐπικουρίη ψ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.108; ἐπαρθεὶς ψυχρῇ νίκῃ [prev. author] 9.49; ψ. παραγκάλισμα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 650}}; θερμὴν ἐπὶ ψυχροῖσι καρδίαν ἔχεις a hot spirit in **a cold business,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 88.}}
**II.2** of feelings, ψ. τέρψις, ἐλπίς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 353, “IA” 1014.}}
**II.3** of persons, **cold-hearted, heartless, indifferent,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.4.22, 23; ψ. καὶ μελαγχολικοί 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Magna Moralia” 1203b1; ἐκ σιδάρου κεχάλκευται μέλαιναν καρδίαν ψυχρᾷ φλογί 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 123.5; οὔτε ψ. εἶ οὔτε ζεστός NT.Rev.3.15.}}
**II.4** of **flat, lifeless, insipid** productions, τὸν Παλαμήδην (the play so named) ψυχρὸν ὄντ᾽ αἰσχύνεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 848; σκῶμμα.. σφόδρα ψ. 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 244}}; ψ. καὶ ἀηδὴς [Μοῦσα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 802d; ἕωλα καὶ ψ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.112; πρᾶγμα.. φρέατος.. ψυχρότερον Ἀραρότος 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 179, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1405b34, 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 114, etc.}}: hence jokes in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 138 - 140, Machoap. 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.580a}}; also of authors themselves, γίνεται ψυχρός {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Isocrate” 3}}. _adverb_, ὁ δ᾽ αὖ Θέογνις ψυχρὸς ὢν ψυχρῶς ποιεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 170}}; σκώψαντι ψ. ἐπιγελάσαι to laugh at a **feeble** joke, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 2.4; τοὺς γοῦν ψυχροὺς ψ. λέγουσι διαλέγεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 284e.}}
**II.5** **silly,** γραϊδίοις ψυχροῖς ὁμιλοῦντες {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Epistulae” 89b. [Written ψυχθρός “IG” 12(5).1104 (Syros, 2nd c.AD)}}; compare conversely μάκρα for μάκτρα (ψυχρός originally 'cooled by blowing' from ψύχω 'blow').]

5594:
** ψύχω**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.104, etc.}}: _future_ ψύξω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 25.10, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 653b4}}: _aorist_ ἔψυξα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.440, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φυσῶν” 7:}}_perfect_ ἔψῡχα Ps.-{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus Gr. in “An. Ox.” 3.256; but ἔψῠχα 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 2.73}} H.:—_passive_, _future_ ψυχθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut.” (“Sp.”)}}: _future_ [{{NT+2nd c.AD+|2 ψῠγήσομαι NT.Matt.24.12 (variant{ψῠχήσομαι}), 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.388}}]: _aorist_ ἐψύχθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.19, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 60d, 76c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.19, compare ἀναψύχω:}}_aorist 2_ ἐψύχην [ῠ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 151, (ἀπ-) 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 104, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phdr..” 242a; κατα-ψῠχῆναι “Inscription Magn.” 103.55 (2nd c.BC): later ἐψύγην 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.55, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.748, (δια-) “PSI” 6.603.11 (3rd c.BC), cf. Moer. p.421}} P.: _perfect_ ἔψυγμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 1.33, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 120b, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 124.15}}:—**breathe, blow,** Ἀθήνη.. ἦκα μάλα ψύξασα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.440.}}
**II** **make cool** or **cold** (not necessarily by blowing), ἀπιὼν ἐπὶ μᾶλλον ψύχει (i.e. ὁ ἥλιος) [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.104, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 16 (variant for{διέψυξε})}}]; opposed to θερμαίνω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 268b; θερμὸν ψύχεται 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 126; ψῦξον τὸν οἶνον 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 56, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.287}}:—_passive_, **grow cool** or **cold,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.181, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 151, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 71b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 931a1; οἶνον.. ψυχόμενον ἐν τῷ φρέατι 5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 57}}; of fire, **to be put out,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 120b: _metaphorically_, ψυγήσεται ἡ ἀγάπη}}**will grow cold,** {{NT|NT.Matt. [prev. cited]}}
**II.2** **cool, refresh,** θάλπουσα καὶ ψύχουσα, of a nurse tending a child, {{Refs|“Trad.Adesp.” 7.2}}: _intransitive_ in _active_, **seek the cool air,** {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 473, LXX.2Ki.19.24.}}
**II.3** **chill, torment,** ἀμφάκει κέντρῳ ψύχειν ψυχὰν ἐμάν (Meineke conjecture ψήχειν) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 693 (Lyric poetry)}}; of death, ψύξει σε δαίμων τῷ πεπρωμένῳ χρόνῳ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 25.10; ἀπαράμονοι καὶ ψύχοντες τὰς πρώτας πράξεις 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 44.28; τοὺς γάμους [prev. author] 116.7}}:—_passive_, ψύχετ᾽ ἀμηχανίῃ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1527.}}
**II.4** _metaphorically_ in _passive_, **to be frigid,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 27.1.}}
**III** **dry, make dry,** δάκρυα δ᾽ οὐ ψύχει γενέτης ἐμός {{LXX|“IG” 3.1335.13; ψ. τι πρὸς τὸν ἥλιον LXX.Jer.8.2}}: **air,** ἱμάτια {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.18.13}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.3}}; οὗ τὰ σῦκα ψύχεται, gloss on{τρασιά}, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.: in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} generally of **drying** in the wind, opposed to τερσήμεναι of **drying** in the sun, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.621. (fragment signf. I comes ψυχή perhaps, but see at {ψυχή}: signification 11 (and with it ψῦχος, ψυχρός, etc.) comes from signf. I: also signification 111 from signification 1.)}}] [ῡ always, except in _aorist 2_ _passive_, see {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 151.]}}

5595:
** ψωμ-ίζω**,
_future_ _Attic dialect_ **-ιῶ**{{LXX|LXX.Num.11.4}}:—**feed by putting little bits into the mouth,** as nurses do to children, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 692, “Lys.” 19, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 4.54}}; or sick people, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Epid.” 7.3; ψ. τινά τι LXX [same place]:}} —_passive_, ἐπίσταμαι γὰρ.., οἷς ψωμίζεται with what **tit-bits he is fed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 715.}}
**II** **give** food **by hand,** σῖτον οὐδ᾽ ἐάν τις ψωμίζῃ δύνανται καταπιεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 592a30}}; **bestow for food,** ψ. πάντα τὰ ὑπάρχοντα {{NT|NT.1Cor.13.3.}}
**II.2** **bait,** ἄγκιστρον {{Refs|“PFay.” 2 iii 14 (“Lyrica Adespota”).}}

5599:
** ὦ**
and **ὤ**, an exclamation, expressing surprise, joy, or pain, **O! oh!** with _nominative_, ὢ τάλας ἐγώ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 981, etc.; ὢ ἔβενος, ὢ χρυσός 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.123}}: also with _genitive_, ὢ τῆς ἀναισχυντίας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pisc.” 5}}; with _interrogative_, ὤ, τί λέγει; {{Refs|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 309d}}; in the middle of a sentence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 362 (Lyric poetry), +others}}
**II** with _vocative_, a mode of address, whether at the beginning of a sentence or in a parenthesis, ὦ Ἀχιλεῦ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.74, etc., especially in dialogue and Oratt., ἐβουλόμην, ὦ ἄνδρες, τὴν δύναμιν κτλ. Antipho. 5.1}}; in invocations of the gods, ὦ Ζεῦ τε καὶ Γῆ καὶ πολισσοῦχοι θεοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 69, etc.; with _imperative_, ὦ χαῖρε [prev. author] “Ag.” 22, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 91}}; ὦ πρὸς θεῶν ὕπεικε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 371, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.98}}: sometimes following the Verb, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 335 (Lyric poetry)}}; in different number from the _vocative_, προσέλθετ᾽, ὦ παῖ, πατρί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1104, compare 1112, Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 66.}}
**II.2** with _nominative_ instead of _vocative_, ὦ δῐος αἰθήρ, ὦ φίλος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 88, 545; ὦ γενναῖος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 227c; ὦ οὗτος Αἴας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 89; ὦ οὗτος οὗτος Οἰδίπους [prev. author] “OC” 1627}}; also οὗτος, ὦ σέ τοι (i.e. καλῶ) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 274.}}
**II.3** with both together, φίλος ὦ Μενέλαε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.189; ὦ τλάμων πάτερ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 641 (Lyric poetry, τλᾶμον codices recentia, editions).}}
**II.4** with the latter of two nouns, Ἀγάμεμνον, ὦ Μενέλαε {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 794}}.—In the first sense usually written ὤ, in the second ὦ: [τὸ ὢ] ἡνίκα θαυμαστικὸν λαμβάνεται βαρύνεται, καὶ χωρεῐ εἰς ἐπιρρηματικὴν σύνταξιν, οἷον ὢ Ἡρακλῆς {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 79.13: 13th-14th c.AD: Thomas Magister Grammaticus p.408R. prescribes ὢ with the _genitive_, but ὦ with the _vocative_, e.g. ὦ Ἡράκλεις, where the whole expression, and not merely the ὤ}} (ὦ,) expresses surprise (but {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 127.24}} seems to imply ὦ in both senses); ὤ as an exclamation is found in forms like{ὤ μοι}, ὤ μοι ἐγώ, ὢ πρὸς τῶν θεῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator [same place]: but ὦ πόποι δυσὶ τόνοις χρῆται Hdn.Gr. 1.503, so that ὢ πόποι}} is improbable,compare {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Theognostus Grammaticus “Can.” 158 (as emended by Lehrs “Aristarch.” 3p.119)}}; ᾤμοι and ὤμοι are both recognized by [{{Refs 7th c.BC+|“Etymologicum Magnum” 822.33, compare “Legal icon Messanense” p.413; ωιμ᾽ 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 23.4; in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus, when it stands in the middle of a sentence, it should be written ὤ, “Hipp.” 362, +others: sometimes doubled, ὢ ὢ κακά 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1214; ἰὼ ὢ ὤ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 224 (variant{ὣ ὣ ὣ})}}]; written ὼ ώ in Papyrus of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 61; tripled, ὢ ὢ ὤ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 985 (Lyric poetry, probably)}}. To those who (like {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 640.11, cf. Scholia 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus p.257H.) took ὦ}} for the _vocative_ of the _Article_ ὁ, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 45.22 - 53}} replies at length.
